function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:r}})}var t={id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp`,title:`The Challenges for Artificial Intelligence in Agriculture`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`A group of corn farmers stands huddled around an agronomist(农学家) and his computer on the side of an irrigation machine in central South Africa. The agronomist has just flown over the field with a hybrid unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) that takes off and lands using propellers yet maintains distance and speed for scanning vast hectares of land through the use of its fixed wings.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`The UAV is fitted with a four spectral band precision sensor that conducts onboard processing immediately after the flight, allowing farmers and field staff to address, almost immediately, any crop abnormalities that the sensor may have recorded, making the data collection truly real-time.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`In this instance, the farmers and agronomist are looking to specialized software to give them an accurate plant population count. It's been 10 days since the corn emerged and the farmer wants to determine if there are any parts of the field that require replanting due to a lack of emergence or wind damage, which can be severe in the early stages of the summer rainy season.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`At this growth stage of the plant's development, the farmer has another 10 days to conduct any replanting before the majority of his fertilizer and chemical applications need to occur. Once these have been applied, it becomes economically unviable to take corrective action, making any further collected data historical and useful only to inform future practices for the season to come.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`The software completes its processing in under 15 minutes producing a plant population count map. It's difficult to grasp just how impressive this is, without understanding that just over a year ago it would have taken three to five days to process the exact same data set, illustrating the advancements that have been achieved in precision agriculture and remote sensing in recent years. With the software having been developed in the United States on the same variety of crops in seemingly similar conditions, the agronomist feels confident that the software will produce a nearly accurate result.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`As the map appears on the screen, the agronomist's face begins to drop. Having walked through the planted rows before the flight to gain a physical understanding of the situation on the ground, he knows the instant he sees the data on his screen that the plant count is not correct, and so do the farmers, even with their limited understanding of how to read remote sensing maps.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Hypothetically, it is possible for machines to learn to solve any problem on earth relating to the physical interaction of all things within a defined or contained environment by using artificial intelligence and machine learning.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Remote sensors enable algorithms(算法) to interpret a field's environment as statistical data that can be understood and useful to farmers for decision-making. Algorithms process the data, adapting and learning based on the data received. The more inputs and statistical information collected, the better the algorithm will be at predicting a range of outcomes. And the aim is that farmers can use this artificial intelligence to achieve their goal of a better harvest through making better decisions in the field.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`In 2011, IBM, through its R&D Headquarters in Haifa, Israel, launched an agricultural cloud-computing project. The project, in collaboration with a number of specialized IT and agricultural partners, had one goal in mind—to take a variety of academic and physical data sources from an agricultural environment and turn these into automatic predictive solutions for farmers that would assist them in making real-time decisions in the field.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`Interviews with some of the IBM project team members at the time revealed that the team believed it was entirely possible to "algorithm" agriculture, meaning that algorithms could solve any problem in the world. Earlier that year, IBM's cognitive learning system, Watson, competed in the game Jeopardy against former winners Brad Rutter and Ken Jennings with astonishing results. Several years later, Watson went on to produce ground-breaking achievements in the field of medicine.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`So why did the project have such success in medicine but not agriculture? Because it is one of the most difficult fields to contain for the purpose of statistical quantification. Even within a single field, conditions are always changing from one section to the next. There's unpredictable weather, changes in soil quality, and the ever-present possibility that pests and diseases may pay a visit. Growers may feel their prospects are good for an upcoming harvest, but until that day arrives, the outcome will always be uncertain.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`By comparison, our bodies are a contained environment. Agriculture takes place in nature, among ecosystems of interacting organisms and activity, and crop production takes place within that ecosystem environment. But these ecosystems are not contained. They are subject to climatic occurrences such as weather systems, which impact upon hemispheres as a whole, and from continent to continent. Therefore, understanding how to manage an agricultural environment means taking literally many hundreds if not thousands of factors into account.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`What may occur with the same seed and fertilizer program in the United States' Midwest region is almost certainly unrelated to what may occur with the same seed and fertilizer program in Australia or South Africa. A few factors that could impact on variation would typically include the measurement of rain per unit of a crop planted, soil type, patterns of soil degradation, daylight hours, temperature and so forth.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`So the problem with deploying machine learning and artificial intelligence in agriculture is not that scientists lack the capacity to develop programs and protocols to begin to address the biggest of growers' concerns; the problem is that in most cases, no two environments will be exactly alike, which makes the testing, validation and successful rollout of such technologies much more laborious than in most other industries.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`Practically, to say that AI and Machine Learning can be developed to solve all problems related to our physical environment is to basically say that we have a complete understanding of all aspects of the interaction of physical or material activity on the planet. After all, it is only through our understanding of "the nature of things" that protocols and processes are designed for the rational capabilities of cognitive systems to take place. And, although AI and Machine Learning are teaching us many things about how to understand our environment, we are still far from being able to predict critical outcomes in fields like agriculture purely through the cognitive ability of machines.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`Backed by the venture capital community, which is now investing billions of dollars in the sector, most agricultural technology startups today are pushed to complete development as quickly as possible and then encouraged to flood the market as quickly as possible with their products.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-Q`,marker:`Q`,text:`This usually results in a failure of a product, which leads to skepticism from the market and delivers a blow to the integrity of Machine Learning technology. In most cases, the problem is not that the technology does not work, the problem is that industry has not taken the time to respect that agriculture is one of the most uncontained environments to manage. For technology to truly make an impact on agriculture, more effort, skills, and funding is needed to test these technologies in farmers' fields.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-R`,marker:`R`,text:`There is huge potential for artificial intelligence and machine learning to revolutionize agriculture by integrating these technologies into critical markets on a global scale. Only then can it make a difference to the grower, where it really counts.`}]},n={id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp`,title:`Slow Hope`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Our world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change is possible. They are 'slow' in their unfolding, and they are slow because they come with setbacks.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`At the beginning of time—so goes the myth—humans suffered, shivering in the cold and dark until the titan(巨人) Prometheus stole fire from the gods. Just as in the myth, technology—first fire and stone tools, and later farming, the steam engine and industry, fossil fuels, chemicals and nuclear power—has allowed us to alter and control the natural world. The myth also reminds us that these advances have come at a price: as a punishment for Prometheus' crime, the gods created Pandora, and they gave her a box filled with evils and curses. When Pandora's box was opened, it unleashed swarms of diseases and disasters upon humankind.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Today we can no longer ignore the ecological curses that we have released in our search for warmth and comfort. In engineering and exploiting and transforming our habitat, we have opened tens of thousands of Pandora's boxes. In recent decades, environmental threats have expanded beyond regional boundaries to have global reach and, most hauntingly, are multiplying at a dizzying rate. On a regular basis, we are reminded that we are running out of time. Year after year, faster and faster, consumption outpaces the biological capacity of our planet. Stories of accelerated catastrophe multiply. We fear the breakdown of the electric grid, the end of non-renewable resources, the expansion of deserts, the loss of islands, and the pollution of our air and water.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Acceleration is the signature of our time. Populations and economic activity grew slowly for much of human history. For thousands of years and well into early modern times, world economies saw no growth at all, but from around the mid-19th century and again, in particular, since the mid-20th, the real GDP has increased at an enormous speed, and so has human consumption. In the Middle Ages, households in Central Europe might have owned fewer than 30 objects on average; in 1900, this number had increased to 400, and in 2020 to 15,000. The acceleration of human production, consumption and travel has changed the animate and inanimate spheres. It has echoed through natural processes on which humans depend. Species extinction, deforestation, damming of rivers, occurrence of floods, the depletion of ozone, the degradation of ocean systems and many other areas are all experiencing acceleration. If represented graphically, the curve for all these changes looks rather like that well-known hockey stick: with little change over millennia(数千年) and a dramatic upswing over the past decades.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Some of today's narratives about the future seem to suggest that we too, like Prometheus, will be saved by a new Hercules, a divine engineer, someone who will mastermind, manoeuvre and manipulate our planet. They suggest that geoengineering, cold fusion or faster-than-light spaceships might transcend once and for all the terrestrial constraints of rising temperatures, lack of energy, scarcity of food, lack of space, mountains of waste, polluted water—you name it.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Yet, if we envisage our salvation to come from a deus ex machina(解围之神), from a divine engineer or a tech solutionist who will miraculously conjure up a new source of energy or another cure-all with revolutionary potency, we might be looking in the wrong place. The fact that we now imagine our planet as a whole does not mean that the 'rescue' of our planet will come with one big global stroke of genius and technology. It will more likely come by many small acts. Global heating and environmental degradation are not technological problems. They are highly political issues that are informed by powerful interests. Moreover, if history is a guide, then we can assume that any major transformations will once again be followed by a huge set of unintended consequences. So what do we do?`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`This much is clear: we need to find ways that help us flatten the hockey-stick curves that reflect our ever-faster pace of ecological destruction and social acceleration. If we acknowledge that human manipulation of the Earth has been a destructive force, we can also imagine that human endeavours can help us build a less destructive world in the centuries to come. We might keep making mistakes. But we will also keep learning from our mistakes.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`To counter the fears of disaster, we need to identify stories, visions and actions that work quietly towards a more hopeful future. Instead of one big narrative, a story of unexpected rescue by a larger-than-life hero, we need multiple stories: we need stories, not only of what Rob Nixon of Princeton University has called the 'slow violence' of environmental degradation (that is, the damage that is often invisible at first and develops slowly and gradually), but also stories of what I call 'slow hope'.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`We need an acknowledgement of our present ecological plight but also a language of positive change, visions of a better future. In The Principle of Hope (1954-1959), Ernst Bloch, one of the leading philosophers of the future, wrote that 'the most tragic form of loss…is the loss of the capacity to imagine that things could be different'. We need to identify visions and paths that will help us imagine a different, more just and more ecological world. Hope, for Bloch, has its starting point in fear, in uncertainty, and in crisis: it is a creative force that goes hand in hand with utopian(乌托邦的) 'wishful images'. It can be found in cultural products of the past—in fairy tales, in fiction, in architecture, in music, in the movies—in products of the human mind that contain 'the outlines of a better world'. What makes us 'authentic' as humans are visions of our 'potential'. In other words: living in hope makes us human.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The power of small, grassroots movements to make changes that spread beyond their place of origin can be seen with the Slow Food movement, which began in Italy in the 1980s. The rise of fast-food restaurants after the Second World War produced a society full of cheap, industrially made foodstuffs. Under the leadership of Carlo Petrini, the Slow Food movement began in Piedmont, a region of Italy with a long history of poverty, violence and resistance to oppression. The movement transformed it into a region hospitable to traditional food cultures—based on native plants and breeds of animals. Today, Slow Food operates in more than 160 countries, poor and rich. It has given rise to thousands of projects around the globe, representing democratic politics, food sovereignty, biodiversity and sustainable agriculture.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`The unscrupulous(无所顾忌的) commodification of food and the destruction of foodstuffs will continue to devastate soils, livelihoods and ecologies. Slow Food cannot undo the irresistible developments of the global food economy, but it can upset its theorists, it can 'speak differently', and it can allow people and their local food traditions and environments to flourish. Even in the United States—the fast-food nation—small farms and urban gardens are on the rise. The US Department of Agriculture provides an Urban Agriculture Toolkit and, according to a recent report, American millennials(千禧一代) are changing their diets. In 2017, 6 per cent of US consumers claimed to be strictly vegetarian, up from 1 per cent in 2014. As more people realise that 'eating is an agricultural act', as the US poet and environmental activist Wendell Berry put it in 1989, slow hope advances.`}]},r={id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp`,title:`Why Lifelong Learning Is the International Passport to Success`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Picture yourself at a college graduation day, with a fresh cohort(一群) of students about to set sail for new horizons. What are they thinking while they throw their caps in the air? What is it with this thin sheet of paper that makes it so precious? It's not only the proof of acquired knowledge but plays into the reputation game of where you were trained. Being a graduate from Harvard Law School carries that extra glamour, doesn't it? Yet take a closer look, and the diploma is the perfect ending to the modern tragedy of education.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Why? Because universities and curricula are designed along the three unities of French classical tragedy: time, action, and place. Students meet at the university campus (unity of place) for classes (unity of action) during their 20s (unity of time). This classical model has traditionally produced prestigious universities, but it is now challenged by the digitalisation of society—which allows everybody who is connected to the internet to access learning—and by the need to acquire skills in step with a fast-changing world. Universities must realise that learning in your 20s won't be enough. If technological diffusion and implementation develop faster, workers will have to constantly refresh their skills.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`The university model needs to evolve. It must equip students with the right skills and knowledge to compete in a world 'where value will be derived largely from human interaction and the ability to invent and interpret things that machines cannot', as the English futurist Richard Watson puts it. By teaching foundational knowledge and up-to-date skills, universities will provide students with the future-proof skills of lifelong learning, not just get them 'job-ready'.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Some universities already play a critical role in lifelong learning as they want to keep the value of their diplomas. This new role comes with a huge set of challenges, and needs largely to be invented. One way to start this transformation process could be to go beyond the 'five-year diploma model' to adapt curricula to lifelong learning. We call this model the lifelong passport.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`The Bachelor's degree could be your passport to lifelong learning. For the first few years, students would 'learn to learn' and get endowed with reasoning skills that remain with them for the rest of their lives. For instance, physics allows you to observe and rationalise the world, but also to integrate observations into models and, sometimes, models into theories or laws that can be used to make predictions. Mathematics is the language used to formulate the laws of physics or economy, and to make rigorous computations that turn into predictions. These two disciplines naturally form the foundational pillars of education in technical universities.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Recent advances in computational methods and data science push us into rethinking science and engineering. Computers increasingly become principal actors in leveraging data to formulate questions, which requires radically new ways of reasoning. Therefore, a new discipline blending computer science, programming, statistics and machine learning should be added to the traditional foundational topics of mathematics and physics. These three pillars would allow you to keep learning complex technical subjects all your life because numeracy(计算) is the foundation upon which everything else is eventually built.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`According to this new model, the Master of Science (MSc) would become the first stamp in the lifelong learning journey. The MSc curriculum should prepare students for their professional career by allowing them to focus on acquiring practical skills through projects.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Those projects are then interwoven with fast-paced technical modules(模块) learned 'on-the-fly' and 'at will' depending on the nature of the project. If, for instance, your project is developing an integrated circuit, you will have to take a module on advanced concepts in microelectronics. The most critical skills will be developed before the project even starts, in the form of boot camps(短期强化训练), while the rest can be fostered along with the project, putting them to immediate use and thus providing a rich learning context.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`In addition to technical capabilities, the very nature of projects develops social and entrepreneurial skills, such as design thinking, initiative taking, team leading, activity reporting or resource planning. Not only will those skills be actually integrated into the curriculum but they will be very important to have in the future because they are difficult to automate.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`After the MSc diploma is earned, there would be many more stamps of lifelong learning over the years. If universities decide to engage in this learning model, they will have to cope with many organisational challenges that might shake their unity of place and action. First, the number of students would be unpredictable. If all of a university's alumni(往届毕业生) were to become students again, the student body would be much bigger than it is now, and it could become unsustainable for the campus in terms of both size and resources. Second, freshly graduated students would mix with professionally experienced ones. This would change the classroom dynamics, perhaps for the best. Project-based learning with a mixed team reflects the reality of the professional world and could therefore be a better preparation for it.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Sound like science fiction? In many countries, part-time studying is not exceptional: on average across OECD countries, part-time students in 2016 represented 20 per cent of enrolment in tertiary education. In many countries, this share is higher and can exceed 40 per cent in Australia, New Zealand and Sweden.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`If lifelong learning were to become a priority and the new norm, diplomas, just like passports, could be revalidated periodically. A time-determined revalidation would ease administration for everybody. Universities as well as employers and employees would know when they have to retrain. For instance, graduates from the year 2000 would have to come back in 2005.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`This could fix the main organisational challenges for the university, but not for the learners, due to lack of time, family obligations or funds. Here, online learning might be an option because it allows you to save your 'travel time', but it has its limits. So far, none of the major employers associated with online learning platforms such as Coursera and Udacity has committed to hire or even interview graduates of their new online programmes.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`Even if time were not an issue, who will pay for lifelong learning? That's the eternal debate: should it be the learner's responsibility, that of his employer, or of the state? For example, in Massachusetts, the healthcare professions require continuing education credits, which are carefully evidenced and documented. Yet the same state's lawyers don't require continuing legal education, although most lawyers do participate in it informally. One explanation is that technology is less of a factor in law than it is in healthcare.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`Europe has many scenarios, but the French and Swiss ones are interesting to compare. In France, every individual has a right to lifelong learning organised via a personal learning account that is credited as you work. In Switzerland, lifelong learning is a personal responsibility and not a government one. However, employers and the state encourage continuing education either by funding parts of it or by allowing employees to attend it.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`Universities have a fundamental role to play in this journey, and higher education is in for a change. Just like classical theatre, the old university model produced talent and value for society. We are not advocating its abolition but rather calling for the adaptation of its characteristics to meet the needs of today.`}]},i={id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp`,title:`How Marconi Gave Us the Wireless World`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`A hundred years before iconic figures like Bill Gates and Steve Jobs permeated our lives, an Irish-Italian inventor laid the foundation of the communication explosion of the 21st century. Guglielmo Marconi was arguably the first truly global figure in modern communication. Not only was he the first to communicate globally, he was the first to think globally about communication. Marconi may not have been the greatest inventor of his time, but more than anyone else, he brought about a fundamental shift in the way we communicate.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Today's globally networked media and communication system has its origins in the 19th century, when, for the first time, messages were sent electronically across great distances. The telegraph, the telephone, and radio were the obvious predecessors of the Internet, iPods, and mobile phones. What made the link from then to now was the development of wireless communication. Marconi was the first to develop and perfect this system, using the recently-discovered "air waves" that make up the electromagnetic spectrum.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Between 1896, when he applied for his first patent in England at the age of 22, and his death in Italy in 1937, Marconi was at the center of every major innovation in electronic communication. He was also a skilled and sophisticated organizer, an entrepreneurial innovator, who mastered the use of corporate strategy, media relations, government lobbying, international diplomacy, patents, and prosecution. Marconi was really interested in only one thing: the extension of mobile, personal, long-distance communication to the ends of the earth (and beyond, if we can believe some reports). Some like to refer to him as a genius, but if there was any genius to Marconi it was this vision.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`In 1901 he succeeded in signaling across the Atlantic, from the west coast of England to Newfoundland in the USA, despite the claims of science that it could not be done. In 1924 he convinced the British government to encircle the world with a chain of wireless stations using the latest technology that he had devised, shortwave radio. There are some who say Marconi lost his edge when commercial broadcasting came along; he didn't see that radio could or should be used to frivolous(无聊的) ends. In one of his last public speeches, a radio broadcast to the United States in March 1937, he deplored that broadcasting had become a one-way means of communication and foresaw it moving in another direction, toward communication as a means of exchange. That was visionary genius.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly, and with an elegance that would appear to be intuitive and uncomplicated to the user-friendly, if you will. There is a direct connection from Marconi to today's social media, search engines, and program streaming that can best be summed up by an admittedly provocative exclamation: the 20th century did not exist. In a sense, Marconi's vision jumped from his time to our own.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Marconi invented the idea of global communication—or, more straightforwardly, globally networked, mobile, wireless communication. Initially, this was wireless Morse code telegraphy(电报通讯), the principal communication technology of his day. Marconi was the first to develop a practical method for wireless telegraphy using radio waves. He borrowed technical details from many sources, but what set him apart was a self-confident vision of the power of communication technology on the one hand, and, on the other, of the steps that needed to be taken to consolidate his own position as a player in that field. Tracing Marconi's lifeline leads us into the story of modern communication itself. There were other important figures, but Marconi towered over them all in reach, power, and influence, as well as in the grip he had on the popular imagination of his time. Marconi was quite simply the central figure in the emergence of a modern understanding of communication.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`In his lifetime, Marconi foresaw the development of television and the fax machine, GPS, radar, and the portable hand-held telephone. Two months before he died, newspapers were reporting that he was working on a "death ray," and that he had "killed a rat with an intricate device at a distance of three feet." By then, anything Marconi said or did was newsworthy. Stock prices rose or sank according to his pronouncements. If Marconi said he thought it might rain, there was likely to be a run on umbrellas.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Marconi's biography is also a story about choices and the motivations behind them. At one level, Marconi could be fiercely autonomous and independent of the constraints of his own social class. On another scale, he was a perpetual outsider. Wherever he went, he was never "of" the group; he was always the "other," considered foreign in Britain, British in Italy, and "not American" in the United States. At the same time, he also suffered tremendously from a need for acceptance that drove, and sometimes stained, every one of his relationships.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Marconi placed a permanent stamp on the way we live. He was the first person to imagine a practical application for the wireless spectrum, and to develop it successfully into a global communication system—in both terms of the word; that is, worldwide and all-inclusive. He was able to do this because of a combination of factors—most important, timing and opportunity—but the single-mindedness and determination with which he carried out his self-imposed mission was fundamentally character-based; millions of Marconi's contemporaries had the same class, gender, race, and colonial privilege as he, but only a handful did anything with it. Marconi needed to achieve the goal that was set in his mind as an adolescent; by the time he reached adulthood, he understood, intuitively, that in order to have an impact he had to both develop an independent economic base and align himself with political power. Disciplined, uncritical loyalty to political power became his compass for the choices he had to make.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`At the same time, Marconi was uncompromisingly independent intellectually. Shortly after Marconi's death, the nuclear physicist Enrico Fermi—soon to be the developer of the Manhattan Project—wrote that Marconi proved that theory and experimentation were complementary features of progress. "Experience can rarely, unless guided by a theoretical concept, arrive at results of any great significance…on the other hand, an excessive trust in theoretical conviction would have prevented Marconi from persisting in experiments which were destined to bring about a revolution in the technique of radio-communications." In other words, Marconi had the advantage of not being burdened by preconceived assumptions.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`The most controversial aspect of Marconi's life—and the reason why there has been no satisfying biography of Marconi until now—was his uncritical embrace of Benito Mussolini. At first this was not problematic for him. But as the regressive(倒退的) nature of Mussolini's regime became clear, he began to suffer a crisis of conscience. However, after a lifetime of moving within the circles of power, he was unable to break with authority, and served Mussolini faithfully (as president of Italy's national research council and royal academy, as well as a member of the Fascist Grand Council) until the day he died—conveniently—in 1937, shortly before he would have had to take a stand in the conflict that consumed a world that he had, in part, created.`}]},a={id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp`,title:`France's beloved cathedral only minutes away from complete destruction`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Notre Dame Cathedral in the heart of Paris was within "15 to 30 minutes" of complete destruction as firefighters battled to stop flames reaching its bell towers on Monday evening, French authorities have revealed. A greater disaster was averted by members of the Paris fire brigade, who risked their lives to remain inside the burning monument to create a wall of water between the raging fire and the two towers on the west of the building.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`The revelation of how close France came to losing its most famous cathedral emerged as police investigators questioned workers involved in the restoration of the monument to try to establish the cause of the devastating blaze. Paris prosecutor Remy Heitz said that an initial fire alert was sounded at 6:20 pm on Monday evening but no fire was found. The second alert was sounded at 6:43 pm, and the blaze was discovered on the roof.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`More than €650 million was raised in a few hours on Tuesday as French business leaders and global corporations announced they would donate to a restoration campaign launched by the president, Emmanuel Macron. But as the emergency services picked through the burnt debris, a row was resurfacing over accusations that the beloved cathedral, immortalised in Victor Hugo's novel, was already crumbling before the fire.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`The cathedral is owned by the French state and has been at the centre of a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration work of the collapsing staircases, crumbling statues and cracked walls. Jean-Michel Leniaud, the president of the scientific council at the National Heritage Institute, said: "What happened was bound to happen. The lack of adequate maintenance and daily attention to such a majestic building is the cause of this catastrophe." After the blaze was declared completely extinguished, 15 hours after it started, the junior interior minister, Laurent Nunez, said the structure had been saved but remained vulnerable. He praised the actions of the firefighters but admitted the fate of the cathedral had been uncertain. "They saved the main structure, but it all came down to 15-30 minutes," Nunez said.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`In a surprise televised address on Tuesday evening, Macron said he wanted to see the cathedral rebuilt within five years. "The fire at Notre Dame reminds us that we will always have challenges to overcome," Macron said, "Notre Dame is our history, our literature, the centre of our life. It is the standard by which we measure our distances. It's so many books, so many paintings. It's the cathedral of every French person, even those who have never visited it. This history is ours and so we will rebuild Notre Dame. It is what the French people expect; it is what our history deserves. It is our deep destiny. We will rebuild Notre Dame so it is even more beautiful than before. I want it done in the next five years. We can do it. After the time of testing comes a time of reflection and then of action."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`The fire, which had started at the base of the 93-metre spire(尖塔) at about 6:40 pm on Monday, spread through the cathedral's roof, made up of hundreds of oak beams, some dating back to the 13th century. These beams, known as la forêt (the forest) because of their density, formed the cross-shaped roof that ran the length of the central part of the cathedral. As hundreds of tourists and Parisians stood and watched the flames leaping from the roof, there was shock and tears as the cathedral spire caught fire, burned and then collapsed into itself.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`A collection of dramatic videos and photos quickly spread across social media, showing the horrifying destruction, and attracting emotional responses from people all over the world. Indeed, within minutes the fire occupied headlines of every major global newspaper and television network. This is not surprising given Notre Dame Cathedral, meaning "Our Lady", is one of the most recognised symbols of the city of Paris attracting millions of tourists every year.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`While the world looked on, the 500 firefighters at the scene then battled to prevent the flames from reaching the two main towers, where the cathedral bells hang. If the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire, it could have sent the bells—the largest of which, the Emmanuel Bell, weighs 13 tons—crashing down, potentially causing the collapse of both towers. Police and fire services will spend the next 48 hours assessing the "security and safety" of the 850-year-old structure. Nunez said: "We have identified vulnerabilities throughout the structure, all of which still need securing." As a result, residents of five buildings around the northern side of the cathedral were being temporarily evacuated, he added. Architects have identified three main holes in the structure, in the locations of the spire, the main hall and the upper rooms to the north of the central aisle. Most of the wooden roof beams have been burned, and parts of the concrete holding up the roof have collapsed.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`The interior minister, Christophe Castaner, visited the cathedral on Tuesday afternoon to see the extent of the devastation. Ash covered the marble diamond-patterned floor and floated in large pools of grey water from the fire hoses. Behind a heap of blackened oak beams that lay piled up where they had fallen, daylight from vast holes in the cathedral roof lit a golden cross over a statue by Nicolas Coustou, which appeared to have escaped damage. Preliminary inspections also suggested the three ornate(装饰华丽的) stained glass "rose" windows appeared to have survived the fire, officials said. However, fire officers have said a complete inventory of the damage will not be possible until the cathedral structure has been deemed safe.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The culture minister, Franck Riester, said religious relics saved from the cathedral were being securely held at the Hotel de Ville, and works of art that sustained smoke damage were being taken to the Louvre, the world's largest art museum, where they would be dried out, repaired and stored. Sixteen copper statues that decorated the spire had been removed for restoration only a few days before the fire. Relics at the top of the spire are believed lost as the spire was destroyed. As well as damage from the heat, which firefighters said reached more than 800 ℃, experts also need to assess damage from the vast quantities of water firefighters poured into the cathedral. One casualty of this was The Great Organ constructed in the 1730s, which was said to have escaped the flames but been significantly damaged by water.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`French political commentators noted the devastating fire had succeeded where Macron had failed in uniting the country. But criticism over the original state of the building is likely to intensify over coming days. Leniaud told La Croix newspaper: "This is not about looking for people to blame. The responsibility is collective because this is the most loved monument in the country." Alexandre Gady, an art historian, agreed. "We've been saying for years that the budget for maintaining historic monuments is too low," Gady said. The Paris prosecutor's office has opened an inquiry into "involuntary destruction by fire", indicating they believe the cause of the blaze was accidental rather than criminal.`}]},o={id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp`,title:`What Are the Ethics of CGI Actors—And Will They Replace Real Ones?`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Digital humans are coming to a screen near you. As computer-generated imagery (CGI) has become cheaper and more sophisticated, the film industry can now convincingly recreate people on screen—even actors who have been dead for decades. The technology's ability to effectively keep celebrities alive beyond the grave is raising questions about public legacies and image rights.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Late in 2019, it was announced that US actor James Dean, who died in 1955, will star in a Vietnam War film scheduled for release later this year. In the film, which will be called Finding Jack, Dean will be recreated on screen with CGI based on old footage(影片镜头) and photographs, with another actor voicing him. The news was met with excitement by those keen to see Dean digitally brought back to life for only his fourth film, but it also drew sharp criticism. "This is puppeteering the dead for their fame alone," actress Zelda Williams wrote on Twitter. "It sets such an awful precedent for the future of performance." Her father, Robin Williams, who died in 2014, was keen to avoid the same fate. Before his death, he filed a deed protecting the use of his image until 2039, preventing others from recreating him using CGI to appear in a film, TV show or as a hologram(全息影像).`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`The James Dean film is a way to keep the actor's image relevant for younger generations, says Mark Roesler of CMG Worldwide, the firm that represents Dean's estate. "I think this is the beginning of an entire wave," says Travis Cloyd, CEO of Worldwide XR, one of the companies behind the digital recreation of Dean. "Moving into the future, we want James Dean to be brought into different gaming environments, or different virtual reality environments, or augmented reality environments," he says.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Other actors have been revived, with the permission of their estates, for advertising purposes: for example, a 2011 advertisement for Dior featured contemporary actress Charlize Theron alongside iconic 20th-century stars Marilyn Monroe, Grace Kelly and Marlene Dietrich. Later, Audrey Hepburn was digitally recreated for a chocolate commercial in 2013. In the same year, a CGI Bruce Lee appeared in a Chinese-language ad for a whisky brand, which offended many fans because Lee was widely known not to drink alcohol at all. "In the last five years, it's become more affordable and more achievable in a whole movie," says Tim Webber at UK visual effects firm Framestore, the company behind the Hepburn chocolate ad. Framestore used body doubles with resemblance to Hepburn's facial structure and body shape as a framework for manual animation. The process was extremely difficult and expensive, says Webber, but the technology has moved on.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Now, a person can be animated from scratch. "If they're alive today, you can put them in scanning rigs, you can get every detail of their body analysed very carefully and that makes it much easier, whereas working from available photographs is tricky," says Webber, who won an Academy Award for his visual effects work on the 2013 film Gravity. "I also see a lot of actors today who will have the desire to take advantage of this technology: to have their likeness captured and stored for future content," says Cloyd. "They foresee this being something that could give their estates and give their families the ability to make money from their likeness when they're gone."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`A hidden hazard of digitally recreating a deceased(已故的) celebrity is the risk of damaging their legacy. "We have to respect the security and the integrity of rights holders," says John Canning at Digital Domain, a US firm that created a hologram rapper(说唱艺人) Tupac Shakur, which appeared at the Coachella music festival in 2012, 15 years after his death.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Legally, a person's rights to control the commercial use of their name and image beyond their death differ between and even within countries. In certain US states, for example, these rights are treated similarly to property rights, and are transferable to a person's heirs. In California, under the Celebrities Rights Act, the personality rights for a celebrity last for 70 years after their death. "We've got a societal debate going on about access to our public commons, as it were, about famous faces," says Lilian Edwards at Newcastle University, UK. Should the public be allowed to use or reproduce images of famous people, given how iconic they are? And what is in the best interest of a deceased person's legacy may conflict with the desires of their family or the public, says Edwards.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`A recreation, however lifelike, will never be indistinguishable from a real actor, says Webber. "When we are bringing someone back, representing someone who is no longer alive on the screen, what we are doing is extremely sophisticated digital make-up," he says: "A performance is a lot more than a physical resemblance."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`As it becomes easier to digitally recreate celebrities and to entirely manufacture on-screen identities, could this kind of technology put actors out of jobs? "I think actors are worried about this," says Edwards. "But I think it will take a very long time." This is partly because of the risk that viewers find virtual humans scary. Edwards cites widespread backlash to the digital recreation of Carrie Fisher as a young Princess Leia in Rogue One, a trick later repeated in the recent Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker, which was filmed after Fisher's death in 2016. "People didn't like it," she says. "They discovered the uncanny valley(诡异谷)."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`This refers to the idea that when objects trying to resemble humans aren't quite perfect, they can make viewers feel uneasy because they fall somewhere between obviously non-human and fully human. "That's always a danger when you're doing anything human or human-like," says Webber. "There're a thousand things that could go wrong with a computer-generated facial performance, and any one of those could make it fall into the uncanny valley," he says: "Your brain just knows there's something wrong." The problem often arises around the eyes or mouth, says Webber. "They're the areas that you look at when you're talking to someone."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`An unfamiliar digital human that has been created through CGI will also face the same challenge as an unknown actor: they don't have the appeal of an established name. "You have to spend substantial capital in creating awareness around their likeness and making sure people are familiar with who they are," says Cloyd. This is now starting to happen. "The way you pre-sell a movie in a foreign market is based on relevant talent," he says. "I think we're a long way away from having virtual beings that have the ability to pre-sell content."`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Webber expects that we will see more digital humans on screen. "It's happening because it can happen," he says. Referring to a line from Jurassic Park(侏罗纪公园), he adds: "People are too busy thinking about what they can do to think about whether they should do it."`}]},s={"cet6-2020-12-s1":{subtitle:`2020 年 12 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2020年12月英语六级真题第1套，站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to develop creativity.You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`In an age driven by innovation, the ability to think in original ways has never been more valuable. Therefore, I firmly believe that students should be actively encouraged to develop their creativity, for it benefits both their growth and society at large.

First, creativity makes learning far more engaging. When students are free to imagine and design their own solutions, rather than merely memorizing answers, they become genuinely curious and study with real enthusiasm. Moreover, creativity sharpens the ability to solve problems. A learner who is used to exploring different approaches can adapt flexibly to new challenges, which is exactly what future workplaces demand. For instance, many employers today value an engineer who can propose an original design over one who only follows fixed rules.

In conclusion, encouraging creativity is far from a luxury; it is a necessity. By nurturing imagination and original thinking, schools equip students to enrich their own lives and to meet the uncertain demands of tomorrow.`,guidance:[`立场段落地。开头 'In an age driven by innovation... has never been more valuable' 用点题背景句铺垫；随后 'I firmly believe that students should be actively encouraged to develop their creativity' 明确表态，落实了'立场段先亮态度'的先景后态。`,`三连论证落地。'First, creativity makes learning far more engaging' 是观点句，后接一句解释；'Moreover, creativity sharpens the ability to solve problems' 引出第二个理由；'For instance, many employers today value an engineer who can propose an original design' 用事例把理由说实，正是'观点+解释+例证三连'。`,`收束升华落地。'encouraging creativity is far from a luxury; it is a necessity' 换个说法重申中心立场；'schools equip students to... meet the uncertain demands of tomorrow' 升华到未来意义，落实'重申升华'。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to develop creativity.You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：立场段落地。开头 'In an age driven by innovation... has never been more valuable' 用点题背景句铺垫；随后 'I firmly believe that students should be actively encouraged to develop their creativity' 明确表态，落实了'立场段先亮态度'的先景后态。三连论证落地。'First, creativity makes learning far more engaging' 是观点句，后接一句解释；'Moreover, creativity sharpens the ability to solve problems' 引出第二个理由。收束升华落地。'encouraging creativity is far from a luxury; it is a necessity' 换个说法重申中心立场；'schools equip students to... meet the uncertain demands of tomorrow' 升华到未来意义，落实'重申升华'。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 M: Good morning, Safe House Insurance. My name is Paul. How can I help you today? 2 W: Morning. I wouldn't say that it's good from where I am standing. This is Miss Wilson and this is the third time I've called this week since receiving your letter about our insurance claim. I'm getting a little fed up with my calls about my claim being completely disregarded. 3 M: Miss Wilson, thank you for calling back. Can I take some details to help me look at your claim? 4 W: It's Miss May Wilson of 15 South Sea Road in Cornwall, and the details are that our village was extensively flooded 2 months ago. The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water and five of us have been living in a caravan ever since. You people are still withholding the money we are entitled to over a bizarre technical detail. And it's not acceptable, Paul! 5 M: Miss Wilson, according to the notes on your account, the bizarre technical detail that you mentioned refers to the fact that you hadn't paid house insurance the month before the incident. 6 W: That money left our account, and now that you should be paying out, you're suddenly saying that you didn't receive it on time. I'm really skeptical about this claim. 7 M: The contract does say that any missed payment in a year will affect the terms and conditions of the insurance contract and may affect claims. Of course, I can pass you on to my manager to talk to you more about this. 8 W: I've already spoken to him, and you can tell him I'm furious now, and that your company has a lawsuit on its hands. You will be hearing from my lawyer. Goodbye. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 W: How do you feel about the future of artificial intelligence? Personally, I feel quite optimistic about it. 2 M: AI? I'm not so optimistic actually. In fact, it's something we should be concerned about. 3 W: Well, it will help us humans understand ourselves better. And, when we have a better understanding of ourselves, we can improve the world. 4 M: Well, one thing is for sure: Technology is evolving faster than our ability to understand it. And in the future, AI will make jobs kind of pointless. 5 W: I think artificial intelligence will actually help create new kinds of jobs, which would require less of our time and allow us to be centered on creative tasks. 6 M: I doubt that very much. Probably the last job that will remain will be writing AI software. And then eventually, AI will just write its own software. 7 W: At that time, we're going to have a lot of jobs which nobody will want to do, so we'll need artificial intelligence for the robots to take care of the old guys, like us. 8 M: I don't know. There's a risk that human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life. AI will be able to completely simulate a person in every way possible. In fact, some people think we are in the simulation, right now. 9 W: That's impossible. Humans can't even make a mosquito. Computers only have chips. People have brains, and that's where the wisdom comes from. 10 M: Once it's fully developed, AI will become tired of trying to communicate with humans, as we would be much slower thinkers in comparison. 11 W: I'm not so sure. A computer is a computer, and a computer is just a toy. 12 M: Computers can easily communicate incredibly fast, so the computer will just get impatient talking to humans. It will be barely getting any information out. 13 W: Well, I believe there is a benevolent future with AI. I also think you watch too many science fiction films. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 To achieve financial security, how much you save is always more important than the amount you earn or how shrewdly you invest. If you are under 30 years old, your goal should be to save 20% of your monthly income after tax deductions. This is irrespective of how much you earn. Approximately 50% should be reserved for essentials like food and accommodation. The remaining 30% is for recreation and entertainment. 2 But for many young people, it'll be difficult to designate such a large proportion of their income for savings. If you find it hard to save any money at all, start by cutting all unnecessary spending. Allocate a tiny amount of 1 or 2 percent for savings, and gradually increase that amount. Always keep that 20% goal in mind to prevent yourself from becoming complacent. It can be challenging to stick to such a strict plan, but if you adopt the right mindset, you should be able to make it work for you. 3 So, what should you be doing with the money that you're saving? Some must be kept easily accessible in case you need some cash in an emergency. The largest proportion should be invested in retirement plans, either through your employer or privately. And you can keep some money for high-risk but potentially lucrative investments. Dividends can be reinvested or used to purchase something you like. By following this plan, you should hopefully be able to enjoy your life now, and still be financially secure in the future. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 I work in advertising and I like to keep up with current trends, mainly because I'm aware that we live in an image-obsessed world. However, when I first started my job, occasionally I'd catch a glimpse of myself in the lifts, and find myself thinking that I looked a total mess. Was I being held back by my choice of clothing? The short answer is yes, especially when clients are quick to judge you on your style rather than your work. 2 But no one can be unique with their outfit every day. I mean, that's why uniforms were invented. So, here's what I did. I created my own uniform. To do this, I chose an appropriate outfit. Then I bought multiple items of the same style in different shades. Now, I never worry about what I'm wearing in the morning even if I do get a bit tired of just wearing the same classic pieces. 3 Overall, when it comes to work, you have to ask yourself: will looking smarter enhance my ability to do my job? For some, this question may not be an issue at all. Especially if you work remotely and rarely see your colleagues or clients face to face. But if your job involves interacting with other people, the answer to this is often yes. So rather than fighting the system, I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work. If that means playing it safe with your image, then let's face it. It's probably worth it. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Did you know that Americans have approximately three times the amount of space we had 50 years ago? Therefore, you'd think we'd have sufficient room for all of our possessions. On the contrary, the personal storage business is now a growing industry. We've got triple the space, but we've become such enthusiastic consumers that we require even more. This phenomenon has resulted in significant credit card debt, enormous environmental footprints, and perhaps not coincidentally, our happiness levels have failed to increase over the same half century. 2 I'm here to suggest an alternative: having less might actually be a preferable decision. Many of us have experienced, at some stage, the pleasure of possessing less. I propose that less stuff and less space can not only help you economize, but also simplify your life. 3 I recently started an innovative project to discover some creative solutions that offered me everything I required. By purchasing an apartment that was 40 square meters instead of 60, I immediately saved $200,000. Smaller space leads to reduced utility bills, and also a smaller carbon footprint. Because it is designed around an edited collection of possessions, limited to my favorite stuff, I'm really excited to live there. 4 How can we live more basically? Firstly, we must ruthlessly cut the unnecessary objects out of our lives. To stem consumption, we should think before we buy, and ask ourselves, "Will it truly make me happier?" Obviously, we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years. 5 Secondly, we require space efficiency. We want appliances that are designed for use most of the time, not for occasional use. Why own a six-burner stove when you rarely use even three burners? 6 Finally, we need multifunctional spaces and housewares. I combined a movable wall with transforming furniture to get more out of my limited space. Consider my coffee table. It increases in size to accommodate ten. My office is tucked away, easily hidden. My bed simply pops out of the wall. For guests, I can relocate the movable wall and utilize the foldable guest beds I installed. 7 I'm not saying we should all live in tiny apartments, but consider the benefits of an edited life. When you return home and walk through your front door, take a moment to ask yourselves, "Could I do with a little life editing? Would that give me more freedom and more time?" Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 Now, believe it or not, people sometimes lie in order to maintain a good, honest reputation—even if it hurts them to do so. At least, this is what a team of scientists is suggesting, with evidence to prove it. 2 Picture this scenario: You often drive for work and can be compensated for up to 400 miles per month. Most people at your company drive about 300 miles each month. But this month, you drove 400 miles. How many miles do you think you'd claim in your expense report? 3 The scientists asked this exact question as part of the study we're discussing today. With surprising results, they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure, giving an average answer of 384 miles. In other words, they lied about the number of miles, even though they would forfeit money they were owed. 4 The researchers believe this was to seem honest, with the assumption being that others would be suspicious of a high expense claim. But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment? The researchers explain that many people care a great deal about their reputation and how they'll be judged by others. If they care enough, their concern about appearing honest and not losing the respect of others may be greater than their desire to actually be honest. 5 The researchers assert that the findings suggest that when people obtain very favorable outcomes, they anticipate other people's suspicious reactions and prefer lying and appearing honest to telling the truth and appearing as selfish liars. 6 So why is this research important? Well, experts generally agree there are two main types of lie-selfish lies and lies that are meant to benefit others. The first, as you may predict, is for selfish gain, such as submitting a fraudulent claim to an insurance company, while the second involves lying to help others or not offend others, for example, telling a friend whose outfit you don't like that they look great. 7 But the researchers are suggesting a third type of lying—lying to maintain a good reputation. Now this hypothesis is new and some skeptics argue that this isn't a whole new category of lie. 8 But the findings seem intuitive to me. After all, one of the main motivations for lying is to increase our worth in the eyes of others. So it seems highly likely that people will lie to seem honest. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1 Why do old people dislike new music? As I've grown older, I often hear people my age say things like "They just don't make good music like they used to". Why does this happen? Luckily, psychology can give us some insights into this puzzle. Musical tastes begin to crystallize as early as age 13 or 14. By the time we're in our early 20s, these tastes get locked into place pretty firmly. 2 In fact, studies have found that by the time we turn 33, most of us have stopped listening to new music. Meanwhile, popular songs released when you're in your early teens are likely to remain quite popular among your age group through the rest of your life. 3 There could be a biological explanation for this, as there's evidence that the brain's ability to make subtle distinctions between different chords, rhythms and melodies deteriorates with age. So to older people, newer, less familiar songs might all "sound the same". 4 But there may be some simpler reasons for older people's aversion to newer music. One of the most researched laws of social psychology is something called the "mere exposure effect", which, in essence, means that the more we're exposed to something, the more we tend to like it. This happens with people we know, the advertisements we see and the songs we listen to. 5 When you're in your early teens, you probably spend a fair amount of time listening to music or watching music videos. Your favorite songs and artists become familiar, comforting parts of your routine. 6 For many people over 30, job and family obligations increase, so there's less time to spend discovering new music. Instead, many will simply listen to old, familiar favorites from that period of their lives when they had more free time. 7 Of course, those teen years weren't necessarily carefree. They're famously confusing, which is why so many TV shows and movies revolve around high school turmoil. 8 Psychology research has shown that the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense than those that come later. And we also know that intense emotions are associated with stronger memories and preferences. Both of these might explain why the songs we listen to during this period become so memorable and beloved. 9 So there's nothing wrong with your parents because they don't like your music. Rather it's all part of the natural order of things. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What is the woman complaining about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Her house has not been repaired in time.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She has failed to reach the manager again.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Her claim has been completely disregarded.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She has not received any letter from the man.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“I'm getting a little fed up with my calls about my claim being completely disregarded.”。诉苦情绪句即落点。这句以 I'm getting a little fed up(我快受够了)这种带强烈不满情绪的表态领出，正是女方主动开口诉苦的那一句——抱怨题的答案就落在说话人情绪外露、点名诉苦对象的句子上。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What is the problem the woman's family encountered?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The ground floor of their cottage was flooded.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their caravan was washed away by the flood.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their entire house was destroyed by the flood.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The roof of their cottage collapsed in the flood.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water and five of us have been living in a caravan ever since.”。被问者细述灾情即落点。女方应保险员"take some details"之请，亲口细述受灾经过，The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water 就是她对"灾情"的直接陈述；对话题答案落在被问方正面回应处。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What has caused the so-called "bizarre technical detail" according to the man?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The woman's misreading of the insurance company's letter.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The woman's ignorance of the insurance company's policy.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The woman's inaccurate description of the whole incident.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The woman's failure to pay her house insurance in time.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P5：“Miss Wilson, according to the notes on your account, the bizarre technical detail that you mentioned refers to the fact that you hadn't paid house ins…”。refers to后即解释。男方用 refers to the fact that(指的是…这一事实)这一引子点明缘由，引子后面那句正是对"bizarre technical detail"的官方解释；据男方说法答原因，就锁定这句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she would do at the end of the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) File a lawsuit against the insurance company.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Talk to the manager of Safe House Insurance.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Consult her lawyer about the insurance policy.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Revise the terms and conditions of the contract. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P8：“I've already spoken to him, and you can tell him I'm furious now, and that your company has a lawsuit on its hands.”。结尾表态句即落点。对话最后一段女方撂下狠话 your company has a lawsuit on its hands，这是她临挂电话前的行动宣告；题干问 at the end 的打算，落点就在这句结尾表态。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speakers from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They work in different fields of AI technology.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They disagree about the future of AI technology.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They differ greatly in their knowledge of modern technology.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are both worried about the negative impact of technology.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“W: Personally, I feel quite optimistic about it. M: AI? I'm not so optimistic actually. In fact, it's something we should be concerned about.”。两人态度正面对撞。女方说 I feel quite optimistic about it，男方紧接着以转折否定回应 I'm not so optimistic actually，一个 optimistic、一个 not so optimistic，态度词正面对撞。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What will new kinds of jobs be like, according to the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Simply writing AI software.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stimulating and motivating.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Less time-consuming and focusing on creation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) More demanding and requiring special training.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“W: I think artificial intelligence will actually help create new kinds of jobs, which would require less of our time and allow us to be centered on cr…”。被问者的直接描述句。题干问"女方说新工作怎样"，答案就落在女方描述新工作的那句 require less of our time and allow us to be centered on creative tasks——这是被问者对该对象的直接定性，正是细节题要锁定的应答句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What is the risk the man anticipates?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) There could be jobs nobody wants to do.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Digital life could replace human civilization.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Humans would be tired of communicating with one another.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Old people would be taken care of solely by unfeeling robots.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“M: There's a risk that human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life.”。risk预警词直接领出风险。男方原句以 There's a risk that 直接点出风险，预警信号词 risk 一响，紧跟其后的 human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life 就…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What is the man's concern about AI technology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Life will become like a science fiction film.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will take away humans' jobs altogether.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Chips will be inserted in human brains.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will be smarter than human beings. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P10：“M: Once it's fully developed, AI will become tired of trying to communicate with humans, as we would be much slower thinkers in comparison.”。对比中暗示AI更强。男方 we would be much slower thinkers in comparison 用人机对比，明说人比 AI 慢得多，再加 Computers can easily communicate incredibly fast。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What are people under 30 advised to do to achieve financial security?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Restrain themselves from high-risk investments.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Save one-fifth of their net monthly income.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Invest shrewdly in lucrative businesses.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Try to earn as much money as possible.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“If you are under 30 years old, your goal should be to save 20% of your monthly income after tax deductions.”。目标表态句 your goal should be。讲者用 your goal should be 这一表态结构领出建议，正是听力里锁定答案句的强信号；再加上题干点名的 under 30 在 If you are under 30 years old 同句出现，定位直接命中。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What should people do if they find it difficult to follow the speaker's advice on their financial plan?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Start by doing something small.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Ask a close friend for advice.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Try to stick to their initial plan.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Cut 20% of their daily spending.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“If you find it hard to save any money at all, start by cutting all unnecessary spending.”。条件句 If you find it hard 后的对策。题干的 difficult 在录音里以 If you find it hard to save any money at all 出现。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker think is important for achieving financial security?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) An optimistic attitude.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) An ambitious plan.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A proper mindset.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A keen interest. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“It can be challenging to stick to such a strict plan, but if you adopt the right mindset, you should be able to make it work for you.”。转折 but 之后的 the right mindset。讲者先说计划难坚持，再用 but if you adopt the right mindset 转折，转折后才是真意所在——the right mindset 正是被强调为关键的要素，转折 but 后即答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speaker when she first started her job?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She was uninterested in advertising.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She found her outfit inappropriate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She was unhappy with fashion trends.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She often checked herself in a mirror.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“However, when I first started my job, occasionally I'd catch a glimpse of myself in the lifts, and find myself thinking that I looked a total mess.”。转折 However 后才是真意。前半句 I like to keep up with current trends 是铺垫，真正的落点在转折词 However 之后——她坦白刚入职时觉得 I looked a total mess，这正是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`Why were uniforms invented according to the speaker?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To save the trouble of choosing a unique outfit every day.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To meet the expectations of fashion-conscious clients.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To keep up with the current trends.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To save the expenses on clothing.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But no one can be unique with their outfit every day. I mean, that's why uniforms were invented.”。that's why 直陈因果。因果信号 that's why uniforms were invented 直接把"制服为何被发明"和前一句挂钩——原因就是前句 no one can be unique with their outfit every day。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about looking smarter?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It enhances people's ability to work independently.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It helps people succeed in whatever they are doing.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It matters a lot in jobs involving interaction with others.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It boosts one's confidence when looking for employment.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“But if your job involves interacting with other people, the answer to this is often yes.”。转折 But + 条件句给限定。前一句说有人可能 work remotely and rarely see your colleagues or clients 时不成问题。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker advise people to do in an image-obsessed world?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Design their own uniform to appear unique.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Wear classic pieces to impress their clients.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Fight the ever-changing trends in fashion.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Do whatever is possible to look smart. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“So rather than fighting the system, I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work.”。I think we should 直陈主张。表态信号 I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work 是作者的核心建议。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What has prevented Americans' happiness levels from increasing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Their obsession with consumption.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their failure to accumulate wealth.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The ever-increasing costs of housing.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The deterioration of the environment.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“We've got triple the space, but we've become such enthusiastic consumers that we require even more.”。因果信号 has resulted in 串起原因与结果。答案句靠因果链定位：This phenomenon has resulted in 把前句的『现象』和后面包括『幸福感没上升』在内的一串后果连起来。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What things should we possess according to the speaker?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Things that are rare to find.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Things that cost less money.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Things that boost efficiency.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Things that we cherish most.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Obviously, we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years.”。转折 but 之后才是真意。答案句是个转折结构：we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years，but 之前是垫场。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the items in the speaker's home?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They serve multiple purposes.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They benefit the environment.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are mostly durable.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are easily disposable. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“Finally, we need multifunctional spaces and housewares. I combined a movable wall with transforming furniture to get more out of my limited space.”。举例铺陈共同指向『多功能』。答案句先给总述 we need multifunctional spaces and housewares，再用咖啡桌能变大、床能翻出墙这些例子佐证——一连串例子都在证明同一个特征『一物多用』，总述句就是落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What did a team of scientists find in their study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) All respondents were afraid of making a high expense claim.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A number of respondents gave an average answer of 400 miles.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Over 10% of the respondents lied about the distance they drove.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Most of the respondents got compensated for driving 384 miles.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“With surprising results, they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure,”。研究发现的标志句 they found that。they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure 用 found that 直接领…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`Why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment according to the researchers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They responded to colleagues' suspicion.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They cared about other people's claims.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They wanted to protect their reputation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They endeavored to actually be honest.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment? The researchers explain that many people care a great deal about their reputation and h…”。自问自答 + The researchers explain 领出原因。录音先用 But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment 自设问题。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker think of the researchers' findings?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They seem positive.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They seem intuitive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are illustrative.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are conclusive. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“But the findings seem intuitive to me. After all, one of the main motivations for lying is to increase our worth in the eyes of others.”。转折 But + 第一人称 to me 标出本人态度。前段刚说 some skeptics argue 有人质疑，本段以 But the findings seem intuitive to me 转折，并用 to me 明确这是说话人自己的判断。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker mainly discuss in this talk?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Deterioration in the quality of new music.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Insights into the features of good music.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Older people's changing musical tastes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Older people's aversion to new music.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Why do old people dislike new music? As I've grown older, I often hear people my age say things like "They just don't make good music like they used t…”。开头设问即点题。篇章第一句就是一个问句 Why do old people dislike new music?，开门见山抛出全篇要回答的问题，这种开头设问句就是主旨信号，后面整段都在为它找答案。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What have studies found about most people by the time they turn 33?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They no longer listen to new music.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They find all music sounds the same.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They can make subtle distinctions about music.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They seldom listen to songs released in their teens.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“In fact, studies have found that by the time we turn 33, most of us have stopped listening to new music.”。数字 33 顺听定位。原文 by the time we turn 33 与题干的 turn 33 完全对上，数字是听力里最醒目的定位锚；同时 studies have found 与题干 studies found 呼应，双重信号确认这就是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from one of the most researched laws of social psychology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The more you are exposed to something, the more familiar it'll be to you.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The more you are exposed to something, the deeper you'll understand it.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The more you experience something, the longer you'll remember it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The more you experience something, the better you'll appreciate it.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“One of the most researched laws of social psychology is something called the "mere exposure effect", which, in essence,”。定义句 means that 引出本质。原文 the most researched laws of social psychology 与题干完全对上，紧跟的 in essence, means that（本质上是说）是一个明确的下定义信号。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What might explain the fact that songs people listen to in their teen years are memorable and beloved?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Teenagers' memories are more lasting.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Teenagers' emotions are more intense.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Teenagers are much more sensitive.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Teenagers are much more sentimental.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“Psychology research has shown that the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense than those that come later.”。intense emotions 起始的因果链。原文先说 the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense（少年情绪更强烈）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`discouraging`,B:`dividend`,C:`emotional`,D:`fragments`,E:`impaired`,F:`imprisoned`,G:`incentives`,H:`inherently`,I:`initially`,J:`instrumental`,K:`merging`,L:`predict`,M:`probably`,N:`pump`,O:`swelling`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-1`,text:`The idea of taxing things that are bad for society has a powerful allure. It offers the possibility of a double benefit— [26] harmful activities, while also providing the government with revenue.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-2`,text:`Take sin taxes. Taxes on alcohol make it more expensive to get drunk, which reduces excessive drinking and [27] driving. At the same time, they provide state and local governments with billions of dollars of revenue. Tobacco taxes, which generate more than twice as much, have proven [28] in the decline of smoking, which has saved millions of lives.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-3`,text:`Taxes can also be an important tool for environmental protection, and many economists say taxing carbon would be the best way to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Economic theory says that unlike income or sales taxes, carbon taxes can actually increase economic efficiency; because companies that [29] carbon dioxide into the sky don't pay the costs of the climate change they cause, carbon taxes would restore the proper [30] to the market.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-4`,text:`In reality, carbon taxes alone won't be enough to halt global warming, but they would be a useful part of any climate plan. What's more, the revenue from this tax, which would [31] be hundreds of billions of dollars per year, could be handed out to citizens as a [32] or used to fund green infrastructure projects.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-5`,text:`Similarly, a wealth tax has been put forward as a way to reduce inequality while raising revenue. The revenue from this tax, which some experts [33] will be over $4 trillion per decade, would be designated for housing, child care, health care and other government benefits. If you believe, as many do, that wealth inequality is [34] bad, then these taxes improve society while also [35] government coffers(金库).`}]},t,{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-1`,text:`What is the place of art in a culture of inattention? Recent visitors to the Louvre report that tourists can now spend only a minute in front of the Mona Lisa before being asked to move on. Much of that time, for some of them, is spent taking photographs not even of the painting but of themselves with the painting in the background.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-2`,text:`One view is that we have democratised tourism and gallery-going so much that we have made it effectively impossible to appreciate what we've travelled to see. In this oversubscribed society, experience becomes a commodity like any other. There are queues to climb Mt. Jolmo Lungma as well as to see famous paintings. Leisure, thus conceived, is hard labour, and returning to work becomes a well-earned break from the ordeal.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-3`,text:`What gets lost in this industrialised haste is the quality of looking. Consider an extreme example, the late philosopher Richard Wollheim. When he visited the Louvre he could spend as much as four hours sitting before a painting. The first hour, he claimed, was necessary for misperceptions to be eliminated. It was only then that the picture would begin to disclose itself. This seems unthinkable today, but it is still possible to organise. Even in the busiest museums there are many rooms and many pictures worth hours of contemplation which the crowds largely ignore. Sometimes the largest crowds are partly the products of bad management; the Mona Lisa is such a hurried experience today partly because the museum is being reorganised. The Uffizi in Florence, another site of cultural pilgrimage, has cut its entry queues down to seven minutes by clever management. And there are some forms of art, those designed to be spectacles as well as objects of contemplation, which can work perfectly well in the face of huge crowds.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-4`,text:`Olafur Eliasson's current Tate Modern show, for instance, might seem nothing more than an entertainment, overrun as it is with kids romping(喧闹地玩耍) in fog rooms and spray mist installations. But it's more than that: where Eliasson is at his most entertaining, he is at his most serious too, and his disorienting installations bring home the reality of the destructive effects we are having on the planet—not least what we are doing to the glaciers of Eliasson's beloved Iceland.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-5`,text:`Marcel Proust, another lover of the Louvre, wrote: "It is only through art that we can escape from ourselves and know how another person sees the universe, whose landscapes would otherwise have remained as unknown as any on the moon." If any art remains worth seeing, it must lead us to such escapes. But a minute in front of a painting in a hurried crowd won't do that.`}]},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-1`,text:`Every five years, the government tries to tell Americans what to put in their bellies. Eat more vegetables. Dial back the fats. It's all based on the best available science for leading a healthy life. But the best available science also has a lot to say about what those food choices do to the environment, and some researchers are annoyed that new dietary recommendations of the USDA (United States Department of Agriculture) released yesterday seem to utterly ignore that fact.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-2`,text:`Broadly, the 2016–2020 dietary recommendations aim for balance: More vegetables, leaner meats and far less sugar.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-3`,text:`But Americans consume more calories per capita than almost any other country in the world. So the things Americans eat have a huge impact on climate change. Soil tilling releases carbon dioxide, and delivery vehicles emit exhaust. The government's dietary guidelines could have done a lot to lower that climate cost. Not just because of their position of authority: The guidelines drive billions of dollars of food production through federal programs like school lunches and nutrition assistance for the needy.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-4`,text:`On its own, plant and animal agriculture contributes 9 percent of all the country's greenhouse gas emissions. That's not counting the fuel burned in transportation, processing, refrigeration, and other waypoints between farm and belly. Red meats are among the biggest and most notorious emitters, but trucking a salad from California to Minnesota in January also carries a significant burden. And greenhouse gas emissions aren't the whole story. Food production is the largest user of fresh water, largest contributor to the loss of biodiversity, and a major contributor to using up natural resources.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-5`,text:`All of these points and more showed up in the Dietary Guidelines Advisory Committee's scientific report, released last February. Miriam Nelson chaired the subcommittee in charge of sustainability for the report, and is disappointed that eating less meat and buying local food aren't in the final product. "Especially if you consider that eating less meat, especially red and processed, has health benefits," she says.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-6`,text:`So what happened? The official response is that sustainability falls too far outside the guidelines' official scope, which is to provide "nutritional and dietary information."`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-7`,text:`Possibly the agencies in charge of drafting the decisions are too close to the industries they are supposed to regulate. On one hand, the USDA is compiling dietary advice. On the other, their clients are US agriculture companies.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-8`,text:`The line about keeping the guidelines' scope to nutrition and diet doesn't ring quite right with researchers. David Wallinga, for example, says, "In previous guidelines, they've always been concerned with things like food security—which is presumably the mission of the USDA. You absolutely need to be worried about climate impacts and future sustainability if you want secure food in the future."`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`It offers the possibility of a double benefit—__26__ harmful activities, while also providing the government with revenue`,answer:`A`,explanation:`破折号后并列分词结构，需要动名词/现在分词。“抑制有害活动”，与 providing revenue 构成“双重红利”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-1`,quote:`It offers the possibility of a double benefit— [26] harmful activities, while also providing the government with revenue.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`reduces excessive drinking and __27__ driving`,answer:`E`,explanation:`并列名词 driving 前作前置定语。impaired driving 是固定搭配“能力受损的驾驶”，即酒驾。上文谈酒精税抑制醉酒，下文紧接 reduces excessive drinking and ___ driving，与 excessive drinking 并列，应当是酒精带来的另一恶果。英语里 impaired driving 正是“酒驾/能力受损的驾驶”的标准说法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-2`,quote:`Taxes on alcohol make it more expensive to get drunk, which reduces excessive drinking and [27] driving.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`Tobacco taxes, which generate more than twice as much, have proven __28__ in the decline of smoking`,answer:`J`,explanation:`系动词 proven 之后作表语。be instrumental in doing/sth. 是固定搭配“在…中起重要作用”。“烟草税在吸烟下降中起到重要作用”，instrumental + in 是教材级搭配，且与后半句 saved millions of lives 的因果链相符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-2`,quote:`Tobacco taxes, which generate more than twice as much, have proven [28] in the decline of smoking, which has saved millions of lives.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`companies that __29__ carbon dioxide into the sky don't pay the costs of the climate change they cause`,answer:`N`,explanation:`定语从句主语是复数 companies，需复数现在时谓语。pump … into … 是“泵送/大量排放到…”的固定搭配。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-3`,quote:`Economic theory says that unlike income or sales taxes, carbon taxes can actually increase economic efficiency; because companies that [29] carbon dioxide into the sky don't pay the costs of the climate change they cause, carbon taxes would restore the proper [30] to the market.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`carbon taxes would restore the proper __30__ to the market`,answer:`G`,explanation:`形容词 proper 后作宾语中心词。经济学语境下“恢复正确的市场激励”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-3`,quote:`Economic theory says that unlike income or sales taxes, carbon taxes can actually increase economic efficiency; because companies that [29] carbon dioxide into the sky don't pay the costs of the climate change they cause, carbon taxes would restore the proper [30] to the market.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`the revenue from this tax, which would __31__ be hundreds of billions of dollars per year`,answer:`M`,explanation:`助动词 would 与系动词 be 之间，需要副词。对“未来年收入数额”做概率估计。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-4`,quote:`What's more, the revenue from this tax, which would [31] be hundreds of billions of dollars per year, could be handed out to citizens as a [32] or used to fund green infrastructure projects.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`the revenue…could be handed out to citizens as a __32__ or used to fund green infrastructure projects`,answer:`B`,explanation:`as a + ___ 后接单数可数名词。“作为红利发给公民”——carbon dividend 的标准说法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-4`,quote:`What's more, the revenue from this tax, which would [31] be hundreds of billions of dollars per year, could be handed out to citizens as a [32] or used to fund green infrastructure projects.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`The revenue from this tax, which some experts __33__ will be over $4 trillion per decade`,answer:`L`,explanation:`定语从句中缺谓语，主语是 some experts。“专家预测未来收入”，对接 will be 从句。与第 4 段 would probably be hundreds of billions 的“估算未来收入”句式构成同类表达。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-5`,quote:`The revenue from this tax, which some experts [33] will be over $4 trillion per decade, would be designated for housing, child care, health care and other government benefits.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`If you believe, as many do, that wealth inequality is __34__ bad`,answer:`H`,explanation:`形容词 bad 前的空，需要副词修饰。“本质上是坏的”，呼应作者立场。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-5`,quote:`If you believe, as many do, that wealth inequality is [34] bad, then these taxes improve society while also [35] government coffers(金库).`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`these taxes improve society while also __35__ government coffers`,answer:`O`,explanation:`while also + ___ + 宾语，需要 -ing 形式动词。“充实政府金库”，与全文“增加财政收入”主旨同向。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-bp-5`,quote:`If you believe, as many do, that wealth inequality is [34] bad, then these taxes improve society while also [35] government coffers(金库).`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Farmers will not profit from replanting once they have applied most of the fertilizer and other chemicals to their fields.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`匹配 D 段：施肥施药后补种 + 不再获利。定位句“At this growth stage of the plant's development, the farmer has another 10 days to conduct any replanting before the majority of h…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-D`,quote:`At this growth stage of the plant's development, the farmer has another 10 days to conduct any replanting before the majority of his fertilizer and ch…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Agriculture differs from the medical science of the human body in that its environment is not a contained one.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`匹配 L 段：农业与人体医学的“受限/不受限”之别。定位句“By comparison, our bodies are a contained environment. Agriculture takes place in nature,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-L`,quote:`By comparison, our bodies are a contained environment.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`The agronomist is sure that he will obtain a nearly accurate count of plant population with his software.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`匹配 E 段：农学家相信软件会给出近乎准确的植株计数。定位句“With the software having been developed in the United States on the same variety of crops in seemingly similar conditions,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-E`,quote:`The software completes its processing in under 15 minutes producing a plant population count map.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`The application of artificial intelligence to agriculture is much more challenging than to most other industries.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`匹配 N 段：AI 用于农业比多数其他行业更难。定位句“So the problem with deploying machine learning and artificial intelligence in agriculture is not that scientists lack the capacity…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-N`,quote:`So the problem with deploying machine learning and artificial intelligence in agriculture is not that scientists lack the capacity to develop programs…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Even the farmers know the data provided by the UAV is not correct.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：连农户都知道无人机数据不对。定位句“Having walked through the planted rows before the flight to gain a physical understanding of the situation on the ground,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-F`,quote:`As the map appears on the screen, the agronomist's face begins to drop.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`The pressure for quick results leads to product failure, which, in turn, arouses doubts about the applicability of AI technology to agriculture.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`Q`,explanation:`匹配 Q 段：求快导致产品失败 → 引发对 AI 适用性的怀疑。定位句“This usually results in a failure of a product, which leads to skepticism from the market and delivers a blow to the integrity of…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-Q`,quote:`This usually results in a failure of a product, which leads to skepticism from the market and delivers a blow to the integrity of Machine Learning tec…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Remote sensors are aimed to help farmers improve decision-making to increase yields.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`匹配 H 段：遥感器 → 帮助农户改进决策 → 提高产量。定位句“Remote sensors enable algorithms to interpret a field's environment as statistical data that can be understood and useful to farme…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-H`,quote:`Remote sensors enable algorithms(算法) to interpret a field's environment as statistical data that can be understood and useful to farmers for decision-…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`The farmer expects the software to tell him whether he will have to replant any parts of his farm fields.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：农户期望软件告诉自己是否要补种。定位句“In this instance, the farmers and agronomist are looking to specialized software to give them an accurate plant population count.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-C`,quote:`In this instance, the farmers and agronomist are looking to specialized software to give them an accurate plant population count.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Agriculture proves very difficult to quantify because of the constantly changing conditions involved.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`匹配 K 段：条件不断变化 → 农业极难量化。定位句“Because it is one of the most difficult fields to contain for the purpose of statistical quantification.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-K`,quote:`Because it is one of the most difficult fields to contain for the purpose of statistical quantification.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`The same seed and fertilizer program may yield completely different outcomes in different places.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`匹配 M 段：同样的种子和肥料方案在不同地方结果可能完全不同。定位句“What may occur with the same seed and fertilizer program in the United States' Midwest region is almost certainly unrelated to wha…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-mp-M`,quote:`What may occur with the same seed and fertilizer program in the United States' Midwest region is almost certainly unrelated to what may occur with the…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What does the scene at the Louvre demonstrate according to the author?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The enormous appeal of a great piece of artistic work to tourists.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The near impossibility of appreciating art in an age of mass tourism.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The ever-growing commercial value of long-cherished artistic works.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The real difficulty in getting a glimpse at a masterpiece amid a crowd.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Recent visitors to the Louvre report that tourists can now spend only a minute in front of the Mona Lisa before being asked to move on.”。预判得出的'大众旅游下艺术几乎无法被欣赏'与 The near impossibility of appreciating art in an age of mass tourism. 几乎一一对应，锁定 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-1`,quote:`Recent visitors to the Louvre report that tourists can now spend only a minute in front of the Mona Lisa before being asked to move on.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`Why did the late philosopher Richard Wollheim spend four hours before a picture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It takes time to appreciate a piece of art fully.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is quite common to misinterpret artistic works.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The longer people contemplate a picture, the more likely they will enjoy it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The more time one spends before a painting, the more valuable one finds it.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“When he visited the Louvre he could spend as much as four hours sitting before a painting.”。预判得出的'充分欣赏一幅画需要时间'与 It takes time to appreciate a piece of art fully. 完全对应，锁定 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-3`,quote:`When he visited the Louvre he could spend as much as four hours sitting before a painting.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does the case of the Uffizi in Florence show?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Art works in museums should be better taken care of.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sites of cultural pilgrimage are always flooded with visitors.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Good management is key to handling large crowds of visitors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Large crowds of visitors cause management problems for museums.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“The Uffizi in Florence, another site of cultural pilgrimage, has cut its entry queues down to seven minutes by clever management.”。预判得出的'好管理是处理大客流的关键'与 Good management is key to handling large crowds of visitors. 一一对应，锁定 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-3`,quote:`The Uffizi in Florence, another site of cultural pilgrimage, has cut its entry queues down to seven minutes by clever management.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from Olafur Eliasson's current Tate Modern show?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Children learn to appreciate art works most effectively while they are playing.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is possible to combine entertainment with appreciation of serious art.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Art works about the environment appeal most to young children.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Some forms of art can accommodate huge crowds of visitors.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But it's more than that: where Eliasson is at his most entertaining, he is at his most serious too,”。预判得出的'娱乐性展览能承载严肃艺术意图'与 It is possible to combine entertainment with appreciation of serious art. 完全对应，锁定 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-4`,quote:`But it's more than that: where Eliasson is at his most entertaining, he is at his most serious too,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What can art do according to Marcel Proust?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Enable us to live a much fuller life.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Allow us to escape the harsh reality.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Help us to see the world from a different perspective.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Urge us to explore the unknown domain of the universe.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“It is only through art that we can escape from ourselves and know how another person sees the universe,”。预判得出的'艺术让我们从他人视角看世界'与 Help us to see the world from a different perspective. 完全对应，锁定 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p1-5`,quote:`It is only through art that we can escape from ourselves and know how another person sees the universe,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`Why are some researchers irritated at the USDA's 2016-2020 Dietary Guidelines?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It ignores the harmful effect of red meat and processed food on health.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Too much emphasis is given to eating less meat and buying local food.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The dietary recommendations are not based on medical science.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It takes no notice of the potential impact on the environment.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“But the best available science also has a lot to say about what those food choices do to the environment,”。预判「忽视环境影响」与 It takes no notice of the potential impact on the environment. 在主体与极性上完全一致，D 即定位句的标准同义改写。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-1`,quote:`But the best available science also has a lot to say about what those food choices do to the environment,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the author say the USDA could have contributed a lot to lowering the climate cost through its dietary guidelines?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It has the capacity and the financial resources to do so.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Its researchers have already submitted relevant proposals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Its agencies in charge of drafting the guidelines have the expertise.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can raise students' environmental awareness through its programs.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“The government's dietary guidelines could have done a lot to lower that climate cost.”。预判的「权威+经费」两要素分别由 the capacity 与 the financial resources 接住，It has the capacity and the financial resources to do so. 是定位句两个并列原因的完整概括。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-3`,quote:`The government's dietary guidelines could have done a lot to lower that climate cost. Not just because of their position of authority: The guidelines drive bill…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the Dietary Guidelines Advisory Committee's scientific report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Food is easily contaminated from farm to belly.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Greenhouse effect is an issue still under debate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Modern agriculture has increased food diversity.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Farming consumes most of our natural resources.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Food production is the largest user of fresh water, largest contributor to the loss of biodiversity,”。选项 D 是定位句「农业是最大的淡水用户/生物多样性损失贡献者/自然资源耗用者」事实清单的标准上位概括，未越界、未偏题。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-4`,quote:`Food production is the largest user of fresh water, largest contributor to the loss of biodiversity, and a major contributor to using up natural resources.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What may account for the neglect of sustainability in the USDA's Dietary Guidelines according to the author?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Its exclusive concern with Americans' food safety.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Its sole responsibility for providing dietary advice.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Its close ties with the agriculture companies.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Its alleged failure to regulate the industries.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P7：“Possibly the agencies in charge of drafting the decisions are too close to the industries they are supposed to regulate.”。选项 Its close ties with the agriculture companies. 是定位句揣测原因的标准名词化复述，覆盖了「USDA 与农业公司关系太近」这一核心，与作者意图完全吻合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-7`,quote:`Possibly the agencies in charge of drafting the decisions are too close to the industries they are supposed to regulate.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What should the USDA do to achieve food security according to David Wallinga?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Give top priority to things like nutrition and food security.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Endeavor to ensure the sustainable development of agriculture.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Fulfill its mission by closely cooperating with the industries.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Study the long-term impact of climate change on food production.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“In previous guidelines, they've always been concerned with things like food security—which is presumably the mission of the USDA.”。选项 Endeavor to ensure the sustainable development of agriculture. 把 Wallinga 引语后半句的条件因果（要想 X→必做 Y）翻译为目的+行动（为 X 而做 Y），与预判完全一致。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s1-p2-8`,quote:`In previous guidelines, they've always been concerned with things like food security—which is presumably the mission of the USDA.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Qinghai-Tibet Railway is the highest and longest plateau railway in the world, with a total length of 1,956 kilometers, of which 960 kilometers lie at an altitude of over 4,000 meters. It is also the first railway connecting Tibet with the other regions of China. Since the railway runs through the most fragile ecosystem in the world, ecological protection measures were taken both during and after its construction to ensure that it would become a "green railway". The Qinghai-Tibet Railway has greatly shortened the travel time between mainland China and Tibet. More importantly, it has greatly promoted the economic development of Tibet and improved the lives of local residents. After the railway was opened, more and more people choose to travel to Tibet by train, which also gives them a chance to enjoy the beautiful scenery along the way.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s1-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`青藏铁路是世界上最高最长的高原铁路，全长1 956公里，其中有960公里在海拔4 000多米之上，是连接西藏和中国其他地区的第一条铁路。由于铁路穿越世界上最脆弱的生态系统， 在建设期间和建成后都采取了生态保护措施，以确保其成为一条"绿色铁路"。青藏铁路大大缩短了中国内地与西藏之间的旅行时间。更重要的是，它极大地促进了西藏的经济发展，改善了当地居民的生活。铁路开通后，愈来愈多的人选择乘火车前往西藏，这样还有机会欣赏沿线的美景。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①“青藏铁路”用约定俗成的官方译名 the Qinghai-Tibet Railway（青=Qinghai，藏=Tibet）；②“西藏”统一译为 Tibet，全篇多次出现（连接西藏、促进西藏经济、前往西藏）必须始终用同一拼写，不要时而 Xizang 时而 Tibet；③“中国内地”译为 mainland China 或 the inland regions of China；本篇取 mainland China，；④“绿色铁路”译为加引号的 a "green railway"，保留原文引号表"特定称谓"，green 直译即可达意（指环保），不必另加解释。`,score:15}]}]},"cet6-2020-12-s2":{subtitle:`2020 年 12 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2020年12月英语六级真题第2套，站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to effective communication skills. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Effective communication is far more than a soft skill; it is a core competence that shapes a student's academic success and professional future. For this reason, schools and teachers should actively encourage students to develop this valuable ability from an early stage.

There are several compelling reasons for this. First, strong communication skills facilitate learning. When students can clearly express doubts and exchange ideas in group projects, knowledge is absorbed far more efficiently. Moreover, these skills sharpen employability. In job interviews, an applicant who articulates thoughts logically leaves a stronger impression than one who merely lists qualifications. In addition, good communication enriches personal relationships, helping students voice their emotions and resolve conflicts with empathy. Such emotional connection is essential for a healthy campus life.

In conclusion, effective communication benefits students in study, career and daily life alike. Schools should therefore create more opportunities—debates, presentations and teamwork—so that every student can grow into a confident and articulate communicator.`,guidance:[`拔高加表态。本段落实'先拔高再表态'：首句 'Effective communication is far more than a soft skill; it is a core competence...' 把主题拔高定性，次句 'For this reason, schools and teachers should actively encourage students to develop this valuable ability' 顺势亮立场，直接回应 why students should be encouraged to effective communication skills，完成首段点题。`,`三理由各带例。本段用 'There are several compelling reasons for this' 总起，再以 First / Moreover / In addition 领起三条理由，每条都配了具体场景：group projects、job interviews、resolve conflicts with empathy，把'理由句+例子'的方法落到实处，避免空喊。`,`收束再呼吁。本段用 'In conclusion, effective communication benefits students in study, career and daily life alike' 把三条理由压缩重申，与首段呼应；再以 'Schools should therefore create more opportunities—debates, presentations and teamwork' 给出可操作呼吁，实现'收束加呼吁'。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to effective communication skills. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：拔高加表态。本段落实'先拔高再表态'：首句 'Effective communication is far more than a soft skill; it is a core competence...' 把主题拔高定性，次句 'For this reason。三理由各带例。本段用 'There are several compelling reasons for this' 总起，再以 First / Moreover / In addition 领起三条理由，每条都配了具体场景：group projects、job interviews、resolve conflicts with empathy，把'理由句+例子'的方法落到实处，避免空喊。收束再呼吁。本段用 'In conclusion, effective communication benefits students in study, career and daily life alike' 把三条理由压缩重申，与首段呼应。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 W: This is unbelievable. Unlike any video game I've ever played before, it's so boring, yet so relaxing at the same time. How did you hear about this driving simulator? 2 M: My brother introduced it to me last year. I was surprised to find how educational and realistic it was. It's called Euro Truck Simulator, but they have other versions as well for America and so on. I was really drawn to the scenery. The routes go through parts of the country you don't normally see as a tourist. 3 W: Yeah, I can see that. It seems so simple, just transporting cargo from point A to point B, driving from one city to another. But I really appreciate all the details that go into the game. It's even given me a new appreciation for the logistics industry and traffic on the road. 4 M: I completely agree. My brother also introduced me to some videos of someone that streams their game online. It was fascinating to watch, really. This guy drove very carefully, obeyed all the road signs and traffic rules—such a contrast to most violent games. 5 W: Honestly, playing has inspired me to look into the industry more. I've read articles about how self-driving trucks may soon be available and could greatly impact cargo logistics. Considering all that goes into driving these larger vehicles, it's amazing that we could soon have that kind of technology. 6 M: Ha. I've got one step further. I registered to take a safe driving course to improve my real-life driving skills. In a way, I feel like I have a head start compared to other students in the class. Playing this video game has given me some maneuvering practices already. 7 W: I'm not sure how accurate the video game is compared to real-life situations. But if it results in more drivers looking both ways before entering an intersection, I'd say that's a positive outcome. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 W: How do you like being self-employed? 2 M: There are obvious benefits, though I don't seem to have the freedom I anticipated as I just don't seem able to decline work offers. And working alone, there have been times when I've found that money alone provides insufficient motivation. Have you experienced the same since you began working for yourself? 3 W: Sometimes, yes. Unlike the rest of the workforce who have managers to prompt motivation whenever they're feeling lazy or bored, we self-employed workers perform our jobs without a manager to lift our spirits. There's no one around to offer praise or initiate collaboration, no one to make greater use of our interests and talents. 4 M: That's a fact. Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care, of course. And certainly not all managers have a clue how to motivate people. Still, having a manager nearby at least indicates there's an opportunity they'll be decent enough to look out for you when your energy and focus begin to deplete. 5 W: The motivator I value most is autonomy. I've learned not to sacrifice my prized autonomy by working all hours of the day and by saying yes to every client request. 6 M: Yes. I need to remind myself that I selected this lifestyle for the independence. I don't miss aspects of permanent full-time employment I disliked, such as the office politics, job insecurity, inflexible hours and so on. I wouldn't mind a bit more in the form of praise though, praise which is on the record. 7 W: That'll come with time. Relatedness is inevitably cultivated via human interaction. Engaging with clients, getting written testimony and recommendations, staying in contact with clients afterwards, these are things you'll find will come in due course and provide you with motivation. 8 M: You're right. That's an area I do need to put some more effort into—building closer relationships with those who engage my services and skills. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 Weather is a constant force in our lives, but there is little marketing research on how it affects businesses. Now, a new study reveals how sunny and snowy conditions influence consumer behavior. 2 Those weather conditions trigger consumers to mentally visualize using products associated with the respective weather. This leads to consumers placing a higher value on those products. That is, they're willing to pay more money for them. But the correlation is only found with products related to being outside. 3 How does this work? Researchers give the example of a beach towel. On a sunny day, consumers who see that product are not just looking at the towel itself, they are likely imagining themselves lying on the towel in the sun. This mental picture of using the towel increases the value of the product in the consumers' mind. 4 Researchers put forward the following hypothesis to explain their findings. They think the mental picture works in sunshine and snow because these weather conditions have a positive association with outside activities. The effect is not seen with rainy weather. Researchers assert this is because there aren't many activities that are enabled by rain. Most products associated with rain, like umbrellas, are only used for protection from the weather, and not for any activities. 5 Researchers believe that companies that sell a wide array of products online can benefit most from the insights this study provides. Online sellers often use complex mathematical formulas to determine what products to feature and how to price these products. Incorporating more data about weather would allow them to make better decisions. This could bolster sales. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 Setting the office air-conditioning at about 22℃ has become standard practice across the world. Numerous guidebooks across the world on heating, ventilation and air-conditioning claim office performance peaks at 22 degrees. Many people indeed find relief from soaring summer temperatures in air-conditioned offices. But recent studies have challenged the accepted wisdom that a cool office is more productive. 2 The reality is more complex. Researchers conducted a review of all studies relating to air-conditioning and productivity. They found that 22 degrees was probably a little chilly, even at the height of summer. For a person dressed in typical summer clothing, an optimal range would be between 23 and 26 degrees. And people can even tolerate temperatures beyond this comfort zone as long as they can adjust their clothing and expectations. In fact, even on very hot days, it makes sense to turn the air-conditioning up. People often chase just one optimum temperature, and this is understandable when people feel hot. But there is a range of at least three to four degrees which does not have any adverse impact. 3 Another issue related to this is that people can become psychologically dependent on air conditioning. If they're used to the environment which is air-conditioned, they tend to prefer lower temperatures. But the studies found that almost all humans became accustomed to the new temperature. It was only at the extreme ends of the temperature range where people's productivity suffered. This range was above 26 degrees and below 19 degrees. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Psychology research has tended to portray solitude as a negative experience. Studies conducted in the 1970s and 1990s suggested that people felt less happy when alone as compared to being with others. However, a new paper shows an alternative view of solitude, one in which solitude can be positive. 2 Let's start by looking at the earlier research. It had a couple of shortcomings. First, it measured emotion on a scale from positive to negative, overlooking the possibility that our positive and negative emotions can fluctuate independently. Also, it categorized emotions as simply positive or negative. It didn't consider that emotions arouse us to different degrees, and that both positive and negative emotions can arouse us a lot or a little. That is, whether positive or negative, emotions can be either high-arousal or low-arousal. High-arousal emotions include excitement on the positive side or anger on the negative side, while low-arousal ones include feeling calm on the positive side or lonely on the negative. 3 This new research attempted to overcome these shortcomings. Researchers began with a simple study. They asked participants to spend 15 minutes sitting alone without engaging in any activity, and measured how this solitude influences their emotional state. This experiment specifically aimed to determine the effect of solitude on high-arousal emotions. It looked at positive emotions such as being excited or interested, and negative emotions including being scared or distressed. The results were clear. After 15 minutes of solitude, the participants showed reductions in both types of emotion. 4 A second study measured the effects of solitude on low-arousal emotions. These included both positive and negative emotions, such as feeling calm, relaxed, sad or lonely. That experiment found that all of these emotions were increased by time alone. Thus it seems past depictions of solitude were wrong. It doesn't have a simple emotional effect that can be characterized as good or bad. Rather, it changes the intensity of our inner experience. It amplifies quieter emotions, but it diminishes the intensity of stronger feelings. 5 It's worth clarifying that these findings relate to relatively brief periods of solitude. This is distinct from prolonged loneliness. Research has demonstrated that the latter is correlated with an assortment of negative physical and psychological effects. How can people benefit from being alone? The findings here suggest that people can use solitude to regulate their emotions. Solitude can help us become quiet after excitement, calm after an angry episode, or simply feel at peace. Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 In 1984, the World Chess Championship was called off abruptly, due to the withered frame of a player who was competing for the title. He wasn't alone in experiencing the extreme physical effects of the game. Elite players can reportedly burn up to an absurd 6,000 calories in one day. Does that mean that thinking harder is a simple route to losing weight? 2 Well, when the body is at rest, we know that the brain uses up a startling 20% to 25% of the body's overall energy. This level of utilization actually makes the brain the most energy-expensive organ in the body, and yet it makes up only 2% of the body's weight overall. 3 So the more we put this organ to work, the more calories we'll burn? Technically, the answer is yes, for cognitively difficult tasks. What counts as a "difficult" mental task varies between individuals. But generally, it could be described as something that the brain cannot solve easily using previously learned routines, or tasks that change the conditions continuously. 4 However, deep thinking will not burn off the calories gained from eating a sugary snack. Because in relation to the brain's huge overall energy usage, which is devoted to a multitude of tasks, the energy required just to think harder is actually comparatively tiny. We're unconscious of most of what uses up the brain's energy. A lot of that activity is unrelated to conscious activities like learning how to sing or play the guitar. 5 The brain is able to allocate blood (and thus energy) to particular regions that are being active at that point. But the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant. So, while there might be significant increases in energy use at localized regions of the brain when we perform difficult cognitive tasks, when it comes to the whole brain's energy budget overall, these activities don't significantly alter it. 6 So, why did the chess champion grow too skinny to compete in his chess competition? The general consensus is that it mostly comes down to stress and reduced food consumption, not mental exhaustion. Keeping your body pumped up for action for long periods of time is very energy demanding. If you can't eat as often or as much as you can or would normally—then you might lose weight. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1"Tell me about yourself" may seem like an easy job interview question, but the open-ended nature of this question often leaves job seekers at a loss where to start. This common question is actually a critical test of a job candidate's communication skills, so it's important not to give an unprepared response or mess it up. "Tell me about yourself" is often the first question professional career coaches prepare people for when they give interview guidance. It's the opportunity for the candidate to take control of the narrative and tell their story in a way that really matters to their audience. It takes hard work and extensive preparation to answer this question well. 2 When a person goes to a job interview, their interviewer has presumably read their résumé, so they don't need to repeat the information. But that's what most people will do, as it's their instinct to recite things that are already on their résumé. It's important for job seekers to do their homework on two crucial aspects. 3 First, they are not just telling someone a fact about themselves. They're telling a story, and stories take work to create. Coming up with a good story means getting reflective about what made their career accomplishments something they're proud of and what strengths those accomplishments highlight. Candidates shouldn't pick a broad general strength to elaborate on, such as "I'm smart, I work hard and I get things done." To come up with multiple career accomplishments or examples for different interview questions, job seekers should talk with others, especially people who know them, partners, friends or co-workers who will bring up different stories than the ones they remember. 4 Secondly, candidates should know what's at stake for the company with this job opening. What they really are asking you is "Tell me why you are going to help me." If the person is a prepared candidate, they should have already figured out those things. They've read the job description and researched the company on the Internet. What job applicants ought to be looking for is what the company is up to, what they are trying to accomplish and what is preventing them from accomplishing those things. 5 How long should it take to answer? Around a minute. That's about right for most people's attention spans. Under a minute could seem rushed, while over two minutes will start to feel more like a speech. But the length of the answer is not an exact science, and candidates need to keep their career story focused and tailored to their audience. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What are the speakers mainly talking about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A driving test.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A video game.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Traffic routes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Cargo logistics.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“W: This is unbelievable. Unlike any video game I've ever played before, it's so boring, yet so relaxing at the same time.”。开篇点题 + 全段反复出现。对话第一句女方就用 Unlike any video game I've ever played before 与 this driving simulator 点出核心物『电子游戏 / 驾驶模拟器』。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about the driving simulator?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He found it instructive and realistic.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He bought it when touring Europe.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He was really drawn to its other versions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He introduced it to his brother last year.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“M: My brother introduced it to me last year. I was surprised to find how educational and realistic it was.”。被问者男方的直接应答。本题问男方的说法，答案就落在男方开口评价游戏的那句 I was surprised to find how educational and realistic it was；surprised to find（惊讶地发现）带明显的个人评价语气。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she really appreciates?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Traveling all over the country.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Driving from one city to another.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The details in the driving simulator.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The key role of the logistics industry.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“W: But I really appreciate all the details that go into the game.”。态度信号词 really appreciate。女方亲口说 I really appreciate all the details that go into the game，really appreciate 是直白的『看重』信号。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What outcome does the woman expect from the driving simulator?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Clearer road signs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) More people driving safely.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Stricter traffic rules.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) More self-driving trucks on the road. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P7：“W: But if it results in more drivers looking both ways before entering an intersection, I'd say that's a positive outcome.”。假设 + 积极评价的期望句。女方在末段用 if it results in more drivers looking both ways…I'd say that's a positive outcome。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about his life of being self-employed?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It isn't so enjoyable as he expected.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It isn't so motivating as he believed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It doesn't enable him to earn as much money as he used to.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It doesn't seem to offer as much freedom as he anticipated.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“There are obvious benefits, though I don't seem to have the freedom I anticipated as I just don't seem able to decline work offers.”。转折 though 后才是真意。前半 There are obvious benefits 是铺垫好处，though I don't seem to have the freedom I anticipated 里的 though 一转。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about managers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Not all of them care about their employees' behaviors.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Few of them are aware of their employees' feelings.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Few of them offer praise and reward to their employees.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Not all of them know how to motivate their employees.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care, of course. And certainly not all managers have a clue how to motivate people.”。范围词 not every／not all 直接定调。男士连用 Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care 和 not all managers have a clue how to motivate people…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What do both speakers value most about self-employment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Job satisfaction.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Self-awareness.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Autonomy.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Money.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“The motivator I value most is autonomy. I've learned not to sacrifice my prized autonomy by working all hours of the day and by saying yes to every cl…”。最高级 value most 直接点名。女士说 The motivator I value most is autonomy，value most 是最高级强调。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`On what point does the man agree with the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The importance of cultivating close relationships with clients.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The need for getting recommendations from their managers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The advantages of permanent full-time employment.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The way to explore employees' interests and talents. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P8：“You're right. That's an area I do need to put some more effort into—building closer relationships with those who engage my services and skills.”。表态词 You're right 后承接的观点。男士开口 You're right 明确表示赞同，紧接着复述他认同的那件事 building closer relationships with those who engage my services and skills。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the findings of the new study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Consumers visualize their activities in different weather.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Good weather triggers consumers' desire to go shopping.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Weather conditions influence consumers' buying behavior.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Consumers' mental states change with the prices of goods.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Now, a new study reveals how sunny and snowy conditions influence consumer behavior.”。点题动词 reveals 引出发现。开头段用 a new study reveals how sunny and snowy conditions influence consumer behavior，reveals（揭示）是典型的科研报道点题信号。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say may increase the value of products for consumers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Active consumption.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Direct correlation.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Individual association.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Mental visualization.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“This mental picture of using the towel increases the value of the product in the consumers' mind.”。结果动词 increases the value 锁定。原文 This mental picture of using the towel increases the value of the product 直接出现题干要找的 increases the value。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`How can the findings of the new study benefit online sellers according to the researchers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Enabling them to simplify their mathematical formulas.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Helping them determine what to sell and at what price.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Enabling them to sell their products at a higher price.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Helping them advertise a greater variety of products. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P5：“Online sellers often use complex mathematical formulas to determine what products to feature and how to price these products.”。对象词 + would allow them to 引出好处。录音点到对象 Online sellers 后，用 Incorporating more data about weather would allow them to make better decisions 给出好…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What is the accepted wisdom concerning the office environment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A naturally ventilated office is more comfortable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A cool office will boost employees' productivity.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Office air-conditioning should follow guidebooks.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Air-conditioning improves ventilation in the office.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“But recent studies have challenged the accepted wisdom that a cool office is more productive.”。accepted wisdom 后跟的同位语就是公认看法。信号是原文里 the accepted wisdom that a cool office is more productive 中 accepted wisdom 这个词。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What did researchers find from their review of all studies relating to air-conditioning and productivity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People in their comfort zone of temperature are more satisfied with their productivity.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) People in different countries vary in their tolerance to uncomfortable temperatures.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Twenty-two degrees is the optimal temperature for office workers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) There is a range of temperatures for people to feel comfortable.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But there is a range of at least three to four degrees which does not have any adverse impact.”。but 转折后的 a range 是结论落点。信号是 But there is a range 里的 But。前面说人们总爱追求唯一的最佳温度，But 之后才是研究者要纠正的真意：存在一个温度范围。转折后的内容才是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about using a little less air-conditioning during hot weather?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will have no negative impact on work.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will be immediately noticeable.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It will sharply decrease work efficiency.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will cause a lot of discomfort.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But there is a range of at least three to four degrees which does not have any adverse impact.”。does not have any adverse impact 是后果的否定信号。信号是原文的含蓄否定 does not have any adverse impact（没有任何不良影响）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What happens when people are used to an air-conditioned environment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They tend to favor lower temperatures.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They suffer from rapid temperature changes.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are not bothered by temperature extremes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They become less sensitive to high temperatures. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“If they're used to the environment which is air-conditioned, they tend to prefer lower temperatures.”。If 条件句与题干 when 对应，主句即后果。信号是原文 If they're used to the environment which is air-conditioned 这个 If 条件，与题干 when 习惯空调环境完全对应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the criticisms directed at the early research on solitude?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It overlooked the possibility that emotions may be controlled.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It ignored the fact that emotions are personal and subjective.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It classified emotions simply as either positive or negative.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It measured positive and negative emotions independently.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Also, it categorized emotions as simply positive or negative.”。列举信号 Also 引出第二条缺陷。第二段先说旧研究 It had a couple of shortcomings（有几处不足），再用 First 和 Also 逐条列出。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the results of the new research?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Sitting alone without doing anything seemed really distressing.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Solitude adversely affected the participants' mental well-being.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Sitting alone for 15 minutes made the participants restless.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Solitude had a reductive effect on high-arousal emotions.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“This experiment specifically aimed to determine the effect of solitude on high-arousal emotions. ...”。结果信号 The results were clear 后接 showed reductions。第三段先点明研究对象是 high-arousal emotions（高唤醒情绪），随后用 The results were clear 拉出结果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What did the second experiment in the new research find about solitude?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It proved hard to depict objectively.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It went hand in hand with sadness.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It helped increase low-arousal emotions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It tended to intensify negative emotions. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“A second study measured the effects of solitude on low-arousal emotions. ... That experiment found that all of these emotions were increased by time a…”。实验切换信号 A second study 引出新段结论。第四段以 A second study 切到第二个实验，对象是 low-arousal emotions（低唤醒情绪）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the brain when the body is at rest?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It uses up much less energy than it does in deep thinking.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It remains inactive without burning calories noticeably.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It continues to burn up calories to help us stay in shape.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It consumes almost a quarter of the body's total energy.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Well, when the body is at rest, we know that the brain uses up a startling 20% to 25% of the body's overall energy.”。条件状语 + 数字落点。录音以 when the body is at rest 这个条件状语开头，与题干前提逐字对上，紧接着的数字 20% to 25% 就是答案落点——条件词一出现、数字一念出，这句即答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the consumption of the brain's energy?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Much of the consumption has nothing to do with conscious activities.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It has something to do with the difficulty of the activities in question.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Energy usage devoted to active learning accounts for a big part of it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A significant amount of it is for performing difficult cognitive tasks.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“We're unconscious of most of what uses up the brain's energy. A lot of that activity is unrelated to conscious activities like learning how to sing or…”。占比措辞 most of / a lot of + 关联否定。录音用 most of what uses up the brain's energy 点出消耗的「大部分」。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the overall energy availability in the brain?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is believed to remain basically constant.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is a prerequisite for any mental activity.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is conducive to relieving mental exhaustion.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is thought to be related to food consumption. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P5：“But the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant.”。转折 But + 术语原词。录音先说大脑能给活跃区域调配血液能量，再用转折 But 一转，正面给出 the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant——转折后的这句与题干术语逐字对上。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the job interview question "tell me about yourself"?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Job candidates rarely take it seriously.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Job seekers tend to have a ready answer.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Job seekers often feel at a loss where to start in answering it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Job candidates can respond freely due to its open-ended nature.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“"Tell me about yourself" may seem like an easy job interview question, but the open-ended nature of this question often leaves job seekers at a loss w…”。转折 but 之后才是真意。句子前半 may seem like an easy job interview question 是铺垫，真正态度在转折词 but 之后：the open-ended nature of this question often leaves job…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What will most people do when they come to a job interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Follow their career coaches' guidelines.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Strive to take control of their narrative.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Do their best to impress the interviewer.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Repeat the information on their resume.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But that's what most people will do, as it's their instinct to recite things that are already on their résumé.”。主语 most people 的直接陈述。信号是题干主语 most people 在原文原样出现，紧跟其后的 recite things that are already on their résumé 就是这群人的实际动作，定位干脆。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`Why should job seekers talk with partners, friends and co-workers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To reflect on their past achievements as well as failures.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To produce examples for different interview questions.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To discuss important details they are going to present.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To identify a broad general strength to elaborate on.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“To come up with multiple career accomplishments or examples for different interview questions, job seekers should talk with others,”。目的状语 To… 在前点明意图。句首的目的状语 To come up with multiple career accomplishments or examples for different interview questions 直接交代了"和他人聊"的目的。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What other important preparations should job seekers make before an interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Getting acquainted with the human resources personnel.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Finding out why the company provides the job opening.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Figuring out what benefits the company is able to offer them.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Tailoring their expectations to the company's long-term goal.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Secondly, candidates should know what's at stake for the company with this job opening.”。顺序词 Secondly 引出第二要点。信号是顺序词 Secondly，它接着第三段的"First"列出第二项准备：know what's at stake for the company with this job opening，顺着顺序词就能锁定本题答案段。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`credentials`,B:`credited`,C:`cumulative`,D:`disruptions`,E:`federal`,F:`foreseeable`,G:`predictions`,H:`preference`,I:`scale`,J:`strangle`,K:`subtle`,L:`summoned`,M:`survive`,N:`vacancy`,O:`wedge`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-1`,text:`Virtually every activity that entails or facilitates in-person human interaction seems to be in the midst of a total meltdown as the coronavirus(冠状病毒) outbreak erases Americans' desire to travel. Amtrak says bookings are down 50 percent and cancelations are up 300 percent. Hotels in San Francisco are experiencing [26] rates between 70 and 80 percent. Broadway goes dark on Thursday night. Universities, now emptying their campuses, have never tried online learning on this [27] . White-collar companies like Amazon, Apple, and the New York Times are asking employees to work from home for the [28] future.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-2`,text:`But what happens after the coronavirus?`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-3`,text:`In some ways, the answer is: All the old normal stuff. The pandemic(大流行病) will take lives, [29] economies and destroy routines, but it will pass. Americans will never stop going to basketball games. They won't stop going on vacation. They'll meet to do business. No decentralizing technology so far—not telephones, not television, and not the internet—has dented that human desire to shake hands, despite technologists' [30] to the contrary.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-4`,text:`Yet there are real reasons to think that things will not return to the way they were last week. Small [31] create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good. The New York transit strike of 1980 is [32] with prompting several long-term changes in the city, including bus and bike lanes, and women wearing sports shoes to work. The Spanish flu pandemic of 1918 prompted the development of national health care in Europe.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-5`,text:`Here and now, this might not even be a question of [33] . It's not clear that the cruise industry will [34] . Or that public transit won't go broke without [35] assistance. The infrastructure might not even be in place to do what we were doing in 2019.`}]},n,{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-1`,text:`Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based "planetary diet." Nowhere is this more visible than in India.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-2`,text:`Earlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for "substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods." In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-3`,text:`The EAT report presumes that "traditional diets" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-4`,text:`In India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy "vegetarian diet" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-5`,text:`Currently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-6`,text:`None of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said "India has got such a great example" in sourcing protein from plants.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-7`,text:`But how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-8`,text:`Which is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-9`,text:`A diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-10`,text:`EAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to "spark conversations" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-11`,text:`The government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.`}]},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-1`,text:`Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called "Why Don't You—Yes But", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can "win" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-2`,text:`Part of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,text:`Should you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as "over-validation". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-4`,text:`"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen," Gottlieb writes. The other person "might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels." Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`Hotels in San Francisco are experiencing __26__ rates between 70 and 80 percent.`,answer:`N`,explanation:`experiencing ___ rates 动名词后需名词修饰 rates。vacancy rates = 空房率，与'酒店 70-80%'是直配术语。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-1`,quote:`Hotels in San Francisco are experiencing [26] rates between 70 and 80 percent.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`Universities, now emptying their campuses, have never tried online learning on this __27__.`,answer:`I`,explanation:`on this ___ 介词 + 限定词后需名词。on this scale 表'如此规模'是地道固定搭配。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-1`,quote:`Universities, now emptying their campuses, have never tried online learning on this [27] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`White-collar companies like Amazon, Apple, and the New York Times are asking employees to work from home for the __28__ future.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`the ___ future 中需修饰名词 future 的形容词。固定搭配 the foreseeable future（可预见的未来）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-1`,quote:`White-collar companies like Amazon, Apple, and the New York Times are asking employees to work from home for the [28] future.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`The pandemic(大流行病) will take lives, __29__ economies and destroy routines, but it will pass.`,answer:`J`,explanation:`take ... , ___ ... and destroy ... 三并列谓语动词原形。strangle economies = 扼杀经济，与 take lives/destroy routines 同向递进。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-3`,quote:`The pandemic(大流行病) will take lives, [29] economies and destroy routines, but it will pass.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`No decentralizing technology so far—not telephones, not television, and not the internet—has dented that human desire to shake hands, despite technologists' __30__ to the contrary.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`technologists' ___ to the contrary 所有格后需名词。predictions to the contrary = 与之相反的（技术专家的）预言。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-3`,quote:`No decentralizing technology so far—not telephones, not television, and not the internet—has dented that human desire to shake hands, despite technologists' [30] to the contrary.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`Small __31__ create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`Small ___ create ... 形容词后作主语的名词。Small disruptions ... big ones 与下文'纽约交通大罢工、西班牙流感'同义呼应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-4`,quote:`Small [31] create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`The New York transit strike of 1980 is __32__ with prompting several long-term changes in the city, including bus and bike lanes, and women wearing sports shoes to work.`,answer:`B`,explanation:`is ___ with 系动词后过去分词作被动。be credited with = 被归功于，与'罢工促成长期改变'契合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-4`,quote:`The New York transit strike of 1980 is [32] with prompting several long-term changes in the city, including bus and bike lanes, and women wearing sports shoes to work.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`Here and now, this might not even be a question of __33__.`,answer:`H`,explanation:`a question of ___ 介词 of 后需名词。a question of preference 表'并非偏好问题'。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-5`,quote:`Here and now, this might not even be a question of [33] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`It's not clear that the cruise industry will __34__.`,answer:`M`,explanation:`will ___ 助动词后需动词原形。survive 不及物用法贴合'能否存活'。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-5`,quote:`It's not clear that the cruise industry will [34] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`Or that public transit won't go broke without __35__ assistance.`,answer:`E`,explanation:`___ assistance 名词前需形容词作定语。federal assistance 表'联邦援助'是政经语境固定表达。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-bp-5`,quote:`Or that public transit won't go broke without [35] assistance.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`It seems some people today dream that a cutting-edge new technology might save them from the present ecological disaster.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`匹配 E 段：「a cutting-edge new technology might save them」。定位句“They suggest that geoengineering, cold fusion or faster-than-light spaceships might transcend once and for all the terrestrial con…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-E`,quote:`Some of today's narratives about the future seem to suggest that we too, like Prometheus, will be saved by a new Hercules, a divine engineer,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`According to one great thinker, it is most unfortunate if we lose the ability to think differently.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：「one great thinker」+「lose the ability to think differently」。定位句“Ernst Bloch, one of the leading philosophers of the future, wrote that 'the most tragic form of loss…is the loss of the capacity t…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-I`,quote:`We need an acknowledgement of our present ecological plight but also a language of positive change, visions of a better future.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Urgent attention should be paid to the ecological problems we have created in our pursuit of a comfortable life.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：「pursuit of a comfortable life」造成生态问题。定位句“Today we can no longer ignore the ecological curses that we have released in our search for warmth and comfort.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-C`,quote:`Today we can no longer ignore the ecological curses that we have released in our search for warmth and comfort.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`Even in the fast-food nation America, the number of vegetarians is on the rise.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`匹配 K 段：「fast-food nation America」+「vegetarians is on the rise」。定位句“In 2017, 6 per cent of US consumers claimed to be strictly vegetarian, up from 1 per cent in 2014.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-K`,quote:`The unscrupulous(无所顾忌的) commodification of food and the destruction of foodstuffs will continue to devastate soils, livelihoods and ecologies.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`The deterioration of the ecological system is accelerating because of the dramatic increase of human production and consumption.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`匹配 D 段：「dramatic increase of human production and consumption」导致生态恶化加速。定位句“The acceleration of human production, consumption and travel has changed the animate and inanimate spheres.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-D`,quote:`Acceleration is the signature of our time.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`It is obvious that solutions must be found to curb the fast worsening environment and social acceleration.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`匹配 G 段：「solutions must be found to curb」环境与社会加速。定位句“This much is clear: we need to find ways that help us flatten the hockey-stick curves that reflect our ever-faster pace of ecologi…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-G`,quote:`This much is clear: we need to find ways that help us flatten the hockey-stick curves that reflect our ever-faster pace of ecological destruction and…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Many people believe changing the world is possible, though it may take time and involve setbacks.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`匹配 A 段：「changing the world is possible」+「take time and involve setbacks」。定位句“Our world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change is possible.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-A`,quote:`Our world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change is possible.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`It might be wrong to expect that our world would be saved at one stroke with some miraculous technology.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：「saved at one stroke with some miraculous technology」是错的。定位句“The fact that we now imagine our planet as a whole does not mean that the 'rescue' of our planet will come with one big global str…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-F`,quote:`Yet, if we envisage our salvation to come from a deus ex machina(解围之神), from a divine engineer or a tech solutionist who will miraculously conjure up…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`It is human nature to cherish hopes for a better world.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：「human nature to cherish hopes for a better world」。定位句“What makes us 'authentic' as humans are visions of our 'potential'. In other words: living in hope makes us human.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-I`,quote:`We need an acknowledgement of our present ecological plight but also a language of positive change, visions of a better future.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Technology has given us humans the power to change the natural world, but we have paid a price for the change.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`匹配 B 段：「Technology」改变自然 +「paid a price」。定位句“Just as in the myth, technology—first fire and stone tools, and later farming, the steam engine and industry, fossil fuels,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-mp-B`,quote:`At the beginning of time—so goes the myth—humans suffered, shivering in the cold and dark until the titan(巨人) Prometheus stole fire from the gods.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What is more visible in India than anywhere else according to the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People's positive views on the proposals for a "planetary diet".`},{key:`B`,text:`B) People's reluctance to be compelled to eat plant-based food.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) People's preferences for the kind of food they consume.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) People's unwillingness to give up their eating habits.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based "planetary diet.…”。预判得到的反向强迫在印度最明显，等于 People's reluctance to be compelled to eat plant-based food，完全对齐。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-1`,quote:`Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based "planetary diet." Nowhere…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What would the EAT-Lancet Commission's report do to many people in countries like India?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Radically change their dietary habits.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Keep them further away from politics.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Make them even more undernourished.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Substantially reduce their food choices.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations…”。预判得到的让营养不良人群更糟，等于 Make them even more undernourished，完全对齐。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-2`,quote:`In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about food consumption in India?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People's diet will not change due to the EAT-Lancet report.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many people simply do not have access to foods they prefer.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) There is a growing popularity of a cereal-heavy vegetarian diet.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Policymakers help remove the barriers to people's choice of food.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“In India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers…”。预判得到的想吃的不等于能吃的，等于 do not have access to foods they prefer，完全对齐。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-4`,quote:`In India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around cla…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about a plant-based diet modeled on India?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may benefit populations whose traditional diet is meat-based.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It may be another blow to the economy in developing countries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It may help narrow the gap between the rich and poor countries.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may worsen the nourishment problem in low-income countries.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P8：“Which is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing…”。预判得到的加重弱势国家营养不良，等于 worsen the nourishment problem in low-income countries，完全对齐。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-8`,quote:`Which is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`How does the Indian government respond to the EAT-Lancet Commission's proposals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It accepts them at the expense of the long-term interests of its people.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It intends them to spark conversations among all Indian stakeholders.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It gives them approval regardless of opposition from nutrition experts.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It welcomes them as a tool to address chronic hunger and malnutrition.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P11：“The government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access…”。预判得到的接受但牺牲长远利益，等于 It accepts them at the expense of the long-term interests of its people，完全对齐。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p1-11`,quote:`The government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholes…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What is characteristic of a chronic complainer, according to psychiatrist Eric Berne?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They only feel angry about their ill treatment and resent whoever tries to help.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are chronically unhappy and ceaselessly find fault with people around them.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They constantly dismiss others' proposals while taking no responsibility for tackling the problem.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They lack the knowledge and basic skills required for successful conversations with their interlocutors.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them…”。预判要求同时含"驳回建议"与"不愿担责"两个语义；只有 They constantly dismiss others' proposals while taking no responsibility for tackling the problem. 完整覆盖了这两个语义。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-1`,quote:`The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author try to illustrate with the example of the newborn on one's doorstep?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People tend to think that one should not be held responsible for others' mistakes.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is easy to become attached to the position of overlooking one's own fault.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) People are often at a loss when confronted with a number of choices.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A distinction should be drawn between responsibility and fault.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility.”。例子要说明的不是"门口婴儿怎么处理"，而是"过错≠责任"这一概念区分；只有 A distinction should be drawn between responsibility and fault. 直接表达了这一区分。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-2`,quote:`For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author advise people to do to chronic complainers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Stop them from going further by agreeing with them.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Listen to their complaints ardently and sympathetically.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Ask them to validate their beliefs with further evidence.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Persuade them to clarify the confusion they have caused.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Should you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it,”。预判要求同时含"阻止抱怨"和"通过附和的方式"两层语义；只有 Stop them from going further by agreeing with them. 同时覆盖了这两层。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,quote:`Should you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What happens when chronic complainers receive over-validation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are motivated to find ingenious ways to persuade their interlocutor.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are prompted to come up with ideas for making possible changes.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are stimulated to make more complaints.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are encouraged to start arguing back.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back.”。预判要求答案是"想出改变现状的办法"，They are prompted to come up with ideas for making possible changes. 与原文 prompted to generate ideas 几乎逐对应——动词、宾语、目的状语全部对位。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,quote:`But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`How can one stop being a chronic complainer according to the author?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Analysing the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Avoiding hazardous traps in everyday social life.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Assuming responsibility to free oneself.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Awaiting something magical to happen.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`预判要求答案同时含"承担责任"和"获得解脱"两个语义；只有 Assuming responsibility to free oneself. 同时覆盖这两个语义，并与末段升华句构成精确等值。`,score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge, with a total length of 55 kilometers, is an extraordinary engineering feat in China. It is the longest sea-crossing bridge-and-tunnel system in the world, connecting the three cities. It cuts the travel time between the three cities from three hours to 30 minutes. This massive reinforced concrete bridge fully proves that China is capable of building record-breaking mega-structures. It will boost regional integration and promote economic growth. The bridge is key to China’s master plan to develop its own Greater Bay Area. China hopes to turn the Greater Bay Area into a region that rivals the bay areas of San Francisco, New York and Tokyo in technological innovation and economic prosperity.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`港珠澳大桥(Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge)全长55公里，是我国一项不同寻常的工程壮举。大桥将三个城市连接起来，是世界上最长的跨海桥梁和隧道系统。大桥将三个城市之间的旅行时间从3小时缩短到30分钟。这座跨度巨大的钢筋混凝土大桥充分证明中国有能力建造创纪录的巨型建筑。它将助推区域一体化，促进经济增长。大桥是中国发展自己的大湾区总体规划的关键。中国希望将大湾区建成在技术创新和经济繁荣上能与旧金山、纽约和东京的湾区相媲美的地区。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①港珠澳大桥用官方约定俗成的英文专名 the Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge——输入已给出 港珠澳大桥(Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge)，直接照搬这个权威译名，；②大湾区译为 the Greater Bay Area（约定俗成的官方译名），对应 大湾区总体规划 中的「大湾区」；全篇两处「大湾区」必须同一译法，首字母大写；③钢筋混凝土意译为 reinforced concrete（固定术语，不可逐字拆成 steel and concrete），对应 钢筋混凝土大桥，作定语修饰 bridge；④区域一体化译为 regional integration（固定搭配），对应 它将助推区域一体化，integration 此处为不可数、不加冠词。`,score:15}]}]},"cet6-2020-12-s3":{subtitle:`2020 年 12 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2020年12月英语六级真题第3套（听力与第1套共用），站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`In an era of rapid change, students inevitably encounter difficulties in their studies, career hunting and daily life. It is therefore of great importance that they be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges.

There are several convincing reasons why this ability deserves cultivation. To begin with, facing challenges pushes students to unlock their hidden potential. A first-year student who dares to tackle a tough research project, for instance, often discovers strengths he never knew he possessed. More importantly, meeting challenges sharpens one's resilience and problem-solving skills. Every setback in a competition or an internship teaches valuable lessons that no textbook can offer. As a result, such students grow into independent thinkers who refuse to give up easily.

In conclusion, since the ability to meet challenges prepares young people for fierce competition and lifelong growth, schools and families should spare no effort to foster it. Only by embracing challenges rather than avoiding them can students truly become masters of their own future.`,guidance:[`背景一句随即表态。首段用 In an era of rapid change 一句背景引入，紧接着 It is therefore of great importance that they be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges 直接亮明立场，落实了『背景引入句架』与『亮明立场句架』，一两句就完成表态，正面对准 why students should be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges。`,`递进串两理由各配微例。主体段用 To begin with … More importantly … 把『潜能』『抗挫与解决问题』两条理由分层串起，A first-year student … for instance 与 a competition or an internship 各给一个大学生场景微例，最后 As a result 小结回扣中心，落实了理由分层、举例过渡、因果小结三个句架。`,`因果归纳后倒装呼吁。尾段 In conclusion, since … 把前文理由归纳为一句因果并重申立场，末句 Only by embracing challenges … can students truly become masters of their own future 用倒装强调唯一途径收束，落实了归纳因果与唯一路径两个句架，形成首尾闭环。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：背景一句随即表态。首段用 In an era of rapid change 一句背景引入，紧接着 It is therefore of great importance that they be encouraged to develop the ability to meet challenges 直接亮明立场，落实了『背景引入句架』与『亮明立场句架』，一两句就完成表态。递进串两理由各配微例。主体段用 To begin with … More importantly … 把『潜能』『抗挫与解决问题』两条理由分层串起，A first-year student … for instance 与 a competition or an internship 各给一个大学生场景微例，最后 As a result 小结回扣中心，落实了理由分层、举例过渡、因果小结三个句架。因果归纳后倒装呼吁。尾段 In conclusion, since … 把前文理由归纳为一句因果并重申立场，末句 Only by embracing challenges … can students truly become masters of their own future 用倒装强调唯一途径收束，落实了归纳因果与唯一路径两个句架，形成首尾闭环。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 M: Good morning, Safe House Insurance. My name is Paul. How can I help you today? 2 W: Morning. I wouldn't say that it's good from where I am standing. This is Miss Wilson and this is the third time I've called this week since receiving your letter about our insurance claim. I'm getting a little fed up with my calls about my claim being completely disregarded. 3 M: Miss Wilson, thank you for calling back. Can I take some details to help me look at your claim? 4 W: It's Miss May Wilson of 15 South Sea Road in Cornwall, and the details are that our village was extensively flooded 2 months ago. The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water and five of us have been living in a caravan ever since. You people are still withholding the money we are entitled to over a bizarre technical detail. And it's not acceptable, Paul! 5 M: Miss Wilson, according to the notes on your account, the bizarre technical detail that you mentioned refers to the fact that you hadn't paid house insurance the month before the incident. 6 W: That money left our account, and now that you should be paying out, you're suddenly saying that you didn't receive it on time. I'm really skeptical about this claim. 7 M: The contract does say that any missed payment in a year will affect the terms and conditions of the insurance contract and may affect claims. Of course, I can pass you on to my manager to talk to you more about this. 8 W: I've already spoken to him, and you can tell him I'm furious now, and that your company has a lawsuit on its hands. You will be hearing from my lawyer. Goodbye. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 W: How do you feel about the future of artificial intelligence? Personally, I feel quite optimistic about it. 2 M: AI? I'm not so optimistic actually. In fact, it's something we should be concerned about. 3 W: Well, it will help us humans understand ourselves better. And, when we have a better understanding of ourselves, we can improve the world. 4 M: Well, one thing is for sure: Technology is evolving faster than our ability to understand it. And in the future, AI will make jobs kind of pointless. 5 W: I think artificial intelligence will actually help create new kinds of jobs, which would require less of our time and allow us to be centered on creative tasks. 6 M: I doubt that very much. Probably the last job that will remain will be writing AI software. And then eventually, AI will just write its own software. 7 W: At that time, we're going to have a lot of jobs which nobody will want to do, so we'll need artificial intelligence for the robots to take care of the old guys, like us. 8 M: I don't know. There's a risk that human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life. AI will be able to completely simulate a person in every way possible. In fact, some people think we are in the simulation, right now. 9 W: That's impossible. Humans can't even make a mosquito. Computers only have chips. People have brains, and that's where the wisdom comes from. 10 M: Once it's fully developed, AI will become tired of trying to communicate with humans, as we would be much slower thinkers in comparison. 11 W: I'm not so sure. A computer is a computer, and a computer is just a toy. 12 M: Computers can easily communicate incredibly fast, so the computer will just get impatient talking to humans. It will be barely getting any information out. 13 W: Well, I believe there is a benevolent future with AI. I also think you watch too many science fiction films. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 To achieve financial security, how much you save is always more important than the amount you earn or how shrewdly you invest. If you are under 30 years old, your goal should be to save 20% of your monthly income after tax deductions. This is irrespective of how much you earn. Approximately 50% should be reserved for essentials like food and accommodation. The remaining 30% is for recreation and entertainment. 2 But for many young people, it'll be difficult to designate such a large proportion of their income for savings. If you find it hard to save any money at all, start by cutting all unnecessary spending. Allocate a tiny amount of 1 or 2 percent for savings, and gradually increase that amount. Always keep that 20% goal in mind to prevent yourself from becoming complacent. It can be challenging to stick to such a strict plan, but if you adopt the right mindset, you should be able to make it work for you. 3 So, what should you be doing with the money that you're saving? Some must be kept easily accessible in case you need some cash in an emergency. The largest proportion should be invested in retirement plans, either through your employer or privately. And you can keep some money for high-risk but potentially lucrative investments. Dividends can be reinvested or used to purchase something you like. By following this plan, you should hopefully be able to enjoy your life now, and still be financially secure in the future. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 I work in advertising and I like to keep up with current trends, mainly because I'm aware that we live in an image-obsessed world. However, when I first started my job, occasionally I'd catch a glimpse of myself in the lifts, and find myself thinking that I looked a total mess. Was I being held back by my choice of clothing? The short answer is yes, especially when clients are quick to judge you on your style rather than your work. 2 But no one can be unique with their outfit every day. I mean, that's why uniforms were invented. So, here's what I did. I created my own uniform. To do this, I chose an appropriate outfit. Then I bought multiple items of the same style in different shades. Now, I never worry about what I'm wearing in the morning even if I do get a bit tired of just wearing the same classic pieces. 3 Overall, when it comes to work, you have to ask yourself: will looking smarter enhance my ability to do my job? For some, this question may not be an issue at all. Especially if you work remotely and rarely see your colleagues or clients face to face. But if your job involves interacting with other people, the answer to this is often yes. So rather than fighting the system, I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work. If that means playing it safe with your image, then let's face it. It's probably worth it. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Did you know that Americans have approximately three times the amount of space we had 50 years ago? Therefore, you'd think we'd have sufficient room for all of our possessions. On the contrary, the personal storage business is now a growing industry. We've got triple the space, but we've become such enthusiastic consumers that we require even more. This phenomenon has resulted in significant credit card debt, enormous environmental footprints, and perhaps not coincidentally, our happiness levels have failed to increase over the same half century. 2 I'm here to suggest an alternative: having less might actually be a preferable decision. Many of us have experienced, at some stage, the pleasure of possessing less. I propose that less stuff and less space can not only help you economize, but also simplify your life. 3 I recently started an innovative project to discover some creative solutions that offered me everything I required. By purchasing an apartment that was 40 square meters instead of 60, I immediately saved $200,000. Smaller space leads to reduced utility bills, and also a smaller carbon footprint. Because it is designed around an edited collection of possessions, limited to my favorite stuff, I'm really excited to live there. 4 How can we live more basically? Firstly, we must ruthlessly cut the unnecessary objects out of our lives. To stem consumption, we should think before we buy, and ask ourselves, "Will it truly make me happier?" Obviously, we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years. 5 Secondly, we require space efficiency. We want appliances that are designed for use most of the time, not for occasional use. Why own a six-burner stove when you rarely use even three burners? 6 Finally, we need multifunctional spaces and housewares. I combined a movable wall with transforming furniture to get more out of my limited space. Consider my coffee table. It increases in size to accommodate ten. My office is tucked away, easily hidden. My bed simply pops out of the wall. For guests, I can relocate the movable wall and utilize the foldable guest beds I installed. 7 I'm not saying we should all live in tiny apartments, but consider the benefits of an edited life. When you return home and walk through your front door, take a moment to ask yourselves, "Could I do with a little life editing? Would that give me more freedom and more time?" Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 Now, believe it or not, people sometimes lie in order to maintain a good, honest reputation—even if it hurts them to do so. At least, this is what a team of scientists is suggesting, with evidence to prove it. 2 Picture this scenario: You often drive for work and can be compensated for up to 400 miles per month. Most people at your company drive about 300 miles each month. But this month, you drove 400 miles. How many miles do you think you'd claim in your expense report? 3 The scientists asked this exact question as part of the study we're discussing today. With surprising results, they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure, giving an average answer of 384 miles. In other words, they lied about the number of miles, even though they would forfeit money they were owed. 4 The researchers believe this was to seem honest, with the assumption being that others would be suspicious of a high expense claim. But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment? The researchers explain that many people care a great deal about their reputation and how they'll be judged by others. If they care enough, their concern about appearing honest and not losing the respect of others may be greater than their desire to actually be honest. 5 The researchers assert that the findings suggest that when people obtain very favorable outcomes, they anticipate other people's suspicious reactions and prefer lying and appearing honest to telling the truth and appearing as selfish liars. 6 So why is this research important? Well, experts generally agree there are two main types of lie-selfish lies and lies that are meant to benefit others. The first, as you may predict, is for selfish gain, such as submitting a fraudulent claim to an insurance company, while the second involves lying to help others or not offend others, for example, telling a friend whose outfit you don't like that they look great. 7 But the researchers are suggesting a third type of lying—lying to maintain a good reputation. Now this hypothesis is new and some skeptics argue that this isn't a whole new category of lie. 8 But the findings seem intuitive to me. After all, one of the main motivations for lying is to increase our worth in the eyes of others. So it seems highly likely that people will lie to seem honest. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1 Why do old people dislike new music? As I've grown older, I often hear people my age say things like "They just don't make good music like they used to". Why does this happen? Luckily, psychology can give us some insights into this puzzle. Musical tastes begin to crystallize as early as age 13 or 14. By the time we're in our early 20s, these tastes get locked into place pretty firmly. 2 In fact, studies have found that by the time we turn 33, most of us have stopped listening to new music. Meanwhile, popular songs released when you're in your early teens are likely to remain quite popular among your age group through the rest of your life. 3 There could be a biological explanation for this, as there's evidence that the brain's ability to make subtle distinctions between different chords, rhythms and melodies deteriorates with age. So to older people, newer, less familiar songs might all "sound the same". 4 But there may be some simpler reasons for older people's aversion to newer music. One of the most researched laws of social psychology is something called the "mere exposure effect", which, in essence, means that the more we're exposed to something, the more we tend to like it. This happens with people we know, the advertisements we see and the songs we listen to. 5 When you're in your early teens, you probably spend a fair amount of time listening to music or watching music videos. Your favorite songs and artists become familiar, comforting parts of your routine. 6 For many people over 30, job and family obligations increase, so there's less time to spend discovering new music. Instead, many will simply listen to old, familiar favorites from that period of their lives when they had more free time. 7 Of course, those teen years weren't necessarily carefree. They're famously confusing, which is why so many TV shows and movies revolve around high school turmoil. 8 Psychology research has shown that the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense than those that come later. And we also know that intense emotions are associated with stronger memories and preferences. Both of these might explain why the songs we listen to during this period become so memorable and beloved. 9 So there's nothing wrong with your parents because they don't like your music. Rather it's all part of the natural order of things. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What is the woman complaining about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Her house has not been repaired in time.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She has failed to reach the manager again.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Her claim has been completely disregarded.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She has not received any letter from the man.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“I'm getting a little fed up with my calls about my claim being completely disregarded.”。诉苦情绪句即落点。这句以 I'm getting a little fed up(我快受够了)这种带强烈不满情绪的表态领出，正是女方主动开口诉苦的那一句——抱怨题的答案就落在说话人情绪外露、点名诉苦对象的句子上。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What is the problem the woman's family encountered?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The ground floor of their cottage was flooded.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their caravan was washed away by the flood.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their entire house was destroyed by the flood.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The roof of their cottage collapsed in the flood.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water and five of us have been living in a caravan ever since.”。被问者细述灾情即落点。女方应保险员"take some details"之请，亲口细述受灾经过，The entire ground floor of our cottage was submerged in water 就是她对"灾情"的直接陈述；对话题答案落在被问方正面回应处。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What has caused the so-called "bizarre technical detail" according to the man?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The woman's misreading of the insurance company's letter.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The woman's ignorance of the insurance company's policy.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The woman's inaccurate description of the whole incident.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The woman's failure to pay her house insurance in time.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P5：“Miss Wilson, according to the notes on your account, the bizarre technical detail that you mentioned refers to the fact that you hadn't paid house ins…”。refers to后即解释。男方用 refers to the fact that(指的是…这一事实)这一引子点明缘由，引子后面那句正是对"bizarre technical detail"的官方解释；据男方说法答原因，就锁定这句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she would do at the end of the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) File a lawsuit against the insurance company.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Talk to the manager of Safe House Insurance.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Consult her lawyer about the insurance policy.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Revise the terms and conditions of the contract. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P8：“I've already spoken to him, and you can tell him I'm furious now, and that your company has a lawsuit on its hands.”。结尾表态句即落点。对话最后一段女方撂下狠话 your company has a lawsuit on its hands，这是她临挂电话前的行动宣告；题干问 at the end 的打算，落点就在这句结尾表态。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speakers from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They work in different fields of AI technology.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They disagree about the future of AI technology.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They differ greatly in their knowledge of modern technology.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are both worried about the negative impact of technology.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“W: Personally, I feel quite optimistic about it. M: AI? I'm not so optimistic actually. In fact, it's something we should be concerned about.”。两人态度正面对撞。女方说 I feel quite optimistic about it，男方紧接着以转折否定回应 I'm not so optimistic actually，一个 optimistic、一个 not so optimistic，态度词正面对撞。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What will new kinds of jobs be like, according to the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Simply writing AI software.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stimulating and motivating.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Less time-consuming and focusing on creation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) More demanding and requiring special training.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“W: I think artificial intelligence will actually help create new kinds of jobs, which would require less of our time and allow us to be centered on cr…”。被问者的直接描述句。题干问"女方说新工作怎样"，答案就落在女方描述新工作的那句 require less of our time and allow us to be centered on creative tasks——这是被问者对该对象的直接定性，正是细节题要锁定的应答句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What is the risk the man anticipates?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) There could be jobs nobody wants to do.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Digital life could replace human civilization.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Humans would be tired of communicating with one another.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Old people would be taken care of solely by unfeeling robots.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“M: There's a risk that human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life.”。risk预警词直接领出风险。男方原句以 There's a risk that 直接点出风险，预警信号词 risk 一响，紧跟其后的 human civilization could be replaced by a superior type of digital life 就…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What is the man's concern about AI technology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Life will become like a science fiction film.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will take away humans' jobs altogether.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Chips will be inserted in human brains.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will be smarter than human beings. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P10：“M: Once it's fully developed, AI will become tired of trying to communicate with humans, as we would be much slower thinkers in comparison.”。对比中暗示AI更强。男方 we would be much slower thinkers in comparison 用人机对比，明说人比 AI 慢得多，再加 Computers can easily communicate incredibly fast。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What are people under 30 advised to do to achieve financial security?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Restrain themselves from high-risk investments.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Save one-fifth of their net monthly income.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Invest shrewdly in lucrative businesses.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Try to earn as much money as possible.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“If you are under 30 years old, your goal should be to save 20% of your monthly income after tax deductions.”。目标表态句 your goal should be。讲者用 your goal should be 这一表态结构领出建议，正是听力里锁定答案句的强信号；再加上题干点名的 under 30 在 If you are under 30 years old 同句出现，定位直接命中。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What should people do if they find it difficult to follow the speaker's advice on their financial plan?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Start by doing something small.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Ask a close friend for advice.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Try to stick to their initial plan.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Cut 20% of their daily spending.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“If you find it hard to save any money at all, start by cutting all unnecessary spending.”。条件句 If you find it hard 后的对策。题干的 difficult 在录音里以 If you find it hard to save any money at all 出现。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker think is important for achieving financial security?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) An optimistic attitude.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) An ambitious plan.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A proper mindset.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A keen interest. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“It can be challenging to stick to such a strict plan, but if you adopt the right mindset, you should be able to make it work for you.”。转折 but 之后的 the right mindset。讲者先说计划难坚持，再用 but if you adopt the right mindset 转折，转折后才是真意所在——the right mindset 正是被强调为关键的要素，转折 but 后即答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speaker when she first started her job?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She was uninterested in advertising.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She found her outfit inappropriate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She was unhappy with fashion trends.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She often checked herself in a mirror.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“However, when I first started my job, occasionally I'd catch a glimpse of myself in the lifts, and find myself thinking that I looked a total mess.”。转折 However 后才是真意。前半句 I like to keep up with current trends 是铺垫，真正的落点在转折词 However 之后——她坦白刚入职时觉得 I looked a total mess，这正是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`Why were uniforms invented according to the speaker?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To save the trouble of choosing a unique outfit every day.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To meet the expectations of fashion-conscious clients.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To keep up with the current trends.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To save the expenses on clothing.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But no one can be unique with their outfit every day. I mean, that's why uniforms were invented.”。that's why 直陈因果。因果信号 that's why uniforms were invented 直接把"制服为何被发明"和前一句挂钩——原因就是前句 no one can be unique with their outfit every day。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about looking smarter?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It enhances people's ability to work independently.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It helps people succeed in whatever they are doing.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It matters a lot in jobs involving interaction with others.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It boosts one's confidence when looking for employment.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“But if your job involves interacting with other people, the answer to this is often yes.”。转折 But + 条件句给限定。前一句说有人可能 work remotely and rarely see your colleagues or clients 时不成问题。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker advise people to do in an image-obsessed world?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Design their own uniform to appear unique.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Wear classic pieces to impress their clients.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Fight the ever-changing trends in fashion.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Do whatever is possible to look smart. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“So rather than fighting the system, I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work.”。I think we should 直陈主张。表态信号 I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work 是作者的核心建议。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What has prevented Americans' happiness levels from increasing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Their obsession with consumption.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their failure to accumulate wealth.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The ever-increasing costs of housing.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The deterioration of the environment.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“We've got triple the space, but we've become such enthusiastic consumers that we require even more.”。因果信号 has resulted in 串起原因与结果。答案句靠因果链定位：This phenomenon has resulted in 把前句的『现象』和后面包括『幸福感没上升』在内的一串后果连起来。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What things should we possess according to the speaker?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Things that are rare to find.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Things that cost less money.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Things that boost efficiency.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Things that we cherish most.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Obviously, we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years.”。转折 but 之后才是真意。答案句是个转折结构：we should possess some great stuff, but we want belongings that we're going to love for years，but 之前是垫场。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the items in the speaker's home?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They serve multiple purposes.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They benefit the environment.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are mostly durable.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are easily disposable. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“Finally, we need multifunctional spaces and housewares. I combined a movable wall with transforming furniture to get more out of my limited space.”。举例铺陈共同指向『多功能』。答案句先给总述 we need multifunctional spaces and housewares，再用咖啡桌能变大、床能翻出墙这些例子佐证——一连串例子都在证明同一个特征『一物多用』，总述句就是落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What did a team of scientists find in their study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) All respondents were afraid of making a high expense claim.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A number of respondents gave an average answer of 400 miles.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Over 10% of the respondents lied about the distance they drove.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Most of the respondents got compensated for driving 384 miles.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“With surprising results, they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure,”。研究发现的标志句 they found that。they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure 用 found that 直接领…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`Why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment according to the researchers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They responded to colleagues' suspicion.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They cared about other people's claims.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They wanted to protect their reputation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They endeavored to actually be honest.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment? The researchers explain that many people care a great deal about their reputation and h…”。自问自答 + The researchers explain 领出原因。录音先用 But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment 自设问题。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker think of the researchers' findings?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They seem positive.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They seem intuitive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are illustrative.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are conclusive. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“But the findings seem intuitive to me. After all, one of the main motivations for lying is to increase our worth in the eyes of others.”。转折 But + 第一人称 to me 标出本人态度。前段刚说 some skeptics argue 有人质疑，本段以 But the findings seem intuitive to me 转折，并用 to me 明确这是说话人自己的判断。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker mainly discuss in this talk?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Deterioration in the quality of new music.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Insights into the features of good music.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Older people's changing musical tastes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Older people's aversion to new music.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Why do old people dislike new music? As I've grown older, I often hear people my age say things like "They just don't make good music like they used t…”。开头设问即点题。篇章第一句就是一个问句 Why do old people dislike new music?，开门见山抛出全篇要回答的问题，这种开头设问句就是主旨信号，后面整段都在为它找答案。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What have studies found about most people by the time they turn 33?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They no longer listen to new music.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They find all music sounds the same.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They can make subtle distinctions about music.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They seldom listen to songs released in their teens.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“In fact, studies have found that by the time we turn 33, most of us have stopped listening to new music.”。数字 33 顺听定位。原文 by the time we turn 33 与题干的 turn 33 完全对上，数字是听力里最醒目的定位锚；同时 studies have found 与题干 studies found 呼应，双重信号确认这就是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from one of the most researched laws of social psychology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The more you are exposed to something, the more familiar it'll be to you.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The more you are exposed to something, the deeper you'll understand it.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The more you experience something, the longer you'll remember it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The more you experience something, the better you'll appreciate it.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“One of the most researched laws of social psychology is something called the "mere exposure effect", which, in essence,”。定义句 means that 引出本质。原文 the most researched laws of social psychology 与题干完全对上，紧跟的 in essence, means that（本质上是说）是一个明确的下定义信号。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What might explain the fact that songs people listen to in their teen years are memorable and beloved?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Teenagers' memories are more lasting.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Teenagers' emotions are more intense.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Teenagers are much more sensitive.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Teenagers are much more sentimental.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P8：“Psychology research has shown that the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense than those that come later.”。intense emotions 起始的因果链。原文先说 the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense（少年情绪更强烈）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`amazing`,B:`closer`,C:`driven`,D:`engaged`,E:`malignant`,F:`oppressing`,G:`premises`,H:`random`,I:`sentimentally`,J:`spiritually`,K:`temptations`,L:`thriftier`,M:`tickled`,N:`unique`,O:`vulnerable`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-1`,text:`Social distancing is putting people out of work, canceling school and tanking the stock market. It has been [26] by fear, and it is creating even more fear as money problems and uncertainty grow. However, at its core is love, and a sacrifice to protect those most [27] to the coronavirus(冠状病毒) effects—the elderly, people with compromised immune systems, and those whose life-saving resources would be used up by a [28] epidemic.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-2`,text:`Americans make life-saving decisions every day as a matter of course. We cut food into bite-sized pieces, we wear seatbelts, and we take care not to exceed the speed limit. But social distancing is [29] in that it is completely self-sacrificing. Those who will benefit may be the elderly relatives of the [30] person we didn't pass in Starbucks, on the subway, or in the elevator.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-3`,text:`Social distancing is millions of people making hundreds of sacrifices to keep the elderly alive. It doesn't include the [31] to run from society or make an excuse to avoid one's obligations—such as life-saving medical work or the parental obligation to buy groceries. What it does include is applying love through caution. And in doing so, it offers an [32] opportunity for those who care about the elderly to find new ways to love them.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-4`,text:`If we're not [33] as much in our normal work or school, we have extra time to call parents and grandparents. We can also ask elderly relatives how to best support them [34] and use our sacrifices as an opportunity to bring us, our community and the world [35] .`}]},r,{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-1`,text:`Why does social media trigger feelings of loneliness and inadequacy? Because instead of being real life, it is, for the most part, impression management, a way of marketing yourself, carefully choosing and filtering the pictures and words to put your best face forward.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-2`,text:`Online "friends" made through social media do not follow the normal psychological progression of an interpersonal relationship. You share neither physical time nor emotional conversations over the Internet. You simply communicate photographs and catchy posts to a diverse group of people whom you have "friended" or "followed" based on an accidental interaction. This is not to say that your social media friends can't be real friends. They absolutely can, but the two are not synonymous. Generally speaking, there are no unfiltered comments or casually taken photos on our social media pages. And, rightfully so, because it wouldn't feel safe to be completely authentic and vulnerable with some of our "friends" whom we don't actually know or with whom trust has yet to be built.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-3`,text:`Social media can certainly be an escape from the daily grind, but we must be cautioned against the negative effects, such as addiction, on a person's overall psychological well-being.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-4`,text:`As humans, we yearn for social connection. Scrolling(滚动) through pages of pictures and comments, however, does not provide the same degree of fulfillment as face-to-face interactions do. Also, we tend to idealize others' lives and compare our downfalls to their greatest accomplishments, ending in feelings of loneliness and inadequacy.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-5`,text:`Social media can lead people on the unhealthy quest for perfection. Some people begin to attend certain events or travel to different places so that they can snap that "perfect" photo. They begin to seek validation through the number of people who "like" their posts. In order for it to play a psychologically healthy role in your social life, social media should supplement an already healthy social network. Pictures and posts should be byproducts of life's treasured moments and fun times, not the planned and calculated image that one is putting out into cyberspace in an attempt to fill insecurities or unmet needs.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-6`,text:`Ultimately, social media has increased our ability to connect with various types of people all over the globe. It has opened doors for businesses and allowed us to stay connected to people whom we may not otherwise get to follow. However, social media should feel like a fun experience, not one that contributes to negative thoughts and feelings. If the latter is the case, increasing face-to-face time with trusted friends, and minimizing time scrolling online, will prove to be a reminder that your social network is much more rewarding than any "like," "follow" or "share" can be.`}]},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-1`,text:`Imagine that an alien species landed on Earth and, through their mere presence, those aliens caused our art to vanish, our music to homogenize, and our technological know-how to disappear. That is effectively what humans have been doing to our closest relatives—chimps(大猩猩).`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-2`,text:`Back in 1999, a team of scientists led by Andrew Whiten showed that chimps from different parts of Africa behave very differently from one another. Some groups would get each other's attention by rapping branches with their knuckles(指关节), while others did it by loudly ripping leaves with their teeth. The team identified 39 of these traditions that are practiced by some communities but not others—a pattern that, at the time, hadn't been seen in any animal except humans. It was evidence, the team said, that chimps have their own cultures.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-3`,text:`It took a long time to convince skeptics that such cultures exist, but now we have plenty of examples of animals learning local traditions from one another.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-4`,text:`But just when many scientists have come to accept the existence of animal cultures, many of those cultures might vanish. Ammie Kalan and her colleagues have shown, through years of intensive fieldwork, that the very presence of humans has eroded the diversity of chimp behavior. Where we flourish, their cultures wither. It is a bitterly ironic thing to learn on the 20th anniversary of Whiten's classic study.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-5`,text:`"It's amazing to think that just 60 years ago, we knew next to nothing of the behavior of our sister species in the wild," Whiten says. "But now, just as we are truly getting to know our primate(灵长类) cousins, the actions of humans are closing the window on all we have discovered."`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-6`,text:`"Sometimes in the rush to conserve the species, I think we forget about the individuals," says Cat Hobaiter, a professor at the University of St. Andrews. "Each population, each community, even each generation of chimps is unique. An event might only have a small impact on the total population of chimps, but it may wipe out an entire community—an entire culture. No matter what we do to restore habitat or support population growth, we may never be able to restore that culture."`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-7`,text:`No one knows whether the destruction of chimp culture is getting worse. Few places have tracked chimp behavior over long periods, and those that have are also more likely to have protected their animals from human influence.`},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-8`,text:`Obviously, conservationists need to think about saving species in a completely new way—by preserving animal traditions as well as bodies and genes. "Instead of focusing only on the conservation of genetically based entities like species, we now need to also consider culturally based entities," says Andrew Whiten.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`It has been __26__ by fear`,answer:`C`,explanation:`has been ___ by=现在完成被动。be driven by fear=被恐惧驱动。driven 与 by 经典搭配 be driven by『被…驱动』，主语 It 指代 social distancing，整句意为『社交距离是由恐惧驱动的，反过来又制造更多恐惧』。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-1`,quote:`It has been [26] by fear, and it is creating even more fear as money problems and uncertainty grow.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`a sacrifice to protect those most __27__ to the coronavirus(冠状病毒) effects`,answer:`O`,explanation:`those most ___ to=形容词最高级位。be vulnerable to=易受…伤害。vulnerable 与 to 是经典搭配 be vulnerable to sth，意为『易受…伤害的』，正好契合下文列举的弱势群体 the elderly, people with compromised immune systems——他们正是最易受冠状病毒影响的人群。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-1`,quote:`However, at its core is love, and a sacrifice to protect those most [27] to the coronavirus(冠状病毒) effects—the elderly, people with compromised immune systems, and those whose life-saving resources would be used up by a [28] epidemic.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`those whose life-saving resources would be used up by a __28__ epidemic`,answer:`E`,explanation:`a ___ epidemic=定冠词+空+名词。malignant epidemic=恶性疫情。malignant 本义『恶性的』，常与疾病/疫情搭配，表程度严重、致命；前文 life-saving resources would be used up 暗示这是一场会把救命资源耗尽的严重疫情，正与 malignant 的『恶性』语义匹配。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-1`,quote:`However, at its core is love, and a sacrifice to protect those most [27] to the coronavirus(冠状病毒) effects—the elderly, people with compromised immune systems, and those whose life-saving resources would be used up by a [28] epidemic.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`But social distancing is __29__ in that it is completely self-sacrificing`,answer:`N`,explanation:`is ___ in that=系动词后表语。unique in that=独特之处在于。unique in that 是固定句式『独特之处在于…』，与前句 Americans make life-saving decisions every day as a matter of course 中举的系安全带、不超速等日常救命决策形成转折——social distancing 与众不同之…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-2`,quote:`But social distancing is [29] in that it is completely self-sacrificing.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`Those who will benefit may be the elderly relatives of the __30__ person we didn't pass in Starbucks, on the subway, or in the elevator`,answer:`H`,explanation:`the ___ person=冠词+空+名词。random person=路上随便一个人。random 表『随机的、任意的』，random person 即『偶然遇到的某个不特定的人』；下文列举的 Starbucks, on the subway, or in the elevator 正是这种生活里『随便碰到一个陌生人』的典型场所，与 random 高度契合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-2`,quote:`Those who will benefit may be the elderly relatives of the [30] person we didn't pass in Starbucks, on the subway, or in the elevator.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`It doesn't include the __31__ to run from society or make an excuse`,answer:`K`,explanation:`include the ___ to do=不定式逻辑主语位。temptation to do=想…的诱惑。temptations 与 to do 是搭配 temptation to do（做某事的诱惑），与上下文 run from society or make an excuse to avoid one's obligations 语义紧密——文章在说『社交距离并不等于趁机逃避社会与责任』。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-3`,quote:`It doesn't include the [31] to run from society or make an excuse to avoid one's obligations—such as life-saving medical work or the parental obligation to buy groceries.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`it offers an __32__ opportunity for those who care about the elderly`,answer:`A`,explanation:`an ___ opportunity=冠词+空+名词。amazing opportunity=绝佳机会。amazing 与 opportunity 是高频褒义搭配『绝佳机会』，且与上下文情感色彩一致：作者把保持距离视为 applying love through caution，由此带来一个让人 find new ways to love them 的难得机会，正是 amazing 的褒义场域。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-3`,quote:`And in doing so, it offers an [32] opportunity for those who care about the elderly to find new ways to love them.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`If we're not __33__ as much in our normal work or school`,answer:`D`,explanation:`we're not ___ as much=系动词后过去分词作表语。be engaged in=投入于…engaged 与 in 构成 be engaged in『忙于、投入于』的固定搭配；句义 we're not engaged as much in our normal work or school 正好解释了下半句『所以才 have extra time to call parents and…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-4`,quote:`If we're not [33] as much in our normal work or school, we have extra time to call parents and grandparents.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`We can also ask elderly relatives how to best support them __34__`,answer:`J`,explanation:`support them ___=动词宾语后状语。在精神层面上支持长辈。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-4`,quote:`We can also ask elderly relatives how to best support them [34] and use our sacrifices as an opportunity to bring us, our community and the world [35] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`use our sacrifices as an opportunity to bring us, our community and the world __35__`,answer:`B`,explanation:`bring sb ___=bring+宾语+补语。bring … closer=让…更紧密。closer 与 bring 是经典搭配 bring sb closer『让某人更亲近』，正与全文 social distancing 表面拉远距离、内核却 at its core is love 形成回环呼应——身体的距离换来心灵的更近。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-bp-4`,quote:`We can also ask elderly relatives how to best support them [34] and use our sacrifices as an opportunity to bring us, our community and the world [35] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Students should develop the key skills before they start a project.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`匹配 H 段：关键技能+项目开始前。定位句“The most critical skills will be developed before the project even starts, in the form of boot camps,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-H`,quote:`Those projects are then interwoven with fast-paced technical modules(模块) learned 'on-the-fly' and 'at will' depending on the nature of the project.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`By acquiring reasoning skills in the first few years of college, students can lay a foundation for lifelong learning.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`匹配 E 段：前几年+推理能力+终身学习地基。定位句“For the first few years, students would 'learn to learn' and get endowed with reasoning skills that remain with them for the rest…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-E`,quote:`The Bachelor's degree could be your passport to lifelong learning.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`The easy access to learning and rapid technological changes have brought the traditional model of education under challenge.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`匹配 B 段：学习易得+技术快变冲击传统模式。定位句“This classical model has traditionally produced prestigious universities,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-B`,quote:`Because universities and curricula are designed along the three unities of French classical tragedy: time, action, and place.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`Unbelievable as it may seem, part-time students constitute a considerable portion of the student body in many universities across the world.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`匹配 K 段：非全日制学生+相当比例。定位句“Sound like science fiction? In many countries, part-time studying is not exceptional: on average across OECD countries,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-K`,quote:`In many countries, part-time studying is not exceptional: on average across OECD countries,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Some social and managerial skills, which are not easily automated, will be of great importance to students' future careers.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：社交管理技能+难自动化+未来职业。定位句“Not only will those skills be actually integrated into the curriculum but they will be very important to have in the future becaus…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-I`,quote:`In addition to technical capabilities, the very nature of projects develops social and entrepreneurial skills, such as design thinking,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`A new model of college education should provide students with the knowledge and skills that will make them more inventive and capable of lifelong learning.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：新模式+让学生更具创造力+终身学习。定位句“It must equip students with the right skills and knowledge to compete in a world 'where value will be derived largely from human i…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-C`,quote:`The university model needs to evolve.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`A mixed student body may change the classroom dynamics and benefit learning.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`匹配 J 段：混合学生群体+改变课堂动态。定位句“Second, freshly graduated students would mix with professionally experienced ones.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-J`,quote:`After the MSc diploma is earned, there would be many more stamps of lifelong learning over the years.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`The question of who will bear the cost of lifelong learning is a topic of constant debate.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`匹配 N 段：谁承担终身学习费用+持续争论。定位句“Even if time were not an issue, who will pay for lifelong learning? That's the eternal debate: should it be the learner's responsi…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-N`,quote:`For example, in Massachusetts, the healthcare professions require continuing education credits, which are carefully evidenced and documented.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`To the traditional subjects of math and physics should be added a new discipline which combines computer science with statistics and other components.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：数学物理之外新增融合计算机与统计的学科。定位句“Therefore, a new discipline blending computer science, programming, statistics and machine learning should be added to the traditi…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-F`,quote:`Recent advances in computational methods and data science push us into rethinking science and engineering.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Students who are burdened with family duties might choose to take online courses.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`匹配 M 段：有家庭负担的学生+在线课程。定位句“This could fix the main organisational challenges for the university, but not for the learners, due to lack of time,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-mp-M`,quote:`This could fix the main organisational challenges for the university, but not for the learners, due to lack of time, family obligations or funds.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply social media may do to our life?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may facilitate our interpersonal relationships.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It may filter our negative impressions of others.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It may make us feel isolated and incompetent.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may render us vulnerable and inauthentic.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Why does social media trigger feelings of loneliness and inadequacy? Because instead of being real life, it is, for the most part,”。预判出的"孤立感+自觉不如人"两点恰好被 feel isolated and incompetent 一对一覆盖,无新增、无遗漏,故锁 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-1`,quote:`Why does social media trigger feelings of loneliness and inadequacy? Because instead of being real life, it is, for the most part, impression management,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`Why do people post comments selectively on social media?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They do not find all their online friends trustworthy.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They want to avoid offending any of their audience.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They do not want to lose their followers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are eager to boost their popularity.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“And, rightfully so, because it wouldn't feel safe to be completely authentic and vulnerable with some of our "friends" whom we don't actually know or…”。预判出的"对没建立信任的网友不敢全坦白"被 They do not find all their online friends trustworthy 同义浓缩,故锁 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-2`,quote:`And, rightfully so, because it wouldn't feel safe to be completely authentic and vulnerable with some of our "friends" whom we don't actually know or with whom…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What are humans inclined to do according to the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Exaggerate their life's accomplishments.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Strive for perfection regardless of the cost.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Paint a rosy picture of other people's lives.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Learn lessons from other people's downfalls.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Also, we tend to idealize others' lives and compare our downfalls to their greatest accomplishments, ending in feelings of loneliness and inadequacy.”。预判的"把别人生活想象得太美"恰被 Paint a rosy picture of other people's lives 一对一覆盖,故锁 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-4`,quote:`Also, we tend to idealize others' lives and compare our downfalls to their greatest accomplishments, ending in feelings of loneliness and inadequacy.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What is the author's view of pictures and posts on social media?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They should record the memorable moments in people's lives.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They should be carefully edited so as to present the best image.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They should be shown in a way that meets one's security needs.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They should keep people from the unhealthy quest for perfection.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P5：“Pictures and posts should be byproducts of life's treasured moments and fun times, not the planned and calculated image that one is putting out into c…”。预判的"自然记录生活美好瞬间"被 record the memorable moments in people's lives 一对一覆盖,故锁 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-5`,quote:`Pictures and posts should be byproducts of life's treasured moments and fun times, not the planned and calculated image that one is putting out into cyberspace…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author advise people to do when they find their online experience unconstructive?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Use social media to increase their ability to connect with various types of people.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stay connected to those whom they may not otherwise get to know and befriend.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Try to prevent negative thoughts and feelings from getting into the online pages.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Strengthen ties with real-life friends instead of caring about their online image.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P6：“However, social media should feel like a fun experience, not one that contributes to negative thoughts and feelings.”。预判的"多见线下挚友、少刷手机"恰被 Strengthen ties with real-life friends instead of caring about their online image 通过 instead of 同步对应,故锁 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p1-6`,quote:`However, social media should feel like a fun experience, not one that contributes to negative thoughts and feelings.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say we humans have been doing to chimps?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ruining their culture.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Accelerating their extinction.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Treating them as alien species.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Homogenizing their living habits.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“That is effectively what humans have been doing to our closest relatives—chimps(大猩猩).”。预判“破坏黑猩猩的文化”后在四项中找含 culture 与“破坏”语义的选项，只有 Ruining their culture 同时满足两点，锁定 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-1`,quote:`That is effectively what humans have been doing to our closest relatives—chimps(大猩猩).`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What is the finding of Andrew Whiten's team?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Chimps demonstrate highly developed skills of communication.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Chimps rely heavily upon their body language to communicate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Chimps behave in ways quite similar to those of human beings.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Different chimp groups differ in their way of communication.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Back in 1999, a team of scientists led by Andrew Whiten showed that chimps from different parts of Africa behave very differently from one another.”。预判“不同地区的黑猩猩行为方式不同”后，四项里同时含“差异”与“交流/沟通”要素的只有 Different chimp groups differ in their way of communication，且有原文两处例证支撑，锁定 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-2`,quote:`Back in 1999, a team of scientists led by Andrew Whiten showed that chimps from different parts of Africa behave very differently from one another.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What did Ammie Kalan and her colleagues find through their intensive fieldwork?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Whiten's classic study has little impact on the diversity of chimp behavior.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Chimp behavior becomes less varied with the increase of human activity.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Chimps alter their culture to quickly adapt to the changed environment.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It might already be too late to prevent animal cultures from extinction.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Ammie Kalan and her colleagues have shown, through years of intensive fieldwork, that the very presence of humans has eroded the diversity of chimp be…”。预判“人类越多→黑猩猩行为越单一”后，四项中只有 Chimp behavior becomes less varied with the increase of human activity 同时覆盖“多样性下降”与“人类影响”两要素，锁定 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-4`,quote:`Ammie Kalan and her colleagues have shown, through years of intensive fieldwork, that the very presence of humans has eroded the diversity of chimp behavior.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does Cat Hobaiter think we should do for chimp conservation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Try to understand our sister species' behavior in the wild.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Make efforts to preserve each individual chimp community.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Study the unique characteristics of each generation of chimps.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Endeavor to restore chimp habitats to expand its total population.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P6：“"Sometimes in the rush to conserve the species, I think we forget about the individuals," says Cat Hobaiter, a professor at the University of St.”。预判“按社群/个体单位保护”后，四项中只有 Make efforts to preserve each individual chimp community 准确体现 Hobaiter“不要遗忘个体/社群”的立场，锁定 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-6`,quote:`"Sometimes in the rush to conserve the species, I think we forget about the individuals," says Cat Hobaiter, a professor at the University of St. Andrews.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author suggest conservationists do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Focus entirely on culturally-based entities rather than genetically-based ones.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Place more stress on animal traditions than on their physical conservation.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Conserve animal species in a novel and all-round way.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Explore the cultures of species before they vanish.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P8：“Obviously, conservationists need to think about saving species in a completely new way—by preserving animal traditions as well as bodies and genes.”。预判“以全新方式且兼顾两面”后，四项中只有 Conserve animal species in a novel and all-round way 同时具备“novel”（新）与“all-round”（兼顾），锁定 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2020-12-s3-p2-8`,quote:`Obviously, conservationists need to think about saving species in a completely new way—by preserving animal traditions as well as bodies and genes.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Located 46 kilometers south of Tian'anmen Square, Beijing Daxing International Airport was put into use on September 30, 2019. Construction of this massive project began in 2014, and at the peak there were more than 40,000 workers on the construction site. The compact design of the terminal allows the maximum number of aircraft to dock directly at the positions closest to its center, which provides great convenience for passengers. The terminal has 82 boarding gates in total, but after passing through security check, passengers can reach any gate in less than 8 minutes. In addition, the design of the airport ensures 300 takeoffs and landings per hour. The airport's annual passenger volume is expected to reach 100 million by 2040, and it is likely to become the busiest airport in the world.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2020-12-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`北京大兴国际机场位于天安门广场以南46公里处，于2019年9月30日投入使用。该巨型工程于2014年开工建设，高峰时工地上有4万多工人。航站楼设计紧凑，可以允许最大数量的飞机直接停靠在最靠近航站楼中心的位置，这给乘客提供了极大的方便。航站楼共有82个登机口，但乘客通过安检后，只需不到8分钟就能抵达任何一个登机口。机场的设计可确保每小时300架次起降。机场年客运量2040年将达到1亿人次，有望成为世界上最繁忙的机场。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①北京大兴国际机场采用约定俗成的官方英文译名 Beijing Daxing International Airport，「大兴」用拼音 Daxing；②天安门广场用通行译名 Tian'anmen Square（首字母大写、an 后加隔音号更规范，写 Tiananmen Square 亦可接受），是地标性专名，全篇只此一处。锚回原文 天安门广场；③航站楼统一译为 the terminal（building）。文中反复出现 航站楼设计紧凑、航站楼共有82个登机口，须全篇用同一个词 terminal，。`,score:15}]}]},"cet6-2021-06-s1":{subtitle:`2021 年 6 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2021年6月英语六级真题第1套，站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the graph below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the graph and comment on China's achievements in urbanization. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第1套 写作的图表内容为：这张折线图标题为 " degree of urbanization in China from 1980 to 2019"，折线从1980年的19.39%开始，逐年增长，到2019年达到60.6%。其中，部分年份的数据点被标记出来，并标注了具体的百分比数值。 1980年: 19.39% 1985年: 23.71% 1990年: 26.41% 1995年: 29.04% 2000年: 36.22% 2005年: 42.99% 2010年: 49.95% 2011年: 51.27% 2012年: 52.57% 2013年: 53.73% 2014年: 54.77% 2015年: 56.1% 2016年: 57.35% 2017年: 58.52% 2018年: 59.58% 2019年: 60.6%）`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`As is vividly shown in the line graph above, the degree of urbanization in China rose steadily and remarkably over nearly four decades. Specifically, the proportion of urban population climbed from a mere 19.39% in 1980 to a striking 60.6% in 2019, tripling within the period, with the growth becoming especially rapid after the year 2000.

Behind this rapid urbanization lie China's tremendous achievements. To begin with, decades of sustained economic growth created vast job opportunities in cities, drawing hundreds of millions of rural residents into urban areas. Moreover, forward-looking government policies and large-scale infrastructure construction accelerated the transfer of population and industries from the countryside to towns. Such progress not only reflects the modernization of the nation but also greatly improves people's living standards and promotes balanced regional development.

In conclusion, the impressive climb of the urbanization curve mirrors the remarkable strides China has made. If this momentum is sustained wisely, the country will surely embrace an even brighter and more prosperous future.`,guidance:[`总述加起止数据。本段用 As is vividly shown … 一句总述趋势，再用 climbed from a mere 19.39% in 1980 to a striking 60.6% in 2019 只取起止两点，兑现了描述段'总述趋势再取点佐证'的方法，没有逐年罗列数字。`,`过渡加原因加意义。本段以 Behind this rapid urbanization lie … 完成从描述到评论的过渡，用 To begin with / Moreover 铺开两条原因，再用 not only … but also … 抬出意义，落实了评论段的展开步骤，篇幅上是全文重头。`,`重申加展望。本段用 In conclusion 引出，以 the urbanization curve mirrors the remarkable strides 重申中心，再用 If this momentum is sustained … 展望收尾，呼应立意且不引入新论点。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the graph below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the graph and comment on China's achievements in urbanization. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第1套 写作的图表内容为：这张折线图标题为 " degree of urbanization in China from 1980 to 2019"，折线从1980年的19.39%开始，逐年增长，到2019年达到60.6%。其中，部分年份的数据点被标记出来，并标注了具体的百分比数值。 1980年: 19.39% 1985年: 23.71% 1990年: 26.41% 1995年: 29.04% 2000年: 36.22% 2005年: 42.99% 2010年: 49.95% 2011年: 51.27% 2012年: 52.57% 2013年: 53.73% 2014年: 54.77% 2015年: 56.1% 2016年: 57.35% 2017年: 58.52% 2018年: 59.58% 2019年: 60.6%）`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：总述加起止数据。本段用 As is vividly shown … 一句总述趋势，再用 climbed from a mere 19.39% in 1980 to a striking 60.6% in 2019 只取起止两点，兑现了描述段'总述趋势再取点佐证'的方法，没有逐年罗列数字。过渡加原因加意义。本段以 Behind this rapid urbanization lie … 完成从描述到评论的过渡，用 To begin with / Moreover 铺开两条原因，再用 not only … but also … 抬出意义，落实了评论段的展开步骤，篇幅上是全文重头。重申加展望。本段用 In conclusion 引出，以 the urbanization curve mirrors the remarkable strides 重申中心，再用 If this momentum is sustained … 展望收尾，呼应立意且不引入新论点。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 M: It's my last day at work tomorrow. I'll start my new job in two weeks. My human resources manager wants to conduct an interview with me before I leave. 2 W: Ah, an exit interview. Are you looking forward to it? 3 M: I'm not sure how I feel about it. I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time, but I'm not sure if I should tell them how I really feel. 4 W: To my way of thinking, there are two main potential benefits that come from unleashing an agitated stream of truth during an exit interview. The first is release. Unburdening yourself of frustration, and perhaps even anger to someone who isn't a friend or close colleague can be wonderfully freeing. 5 M: And let me guess. The second is that the criticism will, theoretically, help the organization I'm leaving to improve, making sure employees of the future are less likely to encounter what I did? 6 W: That's right. But the problem with the company improvement part is that very often it doesn't happen. An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't. In my company, the information gained from these interviews is often not confidential. And the information is used as dirt against another manager, or can be traded among senior managers. 7 M: Now you've got me rethinking what I'll disclose in the interview. There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry. It is a pretty small industry after all. 8 W: Anything you initially gain from the instant satisfaction of telling it like it is, you might lose down the track by injuring your future career prospects. 9 M: Right. Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer websites. 10 W: You could. And don't do the interview at all. Exit interviews are not mandatory. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 M: Today, I'm talking to the renowned botanist, Jane Forster. 2 W: Thank you for inviting me to join you on the show, Henry. 3 M: Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity since the release of your latest documentary. Can you tell us a little about it? 4 W: Well, it follows my expedition to study the vegetation indigenous to the rainforest in equatorial areas of Southeast Asia. 5 M: You certainly get to travel to some very exotic locations. 6 W: It was far from glamorous, to be honest. The area we visited was accessible only by canoe and the living conditions in the hut were primitive, to say the least. There was no electricity. And our water supply was a nearby stream. 7 M: How were the weather conditions while you were there? 8 W: The weather was not conducive to our work at all, since the humidity was almost unbearable. At midday, we stayed in the hut and did nothing. It was too humid to either work or sleep. 9 M: How long did your team spend in the jungle? 10 W: Originally, we planned to be there for a month. But in the end, we stayed for only two weeks. 11 M: Why did you cut the expedition short? 12 W: Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching. And we had to evacuate at very short notice. 13 M: That sounds like a fascinating anecdote. 14 W: It was frightening. The fastest evacuation route was through river rapids. We had to navigate them carrying all of our equipment. 15 M: So overall, was the journey unsuccessful? 16 W: Absolutely not. We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life. 17 M: Why do you put up with such adverse conditions? 18 W: Botany is an obsession for me. Many of the destinations I visit have stunning scenery. And I get to meet a variety of people from all over the world. 19 M: So where will your next destination be? 20 W: I haven't decided yet. 21 M: Then we can leave it for another occasion. Thanks. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 Scientists often use specialized jargon terms while communicating with laymen. Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice. In a new study, people exposed to jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous vehicles and surgical robots later said they were less interested in science than others who read about the same topics, but without the use of specialized terms. They also felt less informed about science and less qualified to discuss science topics. It's noteworthy that it made no difference if the jargon terms were defined in the text. Even when the terms were defined, readers still felt the same lack of engagement as readers who read jargon that wasn't explained. 2 The problem is that the mere presence of jargon sends a discouraging message to readers. Hillary Schulman, the author of the study, asserts that specialized words are a signal. Jargon tells people that the message isn't for them. 3 There's an even darker side to how people react to jargon. In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led people to doubt the actual science. They found the opposite when a text is easier to read. Then people are more persuaded. 4 Thus, it's important to communicate clearly when talking about complex science subjects. This is especially true with issues related to public health, like the safety of new medications and the benefits of vaccines. Schulman concedes that the use of jargon is appropriate with scientific audiences. But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their language. They need to eliminate jargon. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 At the beginning of the twentieth century, on the Gulf Coast in the US state of Texas, there was a hill where gas leakage was so noticeable that schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire. 2 Pattillo Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill. Oil wells weren't drilled back then; they were essentially dug. The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole. Higgins had forecast oil at 1,000 feet, a totally made-up figure. 3 Higgins subsequently hired a mining engineer, Captain Anthony Lucas. After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry. 4 In January 1901, at 1,020 feet-almost precisely the depth predicted by Higgins' wild guess—the well roared and suddenly ejected mud and six tons of drilling pipe out of the ground, terrifying those present. For the next nine days, until the well was capped, the well poured out more oil than all the wells in America combined. 5 In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry; cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy. Higgins' well changed that. The boom made some prospectors millionaires, but the sudden surplus of petroleum was not entirely a blessing for Texas. In the 1930s, prices crashed, to the point that in some parts of the country oil was cheaper than water. That would become a familiar pattern of the boom-or-bust Texas' economy. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Most people dislike their jobs. It's an astonishing but statistical fact. A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe they have terrible managers. Few describe their managers as malicious or manipulative though. While those types certainly exist, they are a minority. The majority of managers, seemingly, just don't know any better. They are often emulating bad managers they've had in the past. It's likely they've never read a management book or attended a management course. They might not have even reflected on what good management looks like and how it would influence their own management style. 2 The researchers interviewed employees about their managers, beginning with a question about the worst manager they had ever had. From this, the researchers came up with four main causes of why some managers are perceived as being simply awful at their jobs. 3 The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor management practices. It was specifically stressful work environments, minimal training and a lack of accountability that were found to be the most blameworthy. Often a manager's superiors can effectively encourage a manager's distasteful behavior when they fail to discipline the person's wrongdoings. Such workplaces are sometimes described as toxic. 4 The second cause was attributed to the managers' characteristics. Those deemed to be most destructive were odd people—those without drive, those who allow personal problems into the workplace, and those with an unpleasant temperament or personality in general. 5 The third cause of poor management was associated with a deficiency of qualifications not so much the formal variety one obtains from a university, but the informal variety that comes from credible work experience and professional accomplishments. 6 The fourth cause concerned managers who've been promoted for reasons other than potential. One reason in particular why these people had been promoted was that they had been around the longest. It wasn't their skill set or other merits that got them the job; it was their tenure. 7 A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees. The researchers didn't ask senior leaders what they thought of their frontline managers. It's quite possible they're content with how the individuals they promoted are now performing, merrily ignorant of the damage they're actually causing, which might explain why, as the researchers conclude, those same middle managers are usually unaware that they are a bad manager. Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead. 2 Iron ore is a key ingredient in steel-making. The mining companies here produce almost 300 million tons of iron ore a year. The 240 giant autonomous trucks in use, in the Western Australian mines, can weigh 400 tons, fully loaded, and travel at speeds of up to 60 kilometers per hour. They are a technological leap, transporting iron ore along routes which run for hundreds of kilometers from mines to their destinations. Here when the truck arrives at its destination, staff in the operation center direct it precisely where to unload. Vast quantities of iron ore are then transported by autonomous trains to ocean ports. 3 Advocates argue these automated vehicles will change mining forever. It may only be five years before the use of automation technology leads to a fully robotic mine. 4 A range of factors has pushed Western Australia's desert region to the lead of this automation revolution. These include the huge size of the mines, the scale of equipment and the repetitive nature of some of the work. Then there's the area's remoteness. At 502,000 square kilometers, it can sometimes make recruiting staff a challenge. Another consideration is the risks when humans interact with large machinery. There are also the financial imperatives. The ongoing push by the mining corporations to be more productive and more efficient is another powerful driver in embracing automation technology. 5 The concept of a fully autonomous mine is a bit of a misleading term, however. This is because the more technology is put into the field, the more people are needed to deploy, maintain and improve it. 6 The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs. These include data scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence. The mining companies claim automation and robotics present opportunities to make mining more sustainable and safer. Employees will be offered a career that is even more fulfilling and more rewarding. 7 Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology. But they still have reservations about the rise of automation technology. Their main concern is the potential impact on remote communities. As automation spreads further, the question is how these remote communities will survive when the old jobs are eliminated. And this may well prove to be the most significant impact of robotic technology in many places around the world. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1 According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average. Thai people know that their roads are dangerous, but they don't know this could easily be changed. 2 Globally, road accidents kill more people every year than any infectious disease. Researchers at the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation in America put the death toll in 2017 at 1.24 million. According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the turn of the century. But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries. 3 In many poor countries, road accidents are killing more people than ever before. Those countries have swelling, young populations, a fast-growing fleet of cars and motorbikes, and a limited supply of surgeons. It is impossible to know for sure because official statistics are so inadequate, but deaths are thought to have risen by 40% since 1990 in many low-income countries. In many rich countries, by contrast, roads are becoming even safer. In Estonia and Ireland, for example, the number of deaths has fallen by about two-thirds since the late 1990s. 4 But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle-income countries, which contain most of the world's people and have some of the most dangerous roads. According to researchers, in China and South Africa, traffic deaths have been falling since 2000, and in India since 2012, and the Philippines reached its peak four years ago. The question is whether Thailand can soon follow suit. 5 Rob MeInerney, head of the International Road Assessment Programme, says that all countries tend to go through three phases. They begin with poor, slow roads. In the second phase, as they grow wealthier, they pave the roads, allowing traffic to move faster and pushing up the death rate. Lastly, in the third phase, countries act to make their roads safer. The trick, then, is to reach the third stage sooner, by focusing earlier and more closely on fatal accidents. 6 How to do that? The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives. Safe driving habits are practices which people know they should follow but often don't. Dangerous driving is not a fixed cultural trait, as some imagine. People respond to incentives, such as traffic laws that are actually enforced. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the man from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He will tell the management how he really feels.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He will meet his new manager in two weeks.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He is going to attend a job interview.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He is going to leave his present job.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time.”。被问者自述 I resigned 的直接陈述。男士开口第一段就说 It's my last day at work tomorrow. I'll start my new job in two weeks.`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman think about the information gained from an exit interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It should be kept private.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It should be carefully analyzed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can be quite useful to senior managers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can improve interviewees' job prospects.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't. In my company, the information gained from these interviews is often not confidential.”。supposed to be 表『本应当』的主张。女士说 An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't，be supposed to 表示『理应、本该』，正是她对这信息该如何的主张：本该保密。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the man want to rethink what he will say in the coming exit interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may leave a negative impression on the interviewer.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It may adversely affect his future career prospects.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It may displease his immediate superiors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may do harm to his fellow employees.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P7：“There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry.”。it could affect 表担忧的因果句。男士说 Now you've got me rethinking what I'll disclose，紧接 There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation a…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man think he had better do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Prepare a comprehensive exit report.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Do some practice for the exit interview.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Network with his close friends to find a better employer.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pour out his frustrations on a rate-your-employer website. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P9：“Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer websites.”。Perhaps it would be better 表打算的建议句。对话临近结尾，男士说 Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-empl…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man want Jane Forster to talk about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Her unsuccessful journey.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Her month-long expedition.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Her latest documentary.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Her career as a botanist.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity since the release of your latest documentary. Can you tell us a little about it?”。主持人的直接请求句。信号是主持人发出的请求 Can you tell us a little about it?，其中 it 回指上一句的 your latest documentary——一问一请求，正是他想让她谈的对象，答案就落在这句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the woman describe her experience as "far from glamorous"?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She had to live like a vegetarian.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She was caught in a hurricane.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She had to endure many hardships.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She suffered from water shortage.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P6：“It was far from glamorous, to be honest. The area we visited was accessible only by canoe and the living conditions in the hut were primitive,”。原话之后的连串列举。信号是她说完 far from glamorous 后一口气列举多重恶劣条件：accessible only by canoe、living conditions in the hut were primitive、no electricity、water s…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`Why did the woman and those who went with her end their trip halfway?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A hurricane was coming.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A flood was approaching.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They had no more food in the canoe.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They could no longer bear the humidity.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P12：“Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching. And we had to evacuate at very short notice.”。received news that + had to 因果链。信号是 we received news that a hurricane was approaching 接着 we had to evacuate——"得知飓风逼近"是因、"被迫撤离"是果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman think of the journey?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It was memorable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was unbearable.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was uneventful.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was fruitful. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P16：“Absolutely not. We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life.”。强烈否定 + 反转后的正向定调。信号是主持人问 was the journey unsuccessful?（负面设问），她以 Absolutely not. 强烈否定反转。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the use of jargon terms by experts?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It diminishes laymen's interest in science.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It ensures the accuracy of their arguments.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It makes their expressions more explicit.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It hurts laymen's dignity and self-esteem.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice. In a new study, people exposed to jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous…”。定性词harmful＋对比。答案句的信号是定性词 the harmful effects（有害影响）和紧随其后的对比结果 less interested in science：录音先给出"有害"的定性，再用接触术语者 vs 未接触者的对照坐实是哪种害处，这两处合起来就是答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What do researchers find about people reading scientific articles containing jargon terms?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They can learn to communicate with scientists.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They tend to disbelieve the actual science.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They feel great respect towards scientists.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They will see the complexity of science.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led people to doubt the actual science.”。研究发现动词＋后续转折。信号是 researchers found that（研究者发现）这个直接报告发现的动词，题干 find 与它对应；其后还有 They found the opposite when a text is easier to read. 的对比。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What does Schulman suggest scientists do when communicating with the general public?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Find appropriate topics.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stimulate their interest.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Explain all the jargon terms.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Do away with jargon terms. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their language. They need to eliminate jargon.”。转折But＋建议need to。双重信号：先是转折 But（前句说术语对专业听众尚可，But 后才是对大众的真正主张），再是建议动词 need to eliminate jargon。转折后＋need to 之处，正是对策题答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What did Texas businessman Pattillo Higgins believe?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The local gassy hill might start a huge fire.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) There was oil leakage along the Gulf Coast.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The erupting gas might endanger local children.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) There were oil deposits below a local gassy hill.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Pattillo Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill.”。信号词 became convinced 直接交代所信。录音里 became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill 中的 became convinced（确信）正是表达人物信念的标志词，题干问 believe。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What prevented Higgins' workers from digging a proper hole to get the oil?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The massive gas underground.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their lack of the needed skill.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The sand under the hill.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Their lack of suitable tools.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole.”。信号词 defeated 标出受阻原因。The sand under the hill defeated several attempts 里 defeated（挫败）正对题干的 prevented，主语 The sand under the hill 就是那个让挖洞失败的障碍。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about Captain Lucas' drilling method?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It rendered many oil workers jobless.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was not as effective as he claimed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It gave birth to the oil drilling industry.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was not popularized until years later.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry.”。信号词 created 点出方法的成果。his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry 里 created（开创）直接交代这套方法的历史成果，问 say about method 时。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Texas oil industry boom?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It radically transformed the state's economy.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It resulted in an oil surplus all over the world.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It totally destroyed the state's rural landscape.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It ruined the state's cotton and beef industries. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P5：“In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry; cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy.”。转折信号 changed that 是结论落点。录音先铺垫德州 almost entirely rural 的旧貌，紧接一句 Higgins' well changed that，changed that（改变了这一切）是从旧貌转向新局的转折落点，答案就在这一转之后。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What is a primary cause of employee dissatisfaction according to recent research?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Unsuitable jobs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Bad managers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Insufficient motivation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Tough regulations.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe they have terrible managers.”。题干原话几乎照搬的点题句。录音开篇第二句就用 A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the causes for poor management practices?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ineffective training.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Toxic company culture.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Lack of regular evaluation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Overburdening of managers.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor management practices.”。分点信号 The first cause + 收尾点出 toxic。录音用 The first cause was company culture 起头明确这是分点罗列的第一条成因。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the study on job dissatisfaction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It collected feedback from both employers and employees.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was conducted from frontline managers' point of view.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It provided meaningful clues to solving the problem.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was based only on the perspective of employees. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P7：“A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees.”。结尾提示语 A point worth making + only。录音末段用 A point worth making is that…（有一点值得说明）这种「特意提醒」的强调语引出关键句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the mining industry in Western Australia?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is seeing an automation revolution.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is bringing prosperity to the region.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is yielding an unprecedented profit.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is expanding at an accelerating speed.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely tak…”。开头点题的总括句。篇章独白的总判断常落在开头：录音第一句 mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead 直接给出西澳…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What is the impact of the digitization of the mining industry?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It exhausts resources sooner.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It creates a lot of new jobs.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It causes conflicts between employers and employees.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It calls for the retraining of unskilled mining workers.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P6：“The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs.”。因果动词 creating 引出影响。录音第 6 段 The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs 用 creating a need for 直接点出…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What is the attitude of workers' unions towards the introduction of new technology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They welcome it with open arms.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They will wait to see its effect.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are strongly opposed to it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They accept it with reservations. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P7：“Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology.”。转折 But 后才是真态度。录音先说工会 accepted（接受），紧接 But they still have reservations 用 But 转出真态度——仍有保留。态度题的落点在转折之后，前半的接受只是铺垫。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about traffic accidents in Thailand?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Their cost to the nation's economy is incalculable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They kill more people than any infectious disease.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their annual death rate is about twice that of the global average.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have experienced a gradual decline since the year of 2017.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average.”。地名加数字即落点。第一段开门见山点出 Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average，地名 Thailand 加上倍数 double 同时出现，正是预读时盯的两个锚点，这一句就是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from an American institute's statistics regarding road deaths?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They show a difference between rich and poor nations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They don't reflect the changes in individual countries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They rise and fall from year to year.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are not as reliable as claimed.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the turn of the century.”。转折 But 后才是真意。前半句说总数基本平稳，紧接 But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries，转折词 But 之后才是说话人真正强调的那层意思——这组数据掩盖了个体国家的变化，正是答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What is said about middle-income countries?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Many of them have increasing numbers of cars on the road.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many of them are following the example set by Thailand.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Many of them have seen a decline in road-death rates.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many of them are investing heavily in infrastructure.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle-income countries… According to researchers, in China and South Africa,”。群体名加并列实例。第四段点出 middle-income countries 后，立即用 China、South Africa、India、Philippines 一连串实例说明 traffic deaths have been falling，群体名加并列例证处即答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What else could be done to reduce fatal road accidents in addition to safer roads?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Foster better driving behavior.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Provide better training for drivers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Abolish all outdated traffic rules.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Impose heavier penalties on speeding.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives.”。提对策句 solution lies in。末段直接给方案 The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`alienating`,B:`appreciation`,C:`bias`,D:`fraud`,E:`housed`,F:`investigated`,G:`overwhelming`,H:`portraying`,I:`representation`,J:`safeguarded`,K:`secondary`,L:`superiority`,M:`temperament`,N:`tentative`,O:`threshold`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,text:`A new study has drawn a bleak picture of cultural inclusiveness reflected in the children's literature available in Australia. Dr. Helen Adam from Edith Cowan University's School of Education [26] the cultural diversity of children's books. She examined the books [27] in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers in Western Australia. Just 18 percent of 2,413 books in the total collection contained any [28] of non-white people. Minority cultures were often featured in stereotypical or tokenistic ways, for example, by [29] Asian culture with chopsticks and traditional dress. Characters that did represent a minority culture usually had [30] roles in the books. The main characters were mostly Caucasian. This causes concern as it can lead to an impression that whiteness is of greater value.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-2`,text:`Dr. Adam said children formed impressions about "difference" and identity from a very young age. Evidence has shown they develop own-race [31] from as young as three months of age. The books we share with young children can be a valuable opportunity to develop children's understanding of themselves and others. Books can also allow children to see diversity. They discover both similarities and differences between themselves and others. This can help develop understanding, acceptance and [32] of diversity.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-3`,text:`Census data has shown Australians come from more than 200 countries. They speak over 300 languages at home. Additionally, Australians belong to more than 100 different religious groups. They also work in more than 1,000 different occupations. "Australia is a multicultural society. The current [33] promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks [34] children from minority groups. This can give white middle-class children a sense of [35] or privilege," Dr. Adam said.`}]},i,{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-1`,text:`Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-2`,text:`This is down to "hereditarian"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed "differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about "genetics determining academic success". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-3`,text:`Yet the paper does say children are "unintentionally genetically selected" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-4`,text:`While there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-5`,text:`Genetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, "the ultimate collective control of human destinies," as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.`}]},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-1`,text:`Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-2`,text:`Tourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-3`,text:`But such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of "over-tourism" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-4`,text:`That the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-5`,text:`On the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-6`,text:`There is no "one size fits all" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`Dr. Helen Adam from Edith Cowan University's School of Education __26__ the cultural diversity of children's books.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`主语后缺谓语动词。「研究 / 调查」最贴学者动作。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,quote:`Helen Adam from Edith Cowan University's School of Education [26] the cultural diversity of children's books.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`She examined the books __27__ in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers in Western Australia.`,answer:`E`,explanation:`名词后非谓语，作后置定语。books housed in… = 存放于…的书。house 作动词意为「容纳 / 存放」，过去分词 housed 修饰 books，意为「存放在日托中心教室里的那些书」，正好对应研究者「考察了四家日托中心里现存的童书」的研究设计。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,quote:`She examined the books [27] in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers in Western Australia.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`Just 18 percent of 2,413 books in the total collection contained any __28__ of non-white people.`,answer:`I`,explanation:`any + 形容词 + ___ + of。「对…的表现 / 描绘」是固定搭配。representation of sb. 意为「对某人的描绘 / 形象呈现」，是讨论文学 / 媒体中群体形象的高频术语；与首句 cultural inclusiveness reflected in the children's literature 中的「文化包容性的体现」同根呼应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,quote:`Just 18 percent of 2,413 books in the total collection contained any [28] of non-white people.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`Minority cultures were often featured in stereotypical or tokenistic ways, for example, by __29__ Asian culture with chopsticks and traditional dress.`,answer:`H`,explanation:`介词 by 后必接动名词。「用筷子和传统服饰刻画亚洲文化」。portray sth. with sth. = 用某物来描绘 / 刻画某物，是固定搭配。整句要表达「刻板地呈现亚洲文化的方式之一就是只用筷子和传统服饰来刻画」，与前句 stereotypical or tokenistic ways 的「刻板化呈现」一脉相承。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,quote:`Minority cultures were often featured in stereotypical or tokenistic ways, for example, by [29] Asian culture with chopsticks and traditional dress.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`Characters that did represent a minority culture usually had __30__ roles in the books.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`名词前作定语。与下句「主角多为白人」形成主次对照。紧接下文 The main characters were mostly Caucasian.，主角 main characters 是白人，那少数族裔角色自然就是「次要 / 配角」。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-1`,quote:`Characters that did represent a minority culture usually had [30] roles in the books.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`Evidence has shown they develop own-race __31__ from as young as three months of age.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`own-race + ___ + from…of age。own-race bias 是心理学固定术语。own-race bias（同族偏好 / 本族偏见）是发展心理学高频术语，指婴幼儿对同族面孔的偏好。本句说「孩子三个月大就开始发展 own-race ___」，与该术语完全吻合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-2`,quote:`Evidence has shown they develop own-race [31] from as young as three months of age.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`This can help develop understanding, acceptance and __32__ of diversity.`,answer:`B`,explanation:`三个 of diversity 并列名词的第三个。「理解—接受—欣赏」三步递进。appreciation of diversity（对多样性的欣赏）是讨论多元文化教育的固定表达，与前两项 understanding, acceptance 构成「理解→接受→欣赏」的程度递进，是英语写作的经典三段式。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-2`,quote:`This can help develop understanding, acceptance and [32] of diversity.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`The current __33__ promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks __33__ children from minority groups.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`the + ___ + 名词 promotion。「压倒性的推崇」呼应主旨张力。全文要说的正是「白人中产观念在童书中占据压倒性比例」（开头 18% 的数据即为佐证）。overwhelming promotion = 压倒性的推崇 / 推广，与下句 risks alienating children from minority groups（有疏远少数族裔孩子的风险）形成「压倒性…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-3`,quote:`The current [33] promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks [34] children from minority groups.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`The current __34__ promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks __34__ children from minority groups.`,answer:`A`,explanation:`risk + V-ing。「使少数族裔孩子被疏远」。alienate sb. 意为「使某人疏远 / 被排斥在外」。整句意为「这种压倒性推崇白人中产观念的做法，有让少数族裔孩子被边缘化的风险」，与上一段 Books can also allow children to see diversity. 的反面后果对应，逻辑闭合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-3`,quote:`The current [33] promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks [34] children from minority groups.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`This can give white middle-class children a sense of __35__ or privilege.`,answer:`L`,explanation:`a sense of + ___ + or privilege。「优越感」与「特权」天然并列。superiority（优越感）和 privilege（特权）是讨论种族 / 阶级议题的经典并列词组；与开篇 This causes concern as it can lead to an impression that whiteness is of greater value.`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-bp-3`,quote:`This can give white middle-class children a sense of [35] or privilege," Dr.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Marconi was central to our present-day understanding of communication.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：对现代通信的中心理解。定位句“Marconi was quite simply the central figure in the emergence of a modern understanding of communication.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-F`,quote:`Marconi invented the idea of global communication—or, more straightforwardly, globally networked, mobile, wireless communication.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`As an adult, Marconi had an intuition that he had to be loyal to politicians in order to be influential.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：成年时直觉效忠政治家。定位句“by the time he reached adulthood, he understood, intuitively, that in order to have an impact he had to both develop an independen…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-I`,quote:`Marconi placed a permanent stamp on the way we live.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Marconi disapproved of the use of wireless communication for commercial broadcasting.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`匹配 D 段：反对将无线用于商业广播。定位句“There are some who say Marconi lost his edge when commercial broadcasting came along; he didn't see that radio could or should be…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-D`,quote:`In 1901 he succeeded in signaling across the Atlantic, from the west coast of England to Newfoundland in the USA,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`Marconi's example demonstrates that theoretical concepts and experiments complement each other in making progress in science and technology.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`匹配 J 段：理论与实验互补。定位句“Marconi proved that theory and experimentation were complementary features of progress.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-J`,quote:`At the same time, Marconi was uncompromisingly independent intellectually.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Marconi's real interest lay in the development of worldwide wireless communication.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：真正兴趣在全球无线通信。定位句“Marconi was really interested in only one thing: the extension of mobile, personal,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-C`,quote:`Between 1896, when he applied for his first patent in England at the age of 22, and his death in Italy in 1937,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Marconi spent his whole life making wireless communication simple to use.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`匹配 E 段：毕生让无线通信易用。定位句“Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-E`,quote:`Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Because of his long-time connection with people in power, Marconi was unable to cut himself off from the fascist regime in Italy.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`匹配 K 段：无法与意大利法西斯政权切割。定位句“However, after a lifetime of moving within the circles of power, he was unable to break with authority,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-K`,quote:`The most controversial aspect of Marconi's life—and the reason why there has been no satisfying biography of Marconi until now—was his uncritical embr…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`In his later years, Marconi exerted a tremendous influence on all aspects of people's life.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`匹配 G 段：晚年对生活各方面巨大影响。定位句“By then, anything Marconi said or did was newsworthy. Stock prices rose or sank according to his pronouncements.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-G`,quote:`In his lifetime, Marconi foresaw the development of television and the fax machine, GPS, radar, and the portable hand-held telephone.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`What connected the 19th century and our present time was the development of wireless communication.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`匹配 B 段：无线通信连接 19 世纪与现在。定位句“What made the link from then to now was the development of wireless communication.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-B`,quote:`Today's globally networked media and communication system has its origins in the 19th century, when, for the first time,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Despite his autonomy, Marconi felt alienated and suffered from a lack of acceptance.`,options:e(i.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`匹配 H 段：自主却疏离、苦于不被接纳。定位句“At one level, Marconi could be fiercely autonomous and independent of the constraints of his own social class.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-mp-H`,quote:`Marconi's biography is also a story about choices and the motivations behind them.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What did a recent research paper claim?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The type of school students attend makes a difference to their future.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Genetic differences between students are far greater than supposed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The advantages of selective schools are too obvious to ignore.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Students' academic performance is determined by their genes.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“This is down to "hereditarian"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed "differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-se…”。预判说「学业表现归因于基因」，Students' academic performance is determined by their genes. 正是这句话的同义换述，且没有添加额外信息，故锁定 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-2`,quote:`This is down to "hereditarian"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed "differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective sc…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author think of the recent research?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Its result was questionable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Its implication was positive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Its influence was rather negligible.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Its conclusions were enlightening.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about "genetics determining academic success".”。预判把作者态度判为质疑/否定，Its result was questionable. 正是这种态度的中性化表达，故锁定 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-2`,quote:`With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about "genetics determining academic success".`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about the relationship between DNA and intelligence?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is one of scientific certainty.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is not one of cause and effect.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is subject to interpretation of statistics.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is not fully examined by gene scientists.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence.”。预判强调「不是因果」，It is not one of cause and effect. 精确对应定位句，故选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-3`,quote:`At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What do hereditarians need to do to make their claims convincing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Take all relevant factors into account in interpreting their data.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Conduct their research using more sophisticated technology.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Gather gene data from people of all social classes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Cooperate with social scientists in their research.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.”。预判要求「全面平衡解读」，Take all relevant factors into account in interpreting their data. 正是这一要求的具体化，故锁定 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p1-4`,quote:`If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author warn against in the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Exaggerating the power of technology in shaping the world.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Losing sight of professional ethics in conducting research.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Misunderstanding the findings of human cognition research.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Promoting discrimination in the name of science.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`预判强调「警惕用科学包装歧视」，Promoting discrimination in the name of science. 正是同义改写，且与全文主线吻合，故选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from Nicola Sturgeon's speech?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The UK is set to adjust its policy on taxation.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Tourists will have to pay a tax to visit Scotland.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The UK will take new measures to boost tourism.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Edinburgh contributes most to Scotland's tourism.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.”。预判说苏格兰将率先对游客征费，Tourists will have to pay a tax to visit Scotland 正是这件事换一个视角的表达，故锁定 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-1`,quote:`Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`How come the UK has been slow in imposing the tourist tax?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Its government wants to attract more tourists.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The tax is unlikely to add much to its revenue.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Its ruling party is opposed to taxes and regulation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It takes time for local governments to reach consensus.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“That the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government,”。题干问「原因」，原文给了两条，C 准确复述其中一条（执政党反对税收与监管），与预判直接吻合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-4`,quote:`That the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`Both international and domestic visitors in the UK should pay tourist tax so as to ______.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) elevate its tourism to international standards`},{key:`B`,text:`B) improve the welfare of its maintenance workers`},{key:`C`,text:`C) promote its cultural exchange with other nations`},{key:`D`,text:`D) ease its financial burden of providing local services`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall,”。预判说「为缓解关键服务承压」，D 把「承压」还原为「财政负担」、把「缓解」还原为 ease，是定位句目的成分的标准抽象。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-4`,quote:`There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about Western tourists?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They don't seem to care about the social cost of tourism.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They don't seem to mind paying for additional services.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They deem travel an important part of their life.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They subject the effects of tourism to scrutiny.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P5：“Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences.”。don't seem to care about the social cost of tourism 把 with little regard for the consequences 的含蓄否定显化，且 consequences=social cost of tourism 由下一句锁死，与预判一致。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s1-p2-5`,quote:`Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What are UK people's opinions about the levy of tourist tax?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Supportive.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Skeptical.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Divided.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Unclear.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`支持方表态与反对方表态在 P6 同段共存，且作者点明 no one-size-fits-all，与预判「意见分歧」完全一致，故锁 C。`,score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Hainan is China's second-largest island after Taiwan, as well as the southernmost province of the country. Hainan Island boasts beautiful scenery, a pleasant climate, abundant sunshine, rich biodiversity, numerous hot springs and crystal-clear seawater, and most of its beaches are ideal places for swimming and sunbathing almost all year round. As a result, it is praised as China's garden of all seasons and a holiday resort, attracting large numbers of tourists from home and abroad every year. Since it was established as a province in 1988, Hainan's tourism, service industry and high-tech industry have developed rapidly, making it China's only provincial-level special economic zone. With the strong support of the central government and people across the country, Hainan will be built into the largest free trade pilot zone in China.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s1-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`海南是仅次于台湾的中国第二大岛，是位于中国最南端的省份。海南岛风景秀丽，气候宜人，阳光充足,生物多样，温泉密布，海水清澈，大部分海滩几乎全年都是游泳和日光浴的理想场所，因而被誉为中国的四季花园和度假胜地，每年都吸引了大批中外游客。 海南1988年建省以来，旅游业、服务业、高新技术产业飞速发展,是中国唯一的省级经济特区。在中央政府和全国人民的大力支持下，海南将建成中国最大的自由贸易试验区。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①海南统一译作 Hainan（地名音译，约定俗成）；指海南岛时用 Hainan Island，指作为省份时用 Hainan 或 Hainan Province。全篇专名只用这一种拼写，对应 海南是仅次于台湾的中国第二大岛；②台湾统一译作 Taiwan（约定俗成译名），出现在 仅次于台湾的中国第二大岛，作比较对象，无需加注；③经济特区统一译作 special economic zone（约定俗成的固定译法，常缩写 SEZ），对应 是中国唯一的省级经济特区；这里是「省级」经济特区，；④自由贸易试验区统一译作 free trade pilot zone（或 pilot free trade zone，固定译法），对应 海南将建成中国最大的自由贸易试验区，「试验」用 pilot 体现。`,score:15}]}]},"cet6-2021-06-s2":{subtitle:`2021 年 6 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2021年6月英语六级真题第2套，站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the chart below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the chart and comment on China's achievements in higher education . You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第2套 写作的图表内容为：图表标题为“Gross enrolment ratio in higher education in China (1990 - 2019)”，图表横轴代表年份，从1990年到2019年，纵轴代表高等教育毛入学率，范围从0.0%到60.0%。数据来源于教育部，从1990年到2019年每年的数据： 1990年: 3.4% 1995年: 6.86% 2000年: 11% 2001年: 13.3% 2002年: 15% 2003年: 17% 2004年: 19% 2005年: 21% 2006年: 22% 2007年: 23% 2008年: 23.3% 2009年: 24.2% 2010年: 26% 2011年: 26.5% 2012年: 30% 2013年: 34.5% 2014年: 37.5% 2015年: 40.0% 2016年: 42.7% 2017年: 45.7% 2018年: 48.1% 2019年: 51.6%）`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`The line chart illustrates the changes in the gross enrolment ratio in higher education in China from 1990 to 2019. As is clearly shown, the figure witnessed a steady and remarkable upward trend over these three decades. In 1990, only 3.4% of the eligible population entered higher education. This proportion climbed steadily, passing 11% in 2000 and 30% in 2012, before finally reaching 51.6% in 2019, roughly fifteen times the initial level.

Behind these figures lie China's impressive achievements in higher education. First, the dramatic rise reflects the massive expansion of university enrolment, which has opened the door of college to millions of ordinary families. Moreover, the higher enrolment ratio means a far larger pool of skilled talent, supplying the economy with engineers, doctors and researchers. In my view, such progress not only improves individual prospects but also strengthens the nation's competitiveness.

In conclusion, the steady climb of the enrolment ratio speaks volumes about how far Chinese higher education has advanced, and I firmly believe this positive momentum will continue in the years ahead.`,guidance:[`总趋势领起再上数据。首句 The line chart illustrates the changes... 套用了"亮出图表主题"句架，完成"简要描述"的第一步；As is clearly shown... a steady and remarkable upward trend 先给整体结论，再用 3.4%、11% in 2000、30% in 2012、51.6% 挑点佐证，最后一句 roughly fifteen times 点出翻倍幅度——描述客观、不逐年罗列，落实了 outline 第一段"简要描述图表趋势"。`,`概括句过渡后分点评论。Behind these figures lie China's impressive achievements 用一句概括句把话题从数字接到成就，承上启下；First... reflects the massive expansion of university enrolment 与 Moreover... a far larger pool of skilled talent 分两点写扩招与人才，每点都拴回图表数据；In my view... not only... but also 谨慎表态，兑现 outline 第二段的评论任务。`,`重申核心并展望收尾。In conclusion, the steady climb... speaks volumes about how far Chinese higher education has advanced 换一种说法重申"上升=成就"这一核心，呼应主题；and I firmly believe this positive momentum will continue 以展望收束，不引入新论点，对应 outline 第三段"收束呼应主题"。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the chart below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the chart and comment on China's achievements in higher education . You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第2套 写作的图表内容为：图表标题为“Gross enrolment ratio in higher education in China (1990 - 2019)”，图表横轴代表年份，从1990年到2019年，纵轴代表高等教育毛入学率，范围从0.0%到60.0%。数据来源于教育部，从1990年到2019年每年的数据： 1990年: 3.4% 1995年: 6.86% 2000年: 11% 2001年: 13.3% 2002年: 15% 2003年: 17% 2004年: 19% 2005年: 21% 2006年: 22% 2007年: 23% 2008年: 23.3% 2009年: 24.2% 2010年: 26% 2011年: 26.5% 2012年: 30% 2013年: 34.5% 2014年: 37.5% 2015年: 40.0% 2016年: 42.7% 2017年: 45.7% 2018年: 48.1% 2019年: 51.6%）`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：总趋势领起再上数据。首句 The line chart illustrates the changes... 套用了"亮出图表主题"句架，完成"简要描述"的第一步；As is clearly shown... a steady and remarkable upward trend 先给整体结论，再用 3.4%、11% in 2000、30% in 2012、51.6% 挑点佐证。概括句过渡后分点评论。Behind these figures lie China's impressive achievements 用一句概括句把话题从数字接到成就，承上启下；First... reflects the massive expansion of university enrolment 与 Moreover... a far larger pool of skilled talent 分两点写扩招与人才。重申核心并展望收尾。In conclusion, the steady climb... speaks volumes about how far Chinese higher education has advanced 换一种说法重申"上升=成就"这一核心，呼应主题；and I firmly believe this positive momentum will continue 以展望收束，不引入新论点。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 M: How are you enjoying your new job? 2 W: So far, so good, I don't miss having managers who deliver blunt, harsh feedback in the name of efficiency. 3 M: From the way you described your last company, no wonder they had a problem with high staff turnover. 4 W: Yeah. I couldn't wait to get out of there once my contract expired. The problem with a company culture that prizes directness above all else is that it creates a toxic culture of brilliant jerks that drives people out and erodes itself from within. 5 M: My company's managers tend to be accommodating and kind, overlooking mistakes or issues so as not to hurt feelings. Issues often get ignored there until they build up and reach a crisis point. 6 W: That's not surprising. My new company seems to employ a feedback policy that combines compassion and directness. Employees have the power to speak up, give feedback, disagree and discuss problems in real time. It seems to help us to course correct, improve and meet challenges while also building teams that collaborate and care for one another. 7 M: But that would be based on an atmosphere of mutual trust, wouldn't it? Otherwise, people might interpret feedback as some kind of personal attack. 8 W: True. Without an atmosphere of trust, feedback can create stress and self-doubt. But I think when we get feedback from someone we trust, we understand that the feedback isn't some kind of personal attack. It's actually a kind of support because it's offered in the spirit of helping us improve. I think sometimes people need to shift their mindsets around how they receive feedback. 9 M: Yes. Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow. It's the basis for healthy parenting, lasting friendships, career development, and so much more. If we shelter our children, friends, and colleagues from information that might enrich and enhance their lives, we're not being caring. We're actually doing harm to them. 10 W: That's exactly right. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 W: How was your holiday? Not too many other tourists around, were there? 2 M: No, very few relatively. But I found myself moving from one accommodation to another, trying to find the perfect place. It made me realize that indecision is a big problem for me. Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot. 3 W: It seems you suffer from "Fear of Better Options." I've read about it. It describes this loop of indecision as part of our programming. Essentially, we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making process, because as human beings, we are hard-wired to optimize. We have always looked to get the best things we can as a sort of survival of the fittest. Optimizing isn't the problem, but rather the process that we go through. 4 M: Well, that makes me feel better. But I think thanks to technology, we can make comparisons more easily and have more access to choice and customization. We can now see what we could have, how we might get it, and what others have that we might want. We keep looking over and return to the same options again and again. 5 W: Yes. "Fear of Better Options" offers little benefit. It's an ailment of abundance. You must have choices to have that fear of missing out on better options. 6 M: Yes, I need to note when I'm worrying about inconsequential things, I guess. If I'm spending too much time worrying over what to have for lunch, I'm robbing myself of the energy to focus on the things that matter. 7 W: Exactly. But for more important matters, I think gut instinct might be overrated. When you have 30 odd options, trusting your gut is not practical. What you need to do is research: have a process, invest time exploring your options, and eliminate as many things as you can. The most toxic part of decision making is going over the same options time and time again. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 The role of homework in classrooms is not a new debate. Many parents and teachers are ardent supporters of homework. But do all students benefit from homework? A 2006 research paper suggested some correlation between the amount of homework done by a student and future academic achievement for middle and high school students but not so much for younger kids. 2 A Stanford study in 2014 suggested the same was true for students in California's affluent communities. The findings challenged the idea that homework was "inherently good." The researchers concluded that there was an upper limit to the correlation between homework and achievement, suggesting that high school students shouldn't be doing more than two hours of homework a night. And the most valuable kind of homework for elementary-level children was simply assigned free reading. 3 The topic gets more complicated when we talk about the divide between rural and urban communities. Studies found that in remote areas, the poor quality or lack of internet access can put students at a disadvantage because 70% of teachers in these areas assign homework that requires internet access. But one in three households doesn't have internet. Experts assert homework requiring the internet isn't fair. 4 While the debate continues about the effect of homework on academic achievements, there are studies focusing on other benefits of homework. A study in Germany found that homework could have an effect on students' personalities, suggesting that doing homework might help kids to become more conscientious and independent learners. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 Robert Goddard, an American born in 1882, is widely regarded as the world's first rocket scientist. 2 At age 27, Goddard published his first book in which he hypothesized that a rocket launched from Earth could reach the Moon. Like many visionaries, the young scientist encountered numerous skeptics. In January 1920, The New York Times ridiculed Goddard's theory that rockets could be utilized for space exploration. Forty-nine years later, Apollo 11 reached the Moon and the famed newspaper published an apology to Goddard. 3 Goddard launched his first rocket from an aunt's farm in his native Massachusetts in March, 1926. His maiden rocket voyage lasted a mere 3 seconds. It scaled an altitude of only 12 meters. Nonetheless, it was a milestone in rocket science. 4 Goddard later consulted with a weather expert and determined that the climate of New Mexico was ideal for year-round rocket launches. In 1930, Goddard and his family relocated there, to a remote valley in the southwest of the country. There he established a laboratory and test range. 5 However, the ambitious scientist received negligible support from the government. For four years, wealthy businessman Daniel Guggenheim provided Goddard with an annual 25,000 dollars grant to pursue his dreams. Other rocket enthusiasts also raised funds for him. Over time, Goddard's rocket grew more sophisticated and included the installation of instruments. 6 In spite of his many successes, Goddard was never able to interest the US military in rocket-propelled weapons. He was granted over 200 patents and continued to pioneer rocket technology until his death in 1945. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Working for a new venture comes with a lot of risks. There's instability, unclear responsibilities, and the need to be a master of all trades. But the primary benefit is usually the passion and excitement associated with playing a role in a promising new company. The person to thank for that passion and excitement is almost always the entrepreneur. 2 There's something about the founder's energy and enthusiasm that infects the rest of the team. The willingness to take risks may inspire others to be more courageous. The optimism and positivity may motivate people to focus less on trivial and unimportant matters. The celebration of milestones may prompt staff to be more grateful about their own accomplishments and privileges. 3 What becomes set in the firm's culture is a contagious collection of affirmative and positive emotions which are usually shared among the team. Science has already done a good job of proving the results that follow. These include better processes, greater team cohesion, reduced conflict and sharper alertness. 4 But what is yet to be demonstrated is whether the founder's passion leads to increased team performance. This was recently tested in research, which analyzed the teams of 73 new companies across a range of industries such as IT, medicine and energy. The CEOs were consulted once again, years after the initial analysis. And most shared their firm's performance reports, so that their success could be more objectively measured. 5 Entrepreneurial teams generally progress through three phases. The first is inventing a product or service, the second is founding the venture to sell that product or service, and the third is developing the firm so it continues to grow. 6 The researchers discovered that when the team is passionate about the third phase—developing the firm—there's a clear link to performance. But the first phase—a passion for invention—is not a reliable indicator that the firm will still be open for business a few years later. Likewise, the second—a passion for founding the venture—doesn't necessarily translate into great success. 7 The solution to great team performance stems from a willingness to recruit others who could direct their passion towards the third phase of entrepreneurialism: developing the business. Employing more staff can in itself be a risk for an entrepreneur, as is paying them big dollars to attract them. On many occasions, the entrepreneurs reported not paying themselves a wage at all initially, in order to cover salaries and expenses. Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 Ageing is a curious thing and people's desire to beat it—and death—has become an industry worth hundreds of billions of dollars. 2 Despite the huge investment into research, ageing remains somewhat obscure, although there are certain things researchers do understand. They know that women tend to have longer lifespans, living on average six years longer than men. No one is really certain of the reason for this, although the speculation centers around the idea that women are more capable of surviving or handling disease than men. For virtually every disease, the effects are greater on men than they are on women. 3 Some suggest that women's immune systems benefit from their tendency to prioritize and nurture social connections. But for me, this explanation is hardly convincing. 4 Researchers also know, to an extent, what causes ageing. For 60 years, it was believed that cells would continue to divide forever. It was only uncovered in relatively recent times that older people's cells divide a smaller number of times than younger people's. Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever. Human cells have a limited reproductive ability. To an extent, we can postpone the eventual stop of cells' dividing, through nutrition, exercise, good sleep, and even relaxation techniques. But we cannot stop the ageing process. 5 And researchers are yet to answer the ultimate question of ageing. Why does the body ultimately fall to pieces? 6 In the opinion of some of the world's best scientific minds on the subject, part of the reason we don't yet have an answer is because many researchers are looking in the wrong direction. Many public health policy makers believe that the resolution of age-associated disease will tell us something fundamental about the ageing process, but say some top scientists, that's completely erroneous. They point to when the diseases of childhood were eliminated, but this did not provide any insight into childhood development. In the same way, the idea that the resolution of age-associated disease like heart disease and stroke will inform us about ageing is not based on sound science or logic. 7 At best, if the major causes of death in developed countries were eliminated, this would only add a decade to average life expectancy. But while there is money available to be spent on it, the search to understand the secrets of ageing will be ongoing. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1 Good afternoon. In today's talk, we'll discuss how managers can get their staff to do what they are asked. 2 Much to their frustration, managers often struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions. Often they blame their employees: "They don't read emails, they don't listen, they don't care"-that kind of thing. But according to recent research conducted in Australia, it looks like it's not the employees' fault but the managers'. 3 It's easy to understand why people sometimes disobey procedures intentionally. Occasionally, it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time. At other times, they may disagree with the spirit of the procedure, the effort demanded, the time consumed, the lack of potential effectiveness. And every now and then, they just don't want to, maybe deliberately or out of stubbornness. 4 So apart from that, what else gets in the way of procedural compliance? The research scholars surveyed 152 blue-collar workers from two separate sites in the mining industry. They asked the workers a range of procedure-related questions, such as whether they found the procedures useful, how confident they felt in their job, how comfortable they were to speak up in the workplace, and how closely they followed any new procedures set by their managers. They were also asked to rate the extent to which they perceived their supervisors to be helpful. 5 That last statement was the most instructive because, as the researchers found, there was a remarkably strong correlation between how helpful supervisors were perceived to be and how likely their employees were to follow their directives. Supervisors' helping behavior was found to be motivational in nature. It increased employees' perception of the likelihood of success in the attainment of job goals, and therefore fostered a willingness to dedicate their effort and ability to their work. 6 In short, managers should be ongoing role models for the change. As the saying goes: "Do as I do, not as I say". To affect behavioral change, what's most required is interaction and involvement—the human touch—and, naturally, processes that add value. Although procedures are designed to guide and support employees' work, employees, it seems, can't always be expected to comply with procedures that are not seen as useful. And of course, managers shouldn't keep resending emails. They're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports, but they're a hopeless tool if what a manager is desiring is a change in behavior. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`How does the woman describe her previous company's culture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Weird.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Efficient.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Tolerant.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Toxic.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“The problem with a company culture that prizes directness above all else is that it creates a toxic culture of brilliant jerks that drives people out…”。被问者亲口的定性词。女士在谈她前公司时，自己直接说出 it creates a toxic culture，“toxic”就是她对那种文化的定性，是被问一方的直接评价，正是答案句的信号。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about his company's managers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are arrogant.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are ignorant.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are ambitious.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are accommodating.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P5：“My company's managers tend to be accommodating and kind, overlooking mistakes or issues so as not to hurt feelings.”。男士直接陈述自家管理者。男士主语直接是 My company's managers tend to be accommodating，紧扣题问的“他公司的管理者”，是说话人对该对象的直接陈述句，定位明确。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say the employees in her new company can do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They can think big.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They can air their views.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They can break conventions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They can work flexible hours.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P6：“Employees have the power to speak up, give feedback, disagree and discuss problems in real time.”。授权表达 have the power to。Employees have the power to speak up 用“have the power to”明确点出员工“有权做某事”，正是“can do”题要找的授权信号，后面罗列的动作即答案来源。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about constructive feedback?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It can alter people's mindsets.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It can lead to new discoveries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It enables people to learn and grow.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is conducive to critical thinking. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P9：“Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow.”。结论口吻 after all + is how we。Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow 用 after all 引出总结、is how we 给出定性结论，是男士对反馈作用下的核心论断。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about his holiday?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He kept looking for the best place to stay.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He met many tourists from other countries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He had a great time sightseeing and relaxing.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He managed to visit a different city each day.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“But I found myself moving from one accommodation to another, trying to find the perfect place. Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot.”。转折 But + 对比 Instead of。答案句的信号是双重的：先有转折 But I found myself moving from one accommodation to another。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say people tend to do when making decisions?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Prioritize what is essential to their best advantage.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stretch out the process in search of the optimal.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Deliberate the consequences that may occur.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Take all relevant factors into consideration.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Essentially, we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making process, because as human beings, we are hard-wired to optimize.”。tendency to + 总结词 Essentially。答案信号是"总结+点天性"：Essentially（本质上）领出结论，紧跟 we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making process…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What has made decision making increasingly difficult?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Time pressure.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Tight budget.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Modern technology.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Fierce competition.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But I think thanks to technology, we can make comparisons more easily and have more access to choice and customization.”。因果信号 thanks to。答案信号是因果短语 thanks to technology——thanks to（多亏、由于）直接引出原因 technology。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`According to the woman, what should people do when making important decisions?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Research as many different options as possible.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Avoid going over the same options repeatedly.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Focus on what is practical.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Trust their gut instinct. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P7：“What you need to do is research: have a process, invest time exploring your options, and eliminate as many things as you can.”。建议句 What you need to do + 最高级 most toxic。答案信号有两处：What you need to do is research 是直接的建议句（你需要做的是…）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What did the 2006 research find about homework?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is beneficial to poor as well as rich communities.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is conducive to children's future development.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is welcome to parents but not to children.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is not of much help to younger children.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“A 2006 research paper suggested some correlation between the amount of homework done by a student and future academic achievement for middle and high…”。转折 but 之后才是落点。本句靠转折信号 but not so much for younger kids 定位：but 之前讲初高中生有关联是铺垫，but 之后对低龄孩子的"没那么有用"才是该研究真正要强调的发现，也正是答案所在。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What do experts think of homework requiring internet access?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may put some students in remote areas at a disadvantage.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It gives the majority of students ready access to their teachers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It effectively improves the learning quality of students in rural areas.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can bridge the learning gap between kids of different backgrounds.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Studies found that in remote areas, the poor quality or lack of internet access can put students at a disadvantage because 70% of teachers in these ar…”。专家断言句 + isn't fair 评价词。定位靠两处信号：一是 Experts assert 直接对应题干问的"专家看法"，二是评价词 put students at a disadvantage 与 isn't fair 给出明确负面定性，正是态度题要抓的落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What conclusion could be drawn from the study in Germany?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Diligent students tend to do their homework independently.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The focus of homework should always be on school subjects.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Doing homework exerts a positive effect on kids' personality development.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The benefits of doing homework vary widely from individual to individual. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“A study in Germany found that homework could have an effect on students' personalities,”。研究标志词 in Germany + suggesting 引出结论。定位靠 A study in Germany found that 对应题干所问研究，再靠 suggesting that 引出该研究的结论。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Goddard's idea of using rockets for space exploration?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It was something he apologized for later.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was ridiculed by the New York Times.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was a forty-nine-year plan.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was considered visionary.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“In January 1920, The New York Times ridiculed Goddard's theory that rockets could be utilized for space exploration.”。专有名词+负面动词定位。答案句的信号是专有名词 The New York Times 加一个鲜明的负面动词 ridiculed（嘲笑）。听到《纽约时报》这个被原样念出的机构名，再抓住紧跟的那个评价动词 ridiculed，这一句就是讲世人怎么看待他设想的答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about Goddard's first rocket voyage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It was of great significance to rocket science.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was completed in the state of New Mexico.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was somehow delayed about 12 minutes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It failed due to a sudden change of weather.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“His maiden rocket voyage lasted a mere 3 seconds. It scaled an altitude of only 12 meters. Nonetheless, it was a milestone in rocket science.”。转折 Nonetheless 后才是定性。前两句净说短处（a mere 3 seconds、only 12 meters），真正给这次首飞定性的，是转折词 Nonetheless 之后那句——milestone（里程碑）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`Why did Goddard move to New Mexico?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A laboratory and test range was already set up there.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Its climate was ideal for year-round rocket launching.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A weather expert invited him to go there for his mission.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Its remote valleys were appealing to him and his family.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Goddard later consulted with a weather expert and determined that the climate of New Mexico was ideal for year-round rocket launches.”。determined that 引出判断即原因。答案信号是 determined that the climate…was ideal for year-round rocket launches——determined that 引出他做出的判断。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about Goddard's achievements?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He won an award from the US government for his work.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He gained recognition from rocket scientists worldwide.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He was granted over 200 patents in rocket technology.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He boosted the military strength of the United States. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P6：“He was granted over 200 patents and continued to pioneer rocket technology until his death in 1945.”。被原样念出的数字硬指标。答案句的信号是一个被原样念出的可量化硬指标 granted over 200 patents（被授予200多项专利）。成就题里，这种数字+专有概念的句子最实、最难造假，听到 over 200 patents 立刻锁定它就是成就答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about working for a new venture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It requires entrepreneurial experience.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is usually financially rewarding.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can be quite frustrating.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can be rather risky.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Working for a new venture comes with a lot of risks. There's instability, unclear responsibilities, and the need to be a master of all trades.”。开篇定性句＋列举佐证。录音第一句就直接定性 comes with a lot of risks，随后用 instability, unclear responsibilities 列举来佐证「风险」，开篇主题句正是答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What has science demonstrated regarding the positive culture of a new venture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It contributes to rapid business expansion.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It inspires willingness to make sacrifices.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It reduces conflict among team members.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It encourages creation and innovation.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Science has already done a good job of proving the results that follow. These include better processes, greater team cohesion,”。Science proving 后 These include 引出清单。信号词 Science has already done a good job of proving 正对题干的 science demonstrated，随后 These include 引出一串结果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about entrepreneurs at the initial stage of a new venture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They have unrealistic expectations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They often work without any pay.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Few can find willing investors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many are idealistic dreamers. 播放本篇`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P7：“On many occasions, the entrepreneurs reported not paying themselves a wage at all initially, in order to cover salaries and expenses.”。时间词 initially 对上题干 initial stage。录音 not paying themselves a wage at all initially 里的 initially 与题干的 initial stage 精确对应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the possible reason why women tend to live longer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They have better dietary habits.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They bear fewer social responsibilities.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are born with a stronger ability to socialize.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are better able to survive or handle disease.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“No one is really certain of the reason for this, although the speculation centers around the idea that women are more capable of surviving or handling…”。原因解释信号 the reason…the speculation centers around the idea。这句用 the speculation centers around the idea that（推测围绕这一想法）明确引出原因的内容。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What is the recent discovery about human cells?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They have a limited reproductive ability.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They depend on adequate sleep to thrive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They keep dividing throughout one's life.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They strengthen with regular exercise.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever. Human cells have a limited reproductive ability.”。对比 only…+ 直陈结论句 Human cells have a limited…。Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever 用 only 把能永远分裂限定给癌细胞。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What do many public health policy makers believe?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The process of ageing can ultimately be brought under control.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Improved health care for the elderly will contribute to longevity.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Prevention of heart disease and stroke will increase life expectancy.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The resolution of age-related diseases will solve the mystery of ageing. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P6：“Many public health policy makers believe that the resolution of age-associated disease will tell us something fundamental about the ageing process,”。主体标志 Many public health policy makers believe that…。Many public health policy makers believe that 直接点出题干所问的主体并带 believe。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`Why are managers often frustrated with their employees?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are reluctant to follow instructions.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They fail to answer emails promptly.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They cannot understand directives.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They do not show due respect.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Much to their frustration, managers often struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions.”。frustration 原词点题。这句开头的 Much to their frustration 正是题干 frustrated 的来源,紧接着 struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`Why do employees sometimes disobey procedures intentionally?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They have not been trained to follow the rules.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are not satisfied with the management.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They want to avoid unnecessary losses.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They find their voice go unheeded.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Occasionally, it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time. At other times, they may disagree with the spirit of the procedure,”。because 引出原因。it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time 用 because 直接给出第一个原因,后面 the effort demanded, the time consumed。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`When are employees more likely to follow instructions, according to the researchers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) When they are on good terms with their managers.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) When they find their job goals easily attainable.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) When they find their supervisors helpful.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) When they are financially motivated.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“There was a remarkably strong correlation between how helpful supervisors were perceived to be and how likely their employees were to follow their dir…”。strong correlation 挂钩条件与结果。a remarkably strong correlation between how helpful supervisors were perceived to be and how likely their employe…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about emails?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are a useless tool for managers to change employee behavior.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They prove to be a good means for managers to give instructions.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They should be reserved for urgent communication.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are seldom used for sharing confidential data.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“They're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports, but they're a hopeless tool if what a manager is desiring is a change in behavior.”。but 后才是真立场。but they're a hopeless tool if what a manager is desiring is a change in behavior 中的 but 是关键转折:前半承认邮件传数据有效,but 后说它对改变行为毫无用处——转折后那…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`abundance`,B:`astonishment`,C:`entailed`,D:`envisaged`,E:`equation`,F:`expended`,G:`feat`,H:`fiscally`,I:`impetus`,J:`overhaul`,K:`permanently`,L:`plight`,M:`prosper`,N:`shatter`,O:`ultimately`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-1`,text:`I'm always baffled when I walk into a pharmacy and see shelves bursting with various vitamins, extracts and other supplements, all promising to accelerate or promote weight loss. Aisles of marketing genius belie(掩饰) the fact that, [26] , weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic. Economist Jessica Irvine wrote a book about how she used math to help her lose more than 18 kilograms. If calories taken in are less than calories [27] , weight shall be lost, and so it is with money.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-2`,text:`Despite the [28] of financial products, services and solutions geared towards accumulating wealth, it all begins with the same [29] : getting ahead financially requires a reduction of spending, so that income is greater than expenses. I was reminded of this again recently listening to an interview with Nicole Haddow, the author of Smashed Avocado, explaining how she cracked the property market at 31. It was quite a [30] , given where she had been two years earlier.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-3`,text:`Nicole didn't celebrate her 30th birthday as she had [31] . She was sobbing at the dinner table with her parents, with whom she had just moved back in. She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan, but to her [32] , her father, an accountant, told her that her financial [33] wasn't as bad as she thought. He said, on her income, with some changes, she would be able to buy an investment unit within two years, which she did.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-4`,text:`Nicole admitted she was fortunate, as she was able to live with her parents and [34] her spending—and life—to get herself on track financially. Creating a gap between her income and spending required a paradigm shift and [35] sacrifice and commitment, but by going into financial lockdown, Nicole gained financial independence.`}]},a,{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-1`,text:`We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-2`,text:`Researchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-3`,text:`Most of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-4`,text:`Some argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of "good" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-5`,text:`Human beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-6`,text:`Some researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-7`,text:`There's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-8`,text:`Rather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.`}]},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-1`,text:`The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-2`,text:`For all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-3`,text:`The problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-4`,text:`This new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-5`,text:`Similar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a "catastrophic failure" of technology.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-6`,text:`Increasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`Aisles of marketing genius belie(掩饰) the fact that, __26__, weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic.`,answer:`O`,explanation:`两个逗号之间的插入语，修饰整句。ultimately = 归根结底，与下句"重量由算术法则决定"形成"广告再花哨，最终还得回到算术"的对照。市场营销天才掩盖了一个事实，归根结底，减重由算术法则决定。ultimately 完美承接 belie the fact 的转折语气。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-1`,quote:`Aisles of marketing genius belie(掩饰) the fact that, [26] , weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`If calories taken in are less than calories __27__, weight shall be lost`,answer:`F`,explanation:`calories ___，名词+过去分词作后置定语。calories expended = 被消耗的卡路里，与 calories taken in 形成"摄入 vs 消耗"对照。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-1`,quote:`If calories taken in are less than calories [27] , weight shall be lost, and so it is with money.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`Despite the __28__ of financial products, services and solutions geared towards accumulating wealth`,answer:`A`,explanation:`the + 空 + of 结构必填名词。an abundance of … 是固定搭配，且"丰富的金融产品"与下文"reduction of spending"形成让步对比。尽管金融产品"丰富"，开头仍是同一套基本原理。an abundance of 是地道搭配，语义指向"大量、丰富"，与转折后"回归本源"形成对照，故选 abundance。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-2`,quote:`Despite the [28] of financial products, services and solutions geared towards accumulating wealth, it all begins with the same [29] : getting ahead financially requires a reduction of spending, so that income is greater than expenses.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`it all begins with the same __29__: getting ahead financially requires a reduction of spending, so that income is greater than expenses`,answer:`E`,explanation:`the same + 空，限定词后填可数名词单数。冒号后给出的"收入 > 支出"正是一道等式，呼应首段"weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-2`,quote:`Despite the [28] of financial products, services and solutions geared towards accumulating wealth, it all begins with the same [29] : getting ahead financially requires a reduction of spending, so that income is greater than expenses.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`It was quite a __30__, given where she had been two years earlier.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`quite a + 空，单数可数名词。quite a feat = 真是了不起的壮举，对应 Nicole 两年内从负债到买房。上句 explaining how she cracked the property market at 31——31 岁就攻克房地产市场，再回看两年前的窘境，称之"壮举"恰到好处；quite a feat 是高频地道表达。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-2`,quote:`It was quite a [30] , given where she had been two years earlier.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`Nicole didn't celebrate her 30th birthday as she had __31__.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`had + 空，现在完成时（过去完成时）需过去分词。as she had envisaged = 如她原本设想的那样，与下文"实际却在哭"形成反差。as she had envisaged 是典型的"contrary to what she had envisaged"句式：Nicole 原本设想 30 岁生日会怎样庆祝。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-3`,quote:`Nicole didn't celebrate her 30th birthday as she had [31] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan, but to her __32__, her father …`,answer:`B`,explanation:`but to her ___ 句式，介词 to + 物主代词 + 名词。to her astonishment 套语 + 与 sobbing → 父亲安慰形成意外反差。这恰是"令她惊讶"的反差，astonishment 完美承接。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-3`,quote:`She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan, but to her [32] , her father, an accountant, told her that her financial [33] wasn't as bad as she thought.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`her father, an accountant, told her that her financial __33__ wasn't as bad as she thought`,answer:`L`,explanation:`形容词 financial + 空，需名词被修饰。financial plight = 财务困境，与上文"没有稳定收入 + 1.2 万信用卡债"对应。上文交代 She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan——典型的财务"困境"；父亲安慰说 financial plight"不像她想的那么糟"，语义、逻辑、搭配三合一。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-3`,quote:`She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan, but to her [32] , her father, an accountant, told her that her financial [33] wasn't as bad as she thought.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`she was able to live with her parents and __34__ her spending—and life—to get herself on track financially`,answer:`J`,explanation:`to + 空 + 宾语，不定式后的动词原形。overhaul her spending = 彻底整顿她的开支，与"回归正轨"目的句呼应。原句目的 to get herself on track financially——为了在财务上回到正轨，需要的正是"对开支（和生活）彻底整顿"。overhaul 既可及物且常与 spending、system 等大范围对象搭配，最贴。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-4`,quote:`Nicole admitted she was fortunate, as she was able to live with her parents and [34] her spending—and life—to get herself on track financially.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`Creating a gap between her income and spending required a paradigm shift and __35__ sacrifice and commitment`,answer:`C`,explanation:`required … and ___，与 required 并列的过去式动词。entailed sacrifice and commitment = 必然要求/牵涉牺牲和承诺。Creating a gap … required a paradigm shift and entailed sacrifice and commitment——"创造收支差距既需要思路转变、也必然牵涉牺牲和承诺"，entail 与 require 同义并列，是地道搭配。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-bp-4`,quote:`Creating a gap between her income and spending required a paradigm shift and [35] sacrifice and commitment, but by going into financial lockdown, Nicole gained financial independence.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`The total amount of damage to Notre Dame Cathedral can be assessed only when its structure is considered safe.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：结构安全才能评估整体损失。定位句“However, fire officers have said a complete inventory of the damage will not be possible until the cathedral structure has been de…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-I`,quote:`The interior minister, Christophe Castaner, visited the cathedral on Tuesday afternoon to see the extent of the devastation.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Once again people began to argue whether Notre Dame Cathedral was going to collapse even without the fire.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：再次开始争论。定位句“But as the emergency services picked through the burnt debris, a row was resurfacing over accusations that the beloved cathedral,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-C`,quote:`More than €650 million was raised in a few hours on Tuesday as French business leaders and global corporations announced they would donate to a restor…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`The Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe was said to have helped unite the French nation.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`匹配 K 段：灾难帮助团结法国。定位句“French political commentators noted the devastating fire had succeeded where Macron had failed in uniting the country.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-K`,quote:`French political commentators noted the devastating fire had succeeded where Macron had failed in uniting the country.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`The roof of Notre Dame Cathedral was built with large numbers of densely laid-out wood beams.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：屋顶密铺木梁。定位句“The fire, which had started at the base of the 93-metre spire(尖塔) at about 6:40 pm on Monday,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-F`,quote:`The fire, which had started at the base of the 93-metre spire(尖塔) at about 6:40 pm on Monday, spread through the cathedral's roof,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Renovation workers of Notre Dame Cathedral were questioned to find out the cause of the accident.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`匹配 B 段：讯问修缮工人查火因。定位句“The revelation of how close France came to losing its most famous cathedral emerged as police investigators questioned workers inv…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-B`,quote:`The revelation of how close France came to losing its most famous cathedral emerged as police investigators questioned workers involved in the restora…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Had the bell towers' wooden frames burned down, the heavy bells would have crashed down.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`匹配 H 段：钟楼木架若烧大钟将砸下（虚拟语气）。定位句“If the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire, it could have sent the bells—the largest of which, the Emmanuel Bell,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-H`,quote:`While the world looked on, the 500 firefighters at the scene then battled to prevent the flames from reaching the two main towers,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`The timely action of the firefighters prevented the fire from reaching the Cathedral's bell towers.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`匹配 A 段：消防员及时行动护钟楼。定位句“Notre Dame Cathedral in the heart of Paris was within "15 to 30 minutes" of complete destruction as firefighters battled to stop f…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-A`,quote:`Notre Dame Cathedral in the heart of Paris was within "15 to 30 minutes" of complete destruction as firefighters battled to stop flames reaching its b…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`Apart from the fire, the water used to extinguish it also caused a lot of damage to Notre Dame Cathedral.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`匹配 J 段：灭火用水也造成损害。定位句“As well as damage from the heat, which firefighters said reached more than 800 ℃,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-J`,quote:`The culture minister, Franck Riester, said religious relics saved from the cathedral were being securely held at the Hotel de Ville,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`There has been argument over the years as to who should pay for the restoration of Notre Dame Cathedral.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`匹配 D 段：多年来争论谁出钱修缮。定位句“The cathedral is owned by the French state and has been at the centre of a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-D`,quote:`The cathedral is owned by the French state and has been at the centre of a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration work of the collapsi…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`News of the Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe instantly caught media attention throughout the world.`,options:e(a.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`匹配 G 段：灾难新闻瞬间引起全球媒体关注。定位句“Indeed, within minutes the fire occupied headlines of every major global newspaper and television network.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-mp-G`,quote:`A collection of dramatic videos and photos quickly spread across social media, showing the horrifying destruction,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What do people generally think about drawing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is a gift creative people are endowed with.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is a skill that is acquired with practice.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is an art form that is appreciated by all.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is an ability everyone should cultivate.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily,”。预判的"天生才能"与 a gift creative people are endowed with 一一对应，A 项是定位句前半的精准同义替换。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-1`,quote:`We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about designers and artists?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are declining gradually in number.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are keen on changing shabby surroundings.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They add beauty and charm to the world.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They spend most of their lives drawing.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.”。把"没有他们世界会 shabby"还原为"他们让世界不 shabby、即美丽迷人"，正好对应 They add beauty and charm to the world.。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-3`,quote:`There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does Professor D. B. Dowd argue in his book?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Drawing is a skill that requires special training.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The value of drawing tends to be overestimated.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Drawing should be redefined as a realistic illusion.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P4：“In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see…”。教授的真正主张是 instead of 后的 a personal capacity（个人能力），其语义内核即"人人都具备"——正好对应 Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-4`,quote:`In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a pr…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What have some researchers found from one study about doodling?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is a must for maintaining a base level of brain activity.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is the most reliable stimulant to activate the brain.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can turn something boring into something interesting.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It helps improve concentration and memory.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P6：“Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention.”。D 的两个并列成分 concentration / memory 各自由原文两句独立支撑（pay attention / remembered more），实现完整覆盖。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-6`,quote:`Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What is characteristic of people with drawing talent?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Sensitivity to cognitive stimulation.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Subtlety of representation.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Accuracy in categorization.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Precision in visual perception.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P7：“There's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world.”。选项 D 把原文"perceive more accurately"整体名词化为"precision in visual perception"，是定位句的最简同义替换。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p1-7`,quote:`There's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about car advertisements?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They portray drivers who enjoy speed on the road.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They present a false picture of the autonomy cars provide.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They pursue individuality and originality in design concept.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They overestimate the potential market of autonomous cars.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality…”。预判要的"广告呈现的自由是失真的"，B 恰好以 false picture + autonomy 两词整齐对应原文，是定位句的同义压缩。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-1`,quote:`There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of fre…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply about the various gadgets on cars?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They can help to alleviate traffic jams.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Most of them are as effective as advertised.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Only some can be put to use under current traffic conditions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are constantly upgraded to make driving easier and safer.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“For all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're g…”。预判要的"只有一部分装置真正派用"，C 用 only some 精准对上原文 those which... 的子集限定，是唯一吻合预判的选项。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-2`,quote:`For all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about the use of navigation apps?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is likely to create traffic jams in other places.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It helps a great deal in easing traffic congestion.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It sharply reduces the incidence of traffic accidents.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It benefits those who are learning to drive.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.”。预判要的"导航把堵车推到别处"，A 用 create traffic jams + in other places 精准对应 spread onto + the side roads，是定位句的同义改写。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-3`,quote:`But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about technology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Its consequences are usually difficult to assess.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It seldom delivers all the benefits as promised.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It depends on the required knowledge for application.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Its benefits are guaranteed by collective wisdom.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended conse…”。预判要的"技术承诺难以完全兑现"，B 用 seldom + all ... as promised 精准对应原文的 never + as fully as we hope。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s2-p2-4`,quote:`The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What key message does the author try to convey in the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The consequences of technological innovation need not be exaggerated.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) There is always a price to pay to develop technology for a better world.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Technological innovation should be properly regulated.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The culture of Silicon Valley ought not to be emulated.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`预判要的"技术需妥善监管"，C 把 thoughtful regulation 直译为 properly regulated，又覆盖了 central control 这条中段证据，是全文主线的最大公约数。`,score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Qinghai is a province in northwest China, with an average elevation of over 3,000 meters, and most of its area is covered by mountains and plateaus. The province is named after Qinghai Lake, the largest saltwater lake in China. Qinghai Lake is praised as "the most beautiful lake in China". It is one of the most popular tourist attractions and a paradise for photographers and artists. Qinghai is blessed with magnificent mountains and rivers, a vast territory rich in natural resources, and abundant reserves of oil and natural gas. Driven by the oil and natural gas industry, the economy of many cities in the province has developed rapidly. Qinghai is especially famous for its abundant water resources. It is the source of China's three major rivers, namely the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the Lancang River, playing an important role in China's water ecology.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`青海是中国西北部的一个省份，平均海拔3000米以上，大部分地区为高山和高原。青海省得名于全国最大的咸水湖青海湖。青海湖被誉为"中国最美的湖泊",是最受欢迎的旅游景点之一,也是摄影师和艺术家的天堂。 青海山川壮丽,地大物博,石油和天然气储量丰富,省内许多城市的经济在石油和天然气工业带动下得到了长足发展。青海尤以水资源丰富而闻名,是中国三大河流长江、黄河和澜沧江的发源地,在中国的水生态中发挥着重要作用。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①青海（省）青海 作省名用约定俗成的音译加省份标注 Qinghai / Qinghai Province。首次出现写 Qinghai Province 点明它是省份，之后再提可只用 Qinghai；②“青海湖”是咸水湖名，采用「音译专名 + 通名 Lake」的约定译法 Qinghai Lake（或 Lake Qinghai，全篇取其一即可）；③“长江”用国际通行约定译名 the Yangtze River（也可 the Yangtze）。专名前要带定冠词 the；④“黄河”用约定译名 the Yellow River，前带定冠词 the；不要按拼音译成 Huanghe；⑤“澜沧江”用音译约定译名 the Lancang River（其境外段即 the Mekong River，考试写 the Lancang River 即可），前带定冠词 the。`,score:15}]}]},"cet6-2021-06-s3":{subtitle:`2021 年 6 月 · 站内收录`,minutes:130,words:1500,tags:[`真题`,`六级`],summary:`2021年6月英语六级真题第3套（听力与第1套共用），站内收录完整题面与答案，听力提供站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the chart below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the chart and comment on China's achievements in poverty alleviation. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第3套 写作的图表内容为：图表标题为“Rural population in poverty”，数据来源为中国国家统计局和中国国务院扶贫开发领导小组办公室。图表包含两组数据：左侧纵轴表示农村贫困人口数量（单位：百万人），范围从0到100；右侧纵轴表示贫困发生率（农村贫困人口占农村总人口的百分比），范围从0到12。横轴表示年份，从2012年到2020年。图表中，灰色柱状图表示每年的农村贫困人口数量，黑色实线和虚线分别连接了2012年到2018年，以及2018年到2020年各年份的贫困发生率数据点，清晰地展现了中国农村贫困人口数量和贫困发生率的下降趋势。 图表中的数据，大致估计： 农村贫困人口数量（Million people）： 2012年：约98 2013年：约82 2014年：约70 2015年：约56 2016年：约43 2017年：约31 2018年：约17 2019年：约5 2020年：约0）`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`The chart clearly illustrates the striking decline of rural poverty in China from 2012 to 2020. During this period, the rural poor population plunged from around 98 million to nearly zero, while the incidence of poverty fell in parallel from about 10.2% to almost 0%. Based on official statistics, this downward trend remained remarkably steady throughout the whole period.

Several factors account for this remarkable achievement. Above all, the Chinese government made poverty alleviation a national priority and rolled out a series of targeted measures, such as developing local industries, relocating residents from harsh areas and improving education. These well-designed policies steadily lifted millions of people out of poverty.

The significance of this progress can hardly be overstated. Not only has it dramatically improved the livelihood of the rural population, but it also offers a valuable model for other developing nations still fighting poverty. In my view, such an accomplishment fully demonstrates what determination and effective governance can bring about.`,guidance:[`一句趋势加起止值。本段落实了'一句概括总趋势'：首句 'The chart clearly illustrates the striking decline of rural poverty' 套用'引出图表'句架点明主题与方向；接着用 'plunged from around 98 million to nearly zero, while the incidence of poverty fell in parallel from about 10.2% to almost 0%' 只交代首尾两端数据，中间年份全部略去，且全段无一句主观评价，严守描述段的边界。`,`成因主句加实例回扣。本段落实了'成因分点加实例'：'Several factors account for this remarkable achievement' 作过渡兼主句，'such as developing local industries, relocating residents... and improving education' 用实例撑起成因，末句 'These well-designed policies steadily lifted millions of people out of poverty' 把举措与结果回扣，评论有据不空喊。`,`双层意义加表态收口。本段落实了'升华意义收全篇'：'can hardly be overstated' 强调意义，'Not only has it... but it also...' 分'民生改善'与'国际示范'两层展开且用了部分倒装，末句 'In my view, such an accomplishment fully demonstrates...' 表态收尾，给全文一个明确落点。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the chart below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the chart and comment on China's achievements in poverty alleviation. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.（站内补充·配图说明：2021年6月英语六级真题第3套 写作的图表内容为：图表标题为“Rural population in poverty”，数据来源为中国国家统计局和中国国务院扶贫开发领导小组办公室。图表包含两组数据：左侧纵轴表示农村贫困人口数量（单位：百万人），范围从0到100；右侧纵轴表示贫困发生率（农村贫困人口占农村总人口的百分比），范围从0到12。横轴表示年份，从2012年到2020年。图表中，灰色柱状图表示每年的农村贫困人口数量，黑色实线和虚线分别连接了2012年到2018年，以及2018年到2020年各年份的贫困发生率数据点，清晰地展现了中国农村贫困人口数量和贫困发生率的下降趋势。 图表中的数据，大致估计： 农村贫困人口数量（Million people）： 2012年：约98 2013年：约82 2014年：约70 2015年：约56 2016年：约43 2017年：约31 2018年：约17 2019年：约5 2020年：约0）`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`写作无标准答案，评分看三点：内容是否切题、结构是否完整、语言是否准确连贯。参考范文思路：一句趋势加起止值。本段落实了'一句概括总趋势'：首句 'The chart clearly illustrates the striking decline of rural poverty' 套用'引出图表'句架点明主题与方向；接着用 'plunged from around 98 million to nearly zero。成因主句加实例回扣。本段落实了'成因分点加实例'：'Several factors account for this remarkable achievement' 作过渡兼主句，'such as developing local industries, relocating residents... and improving education' 用实例撑起成因。双层意义加表态收口。本段落实了'升华意义收全篇'：'can hardly be overstated' 强调意义，'Not only has it... but it also...' 分'民生改善'与'国际示范'两层展开且用了部分倒装，末句 'In my view, such an accomplishment fully demonstrates...' 表态收尾，给全文一个明确落点。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:30,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`1 M: It's my last day at work tomorrow. I'll start my new job in two weeks. My human resources manager wants to conduct an interview with me before I leave. 2 W: Ah, an exit interview. Are you looking forward to it? 3 M: I'm not sure how I feel about it. I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time, but I'm not sure if I should tell them how I really feel. 4 W: To my way of thinking, there are two main potential benefits that come from unleashing an agitated stream of truth during an exit interview. The first is release. Unburdening yourself of frustration, and perhaps even anger to someone who isn't a friend or close colleague can be wonderfully freeing. 5 M: And let me guess. The second is that the criticism will, theoretically, help the organization I'm leaving to improve, making sure employees of the future are less likely to encounter what I did? 6 W: That's right. But the problem with the company improvement part is that very often it doesn't happen. An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't. In my company, the information gained from these interviews is often not confidential. And the information is used as dirt against another manager, or can be traded among senior managers. 7 M: Now you've got me rethinking what I'll disclose in the interview. There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry. It is a pretty small industry after all. 8 W: Anything you initially gain from the instant satisfaction of telling it like it is, you might lose down the track by injuring your future career prospects. 9 M: Right. Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer websites. 10 W: You could. And don't do the interview at all. Exit interviews are not mandatory. Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`1 M: Today, I'm talking to the renowned botanist, Jane Forster. 2 W: Thank you for inviting me to join you on the show, Henry. 3 M: Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity since the release of your latest documentary. Can you tell us a little about it? 4 W: Well, it follows my expedition to study the vegetation indigenous to the rainforest in equatorial areas of Southeast Asia. 5 M: You certainly get to travel to some very exotic locations. 6 W: It was far from glamorous, to be honest. The area we visited was accessible only by canoe and the living conditions in the hut were primitive, to say the least. There was no electricity. And our water supply was a nearby stream. 7 M: How were the weather conditions while you were there? 8 W: The weather was not conducive to our work at all, since the humidity was almost unbearable. At midday, we stayed in the hut and did nothing. It was too humid to either work or sleep. 9 M: How long did your team spend in the jungle? 10 W: Originally, we planned to be there for a month. But in the end, we stayed for only two weeks. 11 M: Why did you cut the expedition short? 12 W: Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching. And we had to evacuate at very short notice. 13 M: That sounds like a fascinating anecdote. 14 W: It was frightening. The fastest evacuation route was through river rapids. We had to navigate them carrying all of our equipment. 15 M: So overall, was the journey unsuccessful? 16 W: Absolutely not. We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life. 17 M: Why do you put up with such adverse conditions? 18 W: Botany is an obsession for me. Many of the destinations I visit have stunning scenery. And I get to meet a variety of people from all over the world. 19 M: So where will your next destination be? 20 W: I haven't decided yet. 21 M: Then we can leave it for another occasion. Thanks. Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`1 Scientists often use specialized jargon terms while communicating with laymen. Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice. In a new study, people exposed to jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous vehicles and surgical robots later said they were less interested in science than others who read about the same topics, but without the use of specialized terms. They also felt less informed about science and less qualified to discuss science topics. It's noteworthy that it made no difference if the jargon terms were defined in the text. Even when the terms were defined, readers still felt the same lack of engagement as readers who read jargon that wasn't explained. 2 The problem is that the mere presence of jargon sends a discouraging message to readers. Hillary Schulman, the author of the study, asserts that specialized words are a signal. Jargon tells people that the message isn't for them. 3 There's an even darker side to how people react to jargon. In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led people to doubt the actual science. They found the opposite when a text is easier to read. Then people are more persuaded. 4 Thus, it's important to communicate clearly when talking about complex science subjects. This is especially true with issues related to public health, like the safety of new medications and the benefits of vaccines. Schulman concedes that the use of jargon is appropriate with scientific audiences. But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their language. They need to eliminate jargon. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`1 At the beginning of the twentieth century, on the Gulf Coast in the US state of Texas, there was a hill where gas leakage was so noticeable that schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire. 2 Pattillo Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill. Oil wells weren't drilled back then; they were essentially dug. The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole. Higgins had forecast oil at 1,000 feet, a totally made-up figure. 3 Higgins subsequently hired a mining engineer, Captain Anthony Lucas. After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry. 4 In January 1901, at 1,020 feet-almost precisely the depth predicted by Higgins' wild guess—the well roared and suddenly ejected mud and six tons of drilling pipe out of the ground, terrifying those present. For the next nine days, until the well was capped, the well poured out more oil than all the wells in America combined. 5 In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry; cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy. Higgins' well changed that. The boom made some prospectors millionaires, but the sudden surplus of petroleum was not entirely a blessing for Texas. In the 1930s, prices crashed, to the point that in some parts of the country oil was cheaper than water. That would become a familiar pattern of the boom-or-bust Texas' economy. Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`,label:`Recording 1`,questionNo:16,text:`1 Most people dislike their jobs. It's an astonishing but statistical fact. A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe they have terrible managers. Few describe their managers as malicious or manipulative though. While those types certainly exist, they are a minority. The majority of managers, seemingly, just don't know any better. They are often emulating bad managers they've had in the past. It's likely they've never read a management book or attended a management course. They might not have even reflected on what good management looks like and how it would influence their own management style. 2 The researchers interviewed employees about their managers, beginning with a question about the worst manager they had ever had. From this, the researchers came up with four main causes of why some managers are perceived as being simply awful at their jobs. 3 The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor management practices. It was specifically stressful work environments, minimal training and a lack of accountability that were found to be the most blameworthy. Often a manager's superiors can effectively encourage a manager's distasteful behavior when they fail to discipline the person's wrongdoings. Such workplaces are sometimes described as toxic. 4 The second cause was attributed to the managers' characteristics. Those deemed to be most destructive were odd people—those without drive, those who allow personal problems into the workplace, and those with an unpleasant temperament or personality in general. 5 The third cause of poor management was associated with a deficiency of qualifications not so much the formal variety one obtains from a university, but the informal variety that comes from credible work experience and professional accomplishments. 6 The fourth cause concerned managers who've been promoted for reasons other than potential. One reason in particular why these people had been promoted was that they had been around the longest. It wasn't their skill set or other merits that got them the job; it was their tenure. 7 A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees. The researchers didn't ask senior leaders what they thought of their frontline managers. It's quite possible they're content with how the individuals they promoted are now performing, merrily ignorant of the damage they're actually causing, which might explain why, as the researchers conclude, those same middle managers are usually unaware that they are a bad manager. Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`,label:`Recording 2`,questionNo:19,text:`1 With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead. 2 Iron ore is a key ingredient in steel-making. The mining companies here produce almost 300 million tons of iron ore a year. The 240 giant autonomous trucks in use, in the Western Australian mines, can weigh 400 tons, fully loaded, and travel at speeds of up to 60 kilometers per hour. They are a technological leap, transporting iron ore along routes which run for hundreds of kilometers from mines to their destinations. Here when the truck arrives at its destination, staff in the operation center direct it precisely where to unload. Vast quantities of iron ore are then transported by autonomous trains to ocean ports. 3 Advocates argue these automated vehicles will change mining forever. It may only be five years before the use of automation technology leads to a fully robotic mine. 4 A range of factors has pushed Western Australia's desert region to the lead of this automation revolution. These include the huge size of the mines, the scale of equipment and the repetitive nature of some of the work. Then there's the area's remoteness. At 502,000 square kilometers, it can sometimes make recruiting staff a challenge. Another consideration is the risks when humans interact with large machinery. There are also the financial imperatives. The ongoing push by the mining corporations to be more productive and more efficient is another powerful driver in embracing automation technology. 5 The concept of a fully autonomous mine is a bit of a misleading term, however. This is because the more technology is put into the field, the more people are needed to deploy, maintain and improve it. 6 The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs. These include data scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence. The mining companies claim automation and robotics present opportunities to make mining more sustainable and safer. Employees will be offered a career that is even more fulfilling and more rewarding. 7 Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology. But they still have reservations about the rise of automation technology. Their main concern is the potential impact on remote communities. As automation spreads further, the question is how these remote communities will survive when the old jobs are eliminated. And this may well prove to be the most significant impact of robotic technology in many places around the world. Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`,label:`Recording 3`,questionNo:22,text:`1 According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average. Thai people know that their roads are dangerous, but they don't know this could easily be changed. 2 Globally, road accidents kill more people every year than any infectious disease. Researchers at the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation in America put the death toll in 2017 at 1.24 million. According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the turn of the century. But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries. 3 In many poor countries, road accidents are killing more people than ever before. Those countries have swelling, young populations, a fast-growing fleet of cars and motorbikes, and a limited supply of surgeons. It is impossible to know for sure because official statistics are so inadequate, but deaths are thought to have risen by 40% since 1990 in many low-income countries. In many rich countries, by contrast, roads are becoming even safer. In Estonia and Ireland, for example, the number of deaths has fallen by about two-thirds since the late 1990s. 4 But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle-income countries, which contain most of the world's people and have some of the most dangerous roads. According to researchers, in China and South Africa, traffic deaths have been falling since 2000, and in India since 2012, and the Philippines reached its peak four years ago. The question is whether Thailand can soon follow suit. 5 Rob MeInerney, head of the International Road Assessment Programme, says that all countries tend to go through three phases. They begin with poor, slow roads. In the second phase, as they grow wealthier, they pave the roads, allowing traffic to move faster and pushing up the death rate. Lastly, in the third phase, countries act to make their roads safer. The trick, then, is to reach the third stage sooner, by focusing earlier and more closely on fatal accidents. 6 How to do that? The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives. Safe driving habits are practices which people know they should follow but often don't. Dangerous driving is not a fixed cultural trait, as some imagine. People respond to incentives, such as traffic laws that are actually enforced. Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the man from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He will tell the management how he really feels.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He will meet his new manager in two weeks.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He is going to attend a job interview.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He is going to leave his present job.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P3：“I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time.”。被问者自述 I resigned 的直接陈述。男士开口第一段就说 It's my last day at work tomorrow. I'll start my new job in two weeks.`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman think about the information gained from an exit interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It should be kept private.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It should be carefully analyzed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can be quite useful to senior managers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can improve interviewees' job prospects.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't. In my company, the information gained from these interviews is often not confidential.”。supposed to be 表『本应当』的主张。女士说 An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't，be supposed to 表示『理应、本该』，正是她对这信息该如何的主张：本该保密。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the man want to rethink what he will say in the coming exit interview?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may leave a negative impression on the interviewer.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It may adversely affect his future career prospects.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It may displease his immediate superiors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may do harm to his fellow employees.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P7：“There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry.”。it could affect 表担忧的因果句。男士说 Now you've got me rethinking what I'll disclose，紧接 There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation a…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man think he had better do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Prepare a comprehensive exit report.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Do some practice for the exit interview.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Network with his close friends to find a better employer.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pour out his frustrations on a rate-your-employer website. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P9：“Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer websites.”。Perhaps it would be better 表打算的建议句。对话临近结尾，男士说 Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-empl…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-1`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man want Jane Forster to talk about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Her unsuccessful journey.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Her month-long expedition.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Her latest documentary.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Her career as a botanist.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity since the release of your latest documentary. Can you tell us a little about it?”。主持人的直接请求句。信号是主持人发出的请求 Can you tell us a little about it?，其中 it 回指上一句的 your latest documentary——一问一请求，正是他想让她谈的对象，答案就落在这句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the woman describe her experience as "far from glamorous"?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She had to live like a vegetarian.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She was caught in a hurricane.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She had to endure many hardships.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She suffered from water shortage.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P6：“It was far from glamorous, to be honest. The area we visited was accessible only by canoe and the living conditions in the hut were primitive,”。原话之后的连串列举。信号是她说完 far from glamorous 后一口气列举多重恶劣条件：accessible only by canoe、living conditions in the hut were primitive、no electricity、water s…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`Why did the woman and those who went with her end their trip halfway?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A hurricane was coming.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A flood was approaching.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They had no more food in the canoe.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They could no longer bear the humidity.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P12：“Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching. And we had to evacuate at very short notice.”。received news that + had to 因果链。信号是 we received news that a hurricane was approaching 接着 we had to evacuate——"得知飓风逼近"是因、"被迫撤离"是果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman think of the journey?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It was memorable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was unbearable.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was uneventful.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was fruitful. Section B Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P16：“Absolutely not. We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life.”。强烈否定 + 反转后的正向定调。信号是主持人问 was the journey unsuccessful?（负面设问），她以 Absolutely not. 强烈否定反转。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-2`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the use of jargon terms by experts?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It diminishes laymen's interest in science.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It ensures the accuracy of their arguments.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It makes their expressions more explicit.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It hurts laymen's dignity and self-esteem.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice. In a new study, people exposed to jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous…”。定性词harmful＋对比。答案句的信号是定性词 the harmful effects（有害影响）和紧随其后的对比结果 less interested in science：录音先给出"有害"的定性，再用接触术语者 vs 未接触者的对照坐实是哪种害处，这两处合起来就是答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What do researchers find about people reading scientific articles containing jargon terms?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They can learn to communicate with scientists.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They tend to disbelieve the actual science.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They feel great respect towards scientists.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They will see the complexity of science.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led people to doubt the actual science.”。研究发现动词＋后续转折。信号是 researchers found that（研究者发现）这个直接报告发现的动词，题干 find 与它对应；其后还有 They found the opposite when a text is easier to read. 的对比。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What does Schulman suggest scientists do when communicating with the general public?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Find appropriate topics.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Stimulate their interest.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Explain all the jargon terms.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Do away with jargon terms. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their language. They need to eliminate jargon.”。转折But＋建议need to。双重信号：先是转折 But（前句说术语对专业听众尚可，But 后才是对大众的真正主张），再是建议动词 need to eliminate jargon。转折后＋need to 之处，正是对策题答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-3`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What did Texas businessman Pattillo Higgins believe?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The local gassy hill might start a huge fire.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) There was oil leakage along the Gulf Coast.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The erupting gas might endanger local children.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) There were oil deposits below a local gassy hill.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P2：“Pattillo Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill.”。信号词 became convinced 直接交代所信。录音里 became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill 中的 became convinced（确信）正是表达人物信念的标志词，题干问 believe。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What prevented Higgins' workers from digging a proper hole to get the oil?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The massive gas underground.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their lack of the needed skill.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The sand under the hill.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Their lack of suitable tools.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P2：“The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole.”。信号词 defeated 标出受阻原因。The sand under the hill defeated several attempts 里 defeated（挫败）正对题干的 prevented，主语 The sand under the hill 就是那个让挖洞失败的障碍。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about Captain Lucas' drilling method?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It rendered many oil workers jobless.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was not as effective as he claimed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It gave birth to the oil drilling industry.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was not popularized until years later.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry.”。信号词 created 点出方法的成果。his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry 里 created（开创）直接交代这套方法的历史成果，问 say about method 时。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Texas oil industry boom?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It radically transformed the state's economy.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It resulted in an oil surplus all over the world.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It totally destroyed the state's rural landscape.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It ruined the state's cotton and beef industries. Section C Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet I with a single line through the centre. 播放本篇`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P5：“In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry; cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy.”。转折信号 changed that 是结论落点。录音先铺垫德州 almost entirely rural 的旧貌，紧接一句 Higgins' well changed that，changed that（改变了这一切）是从旧貌转向新局的转折落点，答案就在这一转之后。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-4`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What is a primary cause of employee dissatisfaction according to recent research?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Unsuitable jobs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Bad managers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Insufficient motivation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Tough regulations.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P1：“A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe they have terrible managers.”。题干原话几乎照搬的点题句。录音开篇第二句就用 A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the causes for poor management practices?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ineffective training.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Toxic company culture.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Lack of regular evaluation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Overburdening of managers.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P3：“The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor management practices.”。分点信号 The first cause + 收尾点出 toxic。录音用 The first cause was company culture 起头明确这是分点罗列的第一条成因。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the study on job dissatisfaction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It collected feedback from both employers and employees.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was conducted from frontline managers' point of view.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It provided meaningful clues to solving the problem.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It was based only on the perspective of employees. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P7：“A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees.”。结尾提示语 A point worth making + only。录音末段用 A point worth making is that…（有一点值得说明）这种「特意提醒」的强调语引出关键句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-5`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the mining industry in Western Australia?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is seeing an automation revolution.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is bringing prosperity to the region.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is yielding an unprecedented profit.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is expanding at an accelerating speed.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P1：“With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely tak…”。开头点题的总括句。篇章独白的总判断常落在开头：录音第一句 mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead 直接给出西澳…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What is the impact of the digitization of the mining industry?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It exhausts resources sooner.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It creates a lot of new jobs.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It causes conflicts between employers and employees.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It calls for the retraining of unskilled mining workers.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P6：“The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs.”。因果动词 creating 引出影响。录音第 6 段 The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs 用 creating a need for 直接点出…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What is the attitude of workers' unions towards the introduction of new technology?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They welcome it with open arms.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They will wait to see its effect.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are strongly opposed to it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They accept it with reservations. 播放本篇`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P7：“Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology.”。转折 But 后才是真态度。录音先说工会 accepted（接受），紧接 But they still have reservations 用 But 转出真态度——仍有保留。态度题的落点在转折之后，前半的接受只是铺垫。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-6`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about traffic accidents in Thailand?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Their cost to the nation's economy is incalculable.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They kill more people than any infectious disease.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their annual death rate is about twice that of the global average.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have experienced a gradual decline since the year of 2017.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average.”。地名加数字即落点。第一段开门见山点出 Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the global average，地名 Thailand 加上倍数 double 同时出现，正是预读时盯的两个锚点，这一句就是答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from an American institute's statistics regarding road deaths?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They show a difference between rich and poor nations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They don't reflect the changes in individual countries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They rise and fall from year to year.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are not as reliable as claimed.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the turn of the century.”。转折 But 后才是真意。前半句说总数基本平稳，紧接 But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries，转折词 But 之后才是说话人真正强调的那层意思——这组数据掩盖了个体国家的变化，正是答案落点。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What is said about middle-income countries?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Many of them have increasing numbers of cars on the road.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many of them are following the example set by Thailand.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Many of them have seen a decline in road-death rates.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many of them are investing heavily in infrastructure.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P4：“But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle-income countries… According to researchers, in China and South Africa,”。群体名加并列实例。第四段点出 middle-income countries 后，立即用 China、South Africa、India、Philippines 一连串实例说明 traffic deaths have been falling，群体名加并列例证处即答案句。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What else could be done to reduce fatal road accidents in addition to safer roads?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Foster better driving behavior.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Provide better training for drivers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Abolish all outdated traffic rules.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Impose heavier penalties on speeding.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P6：“The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives.”。提对策句 solution lies in。末段直接给方案 The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`},groupId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-ls-7`,score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section.Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements.For each of them there are four choices marked A),B),C)and D).You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`adversity`,B:`authentic`,C:`convey`,D:`depictions`,E:`diminished`,F:`drowned`,G:`embark`,H:`fragility`,I:`neglected`,J:`obscurity`,K:`outlines`,L:`prevalent`,M:`purchasable`,N:`submit`,O:`suppress`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-1`,text:`At 43, I've reached the stage where women are warned to watch out for the creeping sadness of middle age. We're served up an endless stream of advice on "how to survive your 40s", as if we're in the endurance stage of a slow limp toward [26] . This is the age women start to become "invisible"—our value, attractiveness and power supposedly [27] by the vanishing of youth. But I don't feel like I'm fading into [28] . I feel more seen than I ever have, and for the first time in my life, I have a clear-eyed view of myself that is [29] , compassionate and accepting.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-2`,text:`When I look in the mirror, I'm proud of who I am—even those "broken" parts that for so long seemed impossible to love. So when advertisers try to sell me ways to "turn back the clock", I have to [30] a laugh. I wouldn't go back to the crippling self-consciousness of my youth if you paid me. This hard-won sense of self-acceptance is one of the joys of being an older woman. But it's a narrative often [31] out by the shame that marketers rely on to peddle us their diet pills, miracle face creams and breathable yoga pants—as if self-love is a [32] commodity.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-3`,text:`For some women I know, this sense of trust and self-belief later in life gave them the courage to leave dysfunctional relationships or [33] on new career paths. Others talked about enjoying their own company, of growth through [34] , deepening bonds of friendships, the ability to be more compassionate, less judgmental and to listen more and appreciate the small pleasures. Life past 40 is far from smooth sailing, but it's so much more than the reductive [35] we see in women's magazines and on the Hollywood big screen.`}]},o,{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-1`,text:`You can't see it, smell it, or hear it, and people disagree on how precisely to define it, or where exactly it comes from. It isn't a school subject or an academic discipline, but it can be learned. It is a quality that is required of artists, but it is also present in the lives of scientists and entrepreneurs. All of us benefit from it and we thrive mentally and spiritually when we are able to wield it. It is a delicate thing, easily stamped out; in fact, it flourishes most fully when people are playful and childlike. Meanwhile, it works best in conjunction with deep knowledge and expertise.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-2`,text:`This mysterious—but teachable—quality is creativity, the subject of a recently-published report by Durham Commission on Creativity and Education. The report concludes that creativity should not inhabit the school curriculum only as it relates to drama, music, art and other obviously creative subjects, but that creative thinking ought to run through all of school life, infusing(充满) the way humanities and natural sciences are learned.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-3`,text:`The authors, who focus on education in England, offer a number of sensible recommendations, some of which are an attempt to alleviate the uninspiring and fact-based approach to education that has crept into policy in recent years. When children are regarded as vessels to be filled with facts, creativity does not prosper; nor does it when teachers' sole objective is coaching children towards exams. One suggestion from the commission is a network of teacher-led "creativity collaboratives", along the lines of existing maths hubs(中心), with the aim of supporting teaching for creativity through the school curriculum.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-4`,text:`Nevertheless, it is arts subjects through which creativity can most obviously be fostered. The value placed on them by the independent education sector is clear. One only has to look at the remarkable arts facilities at Britain's top private schools to comprehend this. But in the state sector the excessive focus on English, maths and science threatens to crush arts subjects; meanwhile, reduced school budgets mean diminishing extracurricular activities. There has been a 28.1% decline in students taking creative subjects at high schools since 2014, though happily, art and design have seen a recent increase.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-5`,text:`This discrepancy between state and private education is a matter of social justice. It is simply wrong and unfair that most children have a fraction of the access to choirs, orchestras, art studios and drama that their more privileged peers enjoy. As lives are affected by any number of looming challenges—climate crisis, automation in the workplace—humans are going to need creative thinking more than ever. For all of our sakes, creativity in education, and for all, must become a priority.`}]},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-1`,text:`Emulating your conversation partner's actions is a common human behavior classified as "mirroring" and has been known and studied by psychologists for years. We all tend to subconsciously copy gestures of people we like. But why do we act like this?`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-2`,text:`As a rule, mirroring means that conversationalists enjoy their communication and that there's a certain level of agreement between them. The topic of discussion is equally interesting for both and they know their interests meet.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-3`,text:`Repeating someone's behavior is typical of talented communicators, not always because the person is sympathetic, but because there is a goal to be achieved. This way new idols have been brought to the stage: politicians, celebrities, and other big names. Popular culture makes people want to look popular, and act and speak like popular people.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-4`,text:`Nowadays celebrities steal lyrics from each other and struggle with copyright violation accusations or straightforwardly claim themselves to be the authors, even though all the work was done by other people.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-5`,text:`Among celebrities, it's trendy nowadays to use their own speech writers as politicians do. The so-called "ghostwriting" can take various forms: books, articles, autobiographies, and even social media posts.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-6`,text:`Who is a true copycat(抄袭者) and who gets copycatted? Sometimes, it is a hard nut to crack without an expert's help. But new authorship defending methods based on identifying individual writing patterns are already here. Their aim is to protect intellectual property. Using scientific methods, some of them can define authorship with 85% accuracy.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-7`,text:`Writing is not an easy craft to master. If you want to write like a professional without plagiarism(抄袭), there are a few lessons to learn and the first one is: "Copy from one, it's plagiarism; copy from two, it's research." The correct interpretation of this statement is not about copying, but rather about creating your own style. When you study an author's writing style, don't stop on a single one, but explore numerous styles instead. Examine types of sentences they use, pay attention to their metaphors, and focus on stories you feel you could write a pretty cool sequel(续篇) to.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-8`,text:`Imitation is rather paradoxical. As an integral part of learning, it brings about positive changes, making people develop and grow. However, it may do a lot of harm. Copying someone's thoughts, ideas or inventions is completely unacceptable. It infringes on intellectual property rights of others.`},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-9`,text:`Still, many things we do are about copying others one way or another. So if you want to compliment someone on the work they have done and imitate it, just make sure you do it the right way to avoid committing plagiarism.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`as if we're in the endurance stage of a slow limp toward __26__`,answer:`H`,explanation:`介词 toward 后接名词。"缓慢蹒跚"暗示终点是衰朽。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-1`,quote:`We're served up an endless stream of advice on "how to survive your 40s", as if we're in the endurance stage of a slow limp toward [26] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`our value, attractiveness and power supposedly __27__ by the vanishing of youth`,answer:`E`,explanation:`过去分词作后置定语/状语。"价值因青春逝去而被削弱"。作者陈述刻板印象：女性的"价值、吸引力和权力"据说会因"青春的消逝"而削减；diminished by"被…削减"语义贴切、与全文反驳的"中年=贬值"刻板印象呼应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-1`,quote:`This is the age women start to become "invisible"—our value, attractiveness and power supposedly [27] by the vanishing of youth.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`But I don't feel like I'm fading into __28__`,answer:`J`,explanation:`fade into 后接名词。fade into obscurity="湮没无名"是铁搭。This is the age women start to become "invisible"提示"中年女性=被看不见的"，作者随即反驳"我不觉得自己 fading into __"，对应 obscurity"默默无闻"——与"invisible"互文，形成完整反讽。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-1`,quote:`But I don't feel like I'm fading into [28] .`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`I have a clear-eyed view of myself that is __29__, compassionate and accepting`,answer:`B`,explanation:`并列形容词中的一员。本真、富有同情、接纳——三重自我品质。a clear-eyed view of myself that is authentic, compassionate and accepting——authentic（本真的）与 compassionate（富有同情心的）和 accepting（接纳的）共同刻画"对自己清醒、真实、慈悲、接纳"的…`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-1`,quote:`I feel more seen than I ever have, and for the first time in my life, I have a clear-eyed view of myself that is [29] , compassionate and accepting.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`I have to __30__ a laugh`,answer:`O`,explanation:`情态动词 have to + 动词原形。suppress a laugh="忍住笑"惯用搭配。作者面对"回到青春"的广告觉得可笑，但出于礼节"忍住笑"。suppress a laugh 是英语固定搭配，意为"克制笑出来"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-2`,quote:`So when advertisers try to sell me ways to "turn back the clock", I have to [30] a laugh.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`But it's a narrative often __31__ out by the shame that marketers rely on`,answer:`F`,explanation:`过去分词 + out by 固定搭配。drown out=淹没/盖过（铁搭）。a narrative often drowned out by the shame that marketers rely on to peddle us their diet pills——"自我接纳的叙事常被商家所利用的羞耻感所盖过"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-2`,quote:`But it's a narrative often [31] out by the shame that marketers rely on to peddle us their diet pills, miracle face creams and breathable yoga pants—as if self-love is a [32] commodity.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`as if self-love is a __32__ commodity`,answer:`M`,explanation:`不定冠词 a + ___ + 名词 commodity。讽刺商家把自爱当"可购买商品"。前文说商家兜售各种产品，as if 引出讽刺：仿佛"自爱"是可以买来的商品，purchasable commodity"可购买的商品"对接最严丝合缝。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-2`,quote:`But it's a narrative often [31] out by the shame that marketers rely on to peddle us their diet pills, miracle face creams and breathable yoga pants—as if self-love is a [32] commodity.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`gave them the courage to leave dysfunctional relationships or __33__ on new career paths`,answer:`G`,explanation:`or 并列"to leave / __ on"，需动词原形。固定搭配 embark on=踏上（新征程）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-3`,quote:`For some women I know, this sense of trust and self-belief later in life gave them the courage to leave dysfunctional relationships or [33] on new career paths.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`of growth through __34__`,answer:`A`,explanation:`介词 through 后接名词。经由逆境而成长=英文励志惯用法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-3`,quote:`Others talked about enjoying their own company, of growth through [34] , deepening bonds of friendships, the ability to be more compassionate, less judgmental and to listen more and appreciate the small pleasures.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`so much more than the reductive __35__ we see in women's magazines`,answer:`D`,explanation:`the + 形容词 + ___。杂志和银幕里"狭隘的刻画"。作者反驳的正是媒体对中年女性"reductive depictions"（简化的、缩水的刻画），depictions 与"在杂志和银幕上看到"完美对回，与开篇"women are warned…"形成首尾呼应。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-bp-3`,quote:`Life past 40 is far from smooth sailing, but it's so much more than the reductive [35] we see in women's magazines and on the Hollywood big screen.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`There is an ongoing debate among the public as to whether the images of deceased celebrities should be recreated.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`匹配 G 段：公众辩论·是否该复活已故名人形象。定位句“We've got a societal debate going on about access to our public commons, as it were, about famous faces,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-G`,quote:`Legally, a person's rights to control the commercial use of their name and image beyond their death differ between and even within countries.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`The CGI technology allows the image of the deceased James Dean to be presented to young people in new settings.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`匹配 C 段：迪恩 + 年轻人 + 新场景。定位句“Moving into the future, we want James Dean to be brought into different gaming environments,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-C`,quote:`The James Dean film is a way to keep the actor's image relevant for younger generations, says Mark Roesler of CMG Worldwide,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`It is very likely that the CGI-recreated image of a deceased celebrity will fail to match the real actor`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`匹配 J 段：CGI 重现可能配不上真演员。定位句“There're a thousand things that could go wrong with a computer-generated facial performance,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-J`,quote:`This refers to the idea that when objects trying to resemble humans aren't quite perfect,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`The use of digital technology can bring images of deceased celebrities back to the screen.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`匹配 A 段：数字技术把已故明星带回屏幕（总起）。定位句“Digital humans are coming to a screen near you. As computer-generated imagery (CGI) has become cheaper and more sophisticated,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-A`,quote:`Digital humans are coming to a screen near you.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Recreating a deceased famous actor or actress may violate their legitimate rights.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`匹配 F 段：复活已故演员可能侵犯合法权利。定位句“A hidden hazard of digitally recreating a deceased celebrity is the risk of damaging their legacy.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-F`,quote:`A hidden hazard of digitally recreating a deceased(已故的) celebrity is the risk of damaging their legacy.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`More CGI-recreated images of deceased celebrities are expected to appear on screen.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`匹配 L 段：未来屏幕上会出现更多 CGI 已故名人。定位句“Webber expects that we will see more digital humans on screen. It's happening because it can happen, he says.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-L`,quote:`Webber expects that we will see more digital humans on screen.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`The image of James Dean will be recreated on screen with his voice dubbed by someone else.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`匹配 B 段：迪恩 + 他人配音。定位句“In the film, which will be called Finding Jack, Dean will be recreated on screen with CGI based on old footage(影片镜头) and photograp…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-B`,quote:`Late in 2019, it was announced that US actor James Dean, who died in 1955, will star in a Vietnam War film scheduled for release later this year.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`However advanced the CGI technology is, the recreated image will differ in a way from the real actor.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`匹配 H 段：再先进的 CGI 也会与真演员有差别。定位句“A recreation, however lifelike, will never be indistinguishable from a real actor, says Webber.”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-H`,quote:`A recreation, however lifelike, will never be indistinguishable from a real actor, says Webber.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`A lot of actors today are likely to make use of the CGI technology to have their images stored for the benefit of their families.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`匹配 E 段：在世演员用 CGI 给家人存形象。定位句“I also see a lot of actors today who will have the desire to take advantage of this technology: to have their likeness captured an…”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-E`,quote:`Now, a person can be animated from scratch.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Some actors are concerned that they may lose jobs because of the CGI technology.`,options:e(o.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`匹配 I 段：演员担心因 CGI 失业。定位句“As it becomes easier to digitally recreate celebrities and to entirely manufacture on-screen identities,”。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-mp-I`,quote:`"I think actors are worried about this," says Edwards.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about creativity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It develops best when people are spiritually prepared.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is most often wielded by scientists and entrepreneurs.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is founded on scientific knowledge and analytical skills.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It contributes to intellectual growth but can easily be killed.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P1：“All of us benefit from it and we thrive mentally and spiritually when we are able to wield it. It is a delicate thing, easily stamped out;”。It contributes to intellectual growth but can easily be killed. 用 but 同时容纳“促进智力成长”和“易被扼杀”两个属性，与预判完全吻合。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-1`,quote:`All of us benefit from it and we thrive mentally and spiritually when we are able to wield it. It is a delicate thing, easily stamped out;`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What is the conclusion of a recently-published report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Natural sciences should be learned the way humanities courses are.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Cultivation of creativity should permeate the entire school curriculum.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Art courses should be made compulsory for all students.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Students should learn more obviously creative subjects.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“The report concludes that creativity should not inhabit the school curriculum only as it relates to drama, music,”。B 项把 run through all of school life 改写为 permeate the entire school curriculum，是 but 后内容的精准同义复述。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-2`,quote:`The report concludes that creativity should not inhabit the school curriculum only as it relates to drama, music, art and other obviously creative subjects,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does the report say is detrimental to the fostering of creativity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Alleviation of pressure.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Teacher-led school activities.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Test-oriented teaching.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Independent learning.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P3：“When children are regarded as vessels to be filled with facts, creativity does not prosper; nor does it when teachers' sole objective is coaching chil…”。C 项把段中两处对应内容压缩为 Test-oriented teaching，是报告明确点名的负面教学方式。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-3`,quote:`When children are regarded as vessels to be filled with facts, creativity does not prosper; nor does it when teachers' sole objective is coaching children towar…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the private schools in the UK?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They encourage extracurricular activities.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They attach great importance to arts education.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They prioritize arts subjects over maths and sciences.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They cater to students from different family backgrounds.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P4：“Nevertheless, it is arts subjects through which creativity can most obviously be fostered.”。B 项把“独立部门给艺术学科赋予的价值”主动化表达为“私立学校非常重视艺术教育”，与定位句完全等值。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-4`,quote:`Nevertheless, it is arts subjects through which creativity can most obviously be fostered.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What should be done to meet the future challenges?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Increasing government investment in school education.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Narrowing the existing gap between the rich and the poor.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Providing all children with equal access to arts education.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Focusing on meeting the needs of under-privileged students.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P5：“As lives are affected by any number of looming challenges—climate crisis, automation in the workplace—humans are going to need creative thinking more…”。C 项 all children with equal access 精准对应 for all 与 fraction of the access 的反向均等化，是对末段呼吁最严谨的同义复述。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p1-5`,quote:`As lives are affected by any number of looming challenges—climate crisis, automation in the workplace—humans are going to need creative thinking more than ever.…`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do people tend to do while engaging in a conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Repeat what their partners say one way or another.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Focus as much as possible on topics of mutual interest.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Imitate their partners' gestures without their knowing it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Observe carefully how their partners make use of gestures.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`定位 P1：“We all tend to subconsciously copy gestures of people we like.”。C 项同时复现"模仿手势"与"无意识"两个预判要素，且无额外添加成分，与定位句严格等值。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-1`,quote:`We all tend to subconsciously copy gestures of people we like.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`When does mirroring usually take place in a conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) When both sides are sympathetic with each other.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) When both sides have a lot of things in common.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) When both sides make interesting contributions.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) When both sides try to seek common ground.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`定位 P2：“As a rule, mirroring means that conversationalists enjoy their communication and that there's a certain level of agreement between them.”。predict 的"双方兴趣/共识相合"正是"have a lot of things in common"的内核，B 同时覆盖"agreement"与"interests meet"两点，匹配度最高。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-2`,quote:`As a rule, mirroring means that conversationalists enjoy their communication and that there's a certain level of agreement between them.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about popular culture?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It encourages people to imitate.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It appeals mostly to big names.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It acquaints young people with their idols.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can change people's mode of cognition.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P3：“Popular culture makes people want to look popular, and act and speak like popular people.”。predict 的"促使/鼓励模仿"正是 A 的字面表达，且 imitate 涵盖 act and speak like popular people 全部具体行为，匹配最严格。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-3`,quote:`Popular culture makes people want to look popular, and act and speak like popular people.`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`Why is the saying "copy from two, it's research" a lesson to learn?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It facilitates the creation of one's own writing style.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It helps to protect one's intellectual property rights.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It fosters correct interpretation of professional writing.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It enables one to write intriguing sequels to famous stories.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`定位 P7：“"Copy from one, it's plagiarism; copy from two, it's research." The correct interpretation of this statement is not about copying,”。选项 A 抓住 not...but 结构中"but rather"那一层的正面信息，并把动名词转为名词化短语——这是格言题的标准改写。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-7`,quote:`"Copy from one, it's plagiarism; copy from two, it's research." The correct interpretation of this statement is not about copying,`},score:1},{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the author say imitation is rather paradoxical?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is liable to different interpretations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is by and large a necessary evil.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can give rise to endless disputes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may do harm as well as good.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`定位 P8：“Imitation is rather paradoxical. As an integral part of learning, it brings about positive changes, making people develop and grow.”。predict 要求"既有利又有害"两面同时落地，D 用 harm as well as good 一次性涵盖，完美贴合 paradoxical 的语义内核。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet6-2021-06-s3-p2-8`,quote:`Imitation is rather paradoxical. As an integral part of learning, it brings about positive changes, making people develop and grow.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Yunnan is a province in southwest China, with an average altitude of 1,500 meters. It has a long history, beautiful scenery and a pleasant climate. With its superior ecological environment and diverse species, it is known as a paradise for wild animals and plants. It also has various mineral resources and abundant water resources, providing favorable conditions for the sustainable development of the province's economy. It is home to 25 ethnic minorities, most of whom have their own languages, customs and religions. Its unique natural scenery and rich ethnic culture make it one of the most popular tourist destinations in China, attracting large numbers of tourists from home and abroad every year.`},questions:[{id:`cet6-2021-06-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`云南是位于中国西南的一个省份，平均海拔 1500米。云南历史悠久，风景秀丽，气候宜人。云南生态环境优越，生物多种多样，被誉为野生动植物的天堂。云南还有多种矿藏和充足的水资源,为全省经济的可持续发展提供了有利条件。 云南居住着25个少数民族,他们大多有自己的语言、习俗和宗教。云南独特的自然景色和丰富的民族文化使其成为中国最受欢迎的旅游目的地之一，每年都吸引着大批国内外游客前往观光旅游。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①云南拟定统一译法 Yunnan（专有地名，约定俗成音译，全篇都用它）。原文 云南是位于中国西南的一个省份 中"云南"反复出现五六次，必须前后只用 Yunnan 一种拼写，；②少数民族拟定统一译法 ethnic minorities（约定俗成意译；单个为 ethnic minority）。原文 云南居住着25个少数民族 是文化负载词，六级常考，。`,score:15}]}]}};export{s as default};